\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  Widens Non- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Zone - Raiffeisen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,106 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 12:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1154 GMT [Dow Jones] By widening the ruble's trading corridor to 32.45-37.35, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has widened the zone in which it does not carry out \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Raiffeissen Bank says. Although the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does not reveal its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  scheme, Raiffeisen estimates the regulator buys $150 million per day in the 33.95-33.45 range versus the dollar-euro basket, increasing its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to $350 million in the 33.45-32.95 range. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1152 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spreads are steady at 872 bps in mostly directionless trade Wednesday, with investors taking a wait-and-see stance ahead of a key EU summit later this month. Trading is modest with a total of EUR42M in turnover. "We have seen spreads widening over the past two weeks amid worries that the EU summit won't reach a market friendly solution, but now the market is in wait-and-see mode," says a bond trader at a local bank. (alkman.granitsas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1151 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro Overnight Index Average fixed lower Tuesday at 0.602% versus Monday's 0.689%. Wednesday. The interbank overnight rate is trading lower at 0.35%-0.45% versus 0.52%-0.58% Tuesday after the ECB's generous weekly tender allotment started bringing rates down, a trader says. The trader expects the ECB's Jean-Claude Trichet to announce an end to full allotment of 3-month tenders at a policy briefing Thursday. Earlier Wednesday, 3-month Euribor fixed at 1.095% vs 1.096% Tuesday. (herbert.rude@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1145 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is certainly back in favor Wednesday despite the risk-aversion drivers that weighed on it overnight still being present. The Saudi stock market has shed another 5% this session and European equity markets are trading 1% lower, gold is close to record highs and oil close to 2 1/2 year highs. However, M&A related buying seems to have come to the euro's rescue with the market chat being of Middle East buying to the tune of around EUR2billion. EUR/USD hit 1.3843, EUR/JPY 113.40, EUR/GBP 0.8488 and EUR/CHF 1.2811 in Europe, all highs for the day. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1129 GMT [Dow Jones] Norway retail sales fall 0.1% on the month in January, hit by high power prices, says SEB. "The weak outcome shouldn't be too surprising coming at the heels of surging electricity prices in late 2010 due to unusually cold weather," SEB says. The heating bills received by households in January were much higher than the previous one, and "most likely put a lid on spending," the bank says. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1124 GMT [Dow Jones] The inverted correlation between gold and USD/CHF is picking up, notes \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Gold is pushing record highs, and this together with a negative technical outlook for USD/CHF while below 0.9350 has BarCap looking for risk toward the 0.9180-0.9150 area. USD/CHF is now at 0.9253, gold trades at $1433.30/oz from its overnight record high of $1435/oz. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1107 GMT [Dow Jones] While risks appear skewed for a stronger ruble in the short term, Citi expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken in the second half of 2011 on current account weakness, uncertainty related to parliamentary elections in December, higher budget spending and seasonal outflows in the third quarter. Adds that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely act to impede any significant weakening, especially since presidential elections are around the corner in 2012. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1107 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece's 5-year sovereign CDS are 20 bps wider at 965 bps, but the remainder of the periphery and soft core is reasonably resilient. Portugal 5-year protection is 10 bps wider at 472, and Ireland, Spain, Italy, and Belgium are 3-6 bps wider, according to Markit. "Cash looks wider because bunds are up, but there is not a huge amount of negative news, and the market is pretty quiet," says a trader. Geo-political risk is the big driver, and our market is looking at where that takes equities and is reacting accordingly." (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1103 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro moves higher in European trading hours Wednesday, largely shrugging off ongoing unrest in the Middle East but instead focusing on Thursday's ECB meeting. The pound is also higher against the dollar, boosted by a strong construction PMI reading. Data-wise monthly ADP employment data for February in the U.S., due at 1315 GMT, will be closely watched. EUR/USD is up at 1.3831, USD/JPY is down at 81.92, while GBP/USD is up at 1.6311. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1044 GMT [Dow Jones] The Norwegian krone is far stronger than \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  forecast in its last monetary policy report, published October last year, which may push the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower its interest rate path on March 16, says Nordea. Unless the \uc2 NOK\uc2  weakens before then, "a dovish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should disappoint the market enough to weaken the krone," Nordea says. "Either way the outlook for the krone is bearish." EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  trades at 7.7176. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1029 GMT [Dow Jones] Norway retail sales fall 0.1% on the month but will grow overall in 1Q stimulated by low interest rates, a relatively low unemployment rate and continued strong house price growth, says Handelsbanken. The retail sales series is "quite volatile and therefore one shouldn't put too much weight on one single month's development," says the bank, adding "these figures don't alter our interest rate view." EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  trades at 7.7148. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1025 GMT [Dow Jones] The tail of the German 2% February 2016 bobl auction, which is the difference between the average price of 98.17 and the minimum price of 98.16, is very small, "further underpinning the good demand at today's auction," says Newedge economist Annalisa Piazza. The relative cheapness of the line versus previous rolls might have attracted dealers at the auction, she adds. Germany sold EUR4.138 billion of the bobl at an average yield of 2.39% and a bid-to-cover ratio of 1.9. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com) [ 03-02-11 0654ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110302e73200079\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
