\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE:Russia Widens Ruble Trading Range Amid High Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 19:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, context)\par \par \uc2 -- Bank of Russia widens ruble's trading corridor to five rubles from four\par \par \uc2 -- Move seen as measure to fight inflation, move towards ruble free float\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects ruble to strengthen in near term\par \par \uc2 By Ira Iosebashvili\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday it would let the ruble trade in a wider range, a move market players said will allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen and moderate a recent bout of steep inflation.\par \par \uc2 Starting Tuesday, the Bank of Russia will manage the ruble in a wider corridor of between RUB32.45 and RUB37.45 to its dollar-euro basket, First Deputy Chairman Alexei Ulyukayev told reporters in Moscow. Previously the ruble was managed at levels of around RUB33 and RUB37 to the basket. It traded at RUB33.64 to the basket at the close of Micex exchange trade in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 "This measure will ensure more effective \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of our interest rate policy, with the aim of providing price stability and controlling inflation," Ulyukayev said.\par \par \uc2 Inflation -- a recurring economic headache in Russia -- has in recent months become a major concern for the government, which is slated to hold presidential elections in 2012. Russians see inflation as the biggest problem facing the country, according to a poll published by the state-run VTsIOM research center last week.\par \par \uc2 In an effort to combat rising prices, the Bank of Russia raised interest rates across the board last week for the first time since the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also reduce the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  it performs at the edge of the ruble's trading corridor to $600 million from $650 million, Ulyukayev said.\par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev also said the ruble has a greater chance of strengthening in the short term, rather than weakening.\par \par \uc2 "Considering today's prices for oil and the state of Russia's current account, the trend will probably be towards strengthening," he added.\par \par \uc2 While higher interest rates typically draw speculative inflows, a wider trading band would "increase the risk for investors in the market," making the ruble less predictable and therefore less attractive to speculators, Ulyukayev said.\par \par \uc2 The ruble on Tuesday hit a new 15-month high against the dollar of RUB28.69, because of higher oil prices and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate hikes. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has strengthened by nearly 10% since late November, when \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Sergei Ignatyev warned that those betting on the weakness of the ruble would be "disappointed."\par \par \uc2 Analysts agreed the move would likely lead to more ruble appreciation in the near term.\par \par \uc2 "Widening of the ruble corridor while the ruble is strengthening big time is clearly a sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not stand on the way of further ruble strengthening," said Aurelija Augulyte, analyst at Nordea Denmark, in a note to investors.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sticking to its annual inflation target of 7% for 2011, Ulyukayev said. Prices have already risen by 3.1% this year, while on-year inflation hit 9.7% as of Feb. 21, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 "We expect that inflation will begin to slacken in the second quarter, and we won't see double digit on-year inflation in 2011 after all," Ulyukayev said.\par \par \uc2 He added that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $4.5 billion in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  during February.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to see capital outflows in January, despite a stronger ruble and higher oil prices, Ulyukayev said, without giving the volume of outflows.\par \par \uc2 Capital outflows may continue in February as well, although final data wasn't yet available, he said. Russia saw some $30 billion in capital outflow in 2010, most of it coming in the second half of the year as domestic companies rushed to repay foreign debt.\par \par \uc2 -By Ira Iosebashvili, Dow Jones Newswires; +7 495 232-9192, ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (William Mauldin in Moscow and Clare Connaghan in London contributed to this article.) [ 03-01-11 1354ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110301e731000lr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
