\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  Buying Up $500M In Daily  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,260 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2011\par 13:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London: +44-20-7842-9464; markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1242 GMT (Dow Jones) Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be buying as much as $500 million per day to slow the ruble's strengthening, says Alexei Borichev, a trader at ING. "They are in a very tough situation right now. There are a ton of inflows coming in because of high oil prices, and they are doing what they can to keep the ruble from appreciating too fast and hurting exporters," he says. But adds that RUB33.75 to RUB33.50 will be "very significant" support that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will not easily penetrate. Ruble trading at RUB33.85 versus the basket, stronger by 0.4%. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1241 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spreads are rising 10 bps on Monday to 820 bps as GGBs follow the widening trend across the periphery on low turnover of EUR12 million. "Investors that were long for the last two or three weeks are taking some money off the table, and not just in the Greek market," says a senior local bond analyst. "There is a long, uncomfortable wait until the EU makes its hopefully comprehensive announcements to resolve the debt crisis, and there had better be details given or we will see a sharp sell-off." (nick.skrekas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] So, now we now why Axel Weber didn't want the ECB presidency, writes David Cottle in today's Taking Stock column. He was worried he'd be in a minority as often as not when it came to policy. Investors should be worried rather that Europe's most credible central banker didn't want the top job. (david.cottle@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1226 GMT (Dow Jones) Poland's unemployment rate looks set to peak at 13.6% at the end of the first quarter, then fall to 12.4% by the end of June and to 11.2% by the end of the year, says Invest-Bank in Warsaw. The spike will be caused by lower government spending on actively fighting unemployment, such as training programs and subsidizing of internships, Invest-Bank says. However, the jobless rate will then ease as seasonal hiring picks up and as Germany is set to open its labor market to Polish workers from May 1. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1219 GMT (Dow Jones) Deteriorating liquidity and rising volatility are the main risks the Hungarian government bond market is facing after the government redirected private pension fund assets to state coffers, says ING. Should there be a positive or a negative shock, "foreign investors may want to enter or exit the same small gate while there will be fewer domestic investors to counterbalance these movements and stabilize the market," economist David Nemeth says. Sees a bond market correction after January's rally. Expects EUR/HUF to trade in 270-275 range in the following weeks. EUR/HUF is 271.85. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1206 GMT (Dow Jones) AUD/USD weakness has extended more than \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  expected and the bank says the rate now appears to be carving out a pennant-type pattern between 0.9915 and 1.0180. This prompts the bank shift to the sidelines as it expects further ranging between these parameters, although a break below 0.9910 would suggest an unfolding bear-flag rather than a pennant and this would open downside risk toward 0.98 and 0.9725 before a base were made. AUD/USD now at 1.0017. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1150 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro Overnight Index Average fixed lower Friday at 0.924% versus Thursday's 1.023%. Monday, the interbank overnight rate is trading at 0.75%-0.85%, below Friday's range of 0.80%-0.90%. Early in the reserve period, excess liquidity is pressuring rates slightly lower, a trader says. Earlier Monday, 3-month Euribor fixed at 1.091% versus 1.093% Friday. (joerg.jaeger@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1132 GMT (Dow Jones) USD/CHF is bottoming out as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets start to price in the end of the Fed's quantative easing, and growth in Germany - Switzerland's main export market - peaks, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2 . In addition, Swiss January inflation was weaker than expected, pushing three-month Swiss Libor rates to 0.17%, well below the SNB's target of 0.25%, says global \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  head Mansoor Mohi-Uddin. "USD/CHF will trend higher back through parity, suggesting it may already have formed a long-term bottom this year, after falling from 1.25 at the onset of the credit crunch in 2007," he points out. USD/CHF at 0.9727 by 1130 GMT.(neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1131 GMT (Dow Jones) Middle East credit defaults swaps spreads are stable after widening Monday morning, as political uncertainty continues to weigh on the region, says index owner Markit. Says Egypt CDS spread is 14bps wider at 328bps, Tunisia 3bps wider at 173bp, Morocco 3bps wider at 170bps, Bahrain 10bps wider at 245bps, Lebanon 1bp tighter at 350bps, Israel 1bp wider at 144bps. After the fall of the Tunisian and Egyptian regimes due to popular revolt, investors are cautious on the risk of turmoil in neighboring countries. (serena.ruffoni@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1126 GMT (Dow Jones) The price of insuring euro-zone government debt is continuing to rise as the secondary markets absorb reports indicating strains in aid packages for the bloc's fragile economies. Greece CDS is now indicated 31 bps wider at 880 bps, while Ireland CDS has gone 15 bps wider to 585 bps, Markit says. Portugal CDS is 20 bps wider at 460 bps, while Spain is 10 bps wider at 254 bps. Belgium CDS is 6 bps wider at 173 bps and Italy CDS is 3 bps wider at 181 bps. (irene.chapple@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1101 GMT (Dow Jones) Capital inflows from VTB's (VTBR.RS) recent secondary public offering are unlikely to support the ruble, Renaissance Capital says. "First, the SPO is being conducted abroad, so local players who may have participated boosted demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than the ruble. Second, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only beneficiary and will be able to sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues directly to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or...save those funds in the Reserve Fund." Short term, the ruble will receive support from tax payments, with 200 billion rubles ($6.8 billion) in pension tax due Tuesday, RenCap says. The ruble trades at RUB33.84 versus the basket, stronger by 0.4%. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1056 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are sharply higher as the market expects no new government paper supplies immediately, says a dealer with a state-run bank. The most-traded 8.13% 2022 bond is at 99.70, recovering from a day's low of 99.38 and from 99.43 last close. Bonds recovered from initial drops on inflation data "on the view that no more auctions are expected this week," the dealer says. India had cut INR100 billion worth of papers from its scheduled auction which ended Friday. India's January WPI rose 8.23% on-year, slightly slower than the 8.43% increase in December, but above the expected 8.01% from a Dow Jones poll of 13 economists. The government also revised November's inflation upward to 8.08% from 7.48%. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com) [ 02-14-11 0742ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110214e72e0008b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgo