\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Russia cbank head eyes move toward rouble free float\b0\par\par\uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2010\par 14:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Says could stop regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  within 2 years\par \par \uc2 * Oil prices, c/a surplus to support rouble\par \par \uc2 * Worried about inflation, but 2011 target "achievable"\par \par \uc2 * Does not rule out rate hikes in Q1\par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds quotes, details, background)\par \par \uc2 By Toni Vorobyova and Yelena Fabrichnaya\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Dec 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be ready to stop regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the rouble in the next two years as it moves ever closer to allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sergei Ignatyev said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In a rare press briefing, Ignatyev also forecast the time of chronic rouble weakness could be over, said he was concerned about inflation and did not rule out near-term rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 His comments boosted the rouble in late trade, pushing it towards Tuesday's one-week high versus the euro-dollar basket used by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to track the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "Anything is possible," he said, when asked about the possibility of interest rate hikes starting late this year or in the first quarter of 2011.\par \par \uc2 Analysts see the refi rate hiked from 7.75 percent in the first quarter of 2011 -- expectations which were fuelled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropping its wording in November on policy likely staying unchanged in comings months.\par \par \uc2 Rates at record lows, rising inflation, foreign debt repayments by corporates, acquisitions abroad, rising imports and global risk aversion all combined this autumn and prompted the rouble to shed all its 2010 gains in just a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Some people expect a further weakness of the rouble -- I think they may be disappointed," Ignatyev said.\par \par \uc2 "At the moment oil prices are very high... and against the backdrop of current account surplus it is very difficult to expect a further rouble weakness."\par \par \uc2 Russia remains committed to increasing exchange rate flexibility, further widening the rouble's floating corridor.\par \par \uc2 "My opinion is that in the next 2-3 years, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot take on this obligation that it will not carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Ignatyev said.\par \par \uc2 "But what I call an "almost free-float" -- we could arrive at that regime in the next two years -- where we go along the path of widening the corridor, and the rouble wobbles in its central part without any \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 UPBEAT ON GROWTH, WORRIED ON INFLATION\par \par \uc2 Ignatyev estimated November's capital outflows at $9 billion -- meaning some $29 billion has now left Russia since the start of the year -- but said an inflow was possible in December.\par \par \uc2 Russia's $32 billion privatisation plan could lead to more inflows in 2011-3, he said.\par \par \uc2 Echoing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent tone, Ignatyev sounded upbeat on the economy, saying he sees no serious macroeconomic risks for Russia in 2011 and hopes for gross domestic product (GDP) growth of 5 percent -- higher than the official forecast.\par \par \uc2 Next year's target to cut inflation to 6-7 percent "is quite achievable", he said but sounded less sanguine on the near-term.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation is starting to worry us," he said, forecasting it could reach 8.4 percent this year -- the lowest in post Soviet history but far higher than had been expected before a severe summer drought wiped out a third of the harvest, pushing up food prices.\par \par \uc2 The rouble's weakness this autumn may also have played a part in fuelling price pressures, Ignatyev conceded.\par \par \uc2 RESERVES\par \par \uc2 Russia has recently started buying the Canadian dollar for its near-$500 billion gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and Ignatyev said that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's share of the portfolio could rise to 1-2 percent next year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also preparing to invest in the Australian dollar and, not least because of the addition of the new \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ignatyev said the structure of reserves could change a little next year.\par \par \uc2 "I do not rule it out. But it is unlikely that something will change significantly," he said.\par \par \uc2 Prior to the addition of the loonie, the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  part of the reserves was held in 47 percent dollars, 41 percent euros, 10 percent sterling and 2 percent yen.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-CBANK/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101208e6c8000uz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
