\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  Would  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In Ruble On Volatile Oil\b0\par\par\uc2 1,352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 10:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0826 GMT [Dow Jones] The Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was able to widen its parameters for allowable ruble fluctuations starting Wednesday because the main drivers for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been relatively subdued, but if volatile oil prices were to return, it might have to meddle in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more than its current plan suggests, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alfa Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. "In the event of higher oil price volatility, it will be hard for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to refrain from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market," Alfa economist Natalia Orlova says. Ruble strengthens to 35.4180 to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dollar-euro basket, from 35.4798 late yesterday. Ruble strengthens to 29.9236 vs the dollar. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR rock steady at 8,920 on suspected Bank Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent local unit from appreciating further, dealers say. Four dealers say they suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought between $300 million-$400 million in total at 8,920 as foreign fund inflows continue. Pair likely to continue trading between 8,920-8,930 Friday with traders expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue defend greenback. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0821 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD has breached important and psychological resistance near 1.60 and a close for the day above that would signal that the uptrend has resumed from its 2010 low with a target of the January highs near 1.6440, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . The bank says given broader weakness, it stays bullish on GBP/USD while it trades above 1.5755 and especially so while above 1.5880. GBP/USD now at 1.6048 down slightly from the day's fresh 8--month high of 1.6066. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0820 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds have drifted lower as stocks extend gains as the EUR rises to its highest vs USD since January. The economic calendar is empty so bonds may continue to take cues from stocks and the EUR until weekly jobless claims in the US at 1230 GMT. Yield spreads on peripheral euro-zone countries are also generally tighter, with Ireland the best performer. December bunds are down 0.13 at 131.44 and the 10-year bund is slightly lower at 99.66 to yield 2.286%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0820 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex flat, last +0.1% at 20,712.33, off 20,854.55 early high, 20,636.70 session low as investors turn cautious, reluctant to buy at these levels after Sensex jumped 2.4% last session. 16 index issues up, 14 down; with most software shares higher for second successive session before \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 2Q earnings, FY11 view due tomorrow. "Strong regional gains had helped our markets open firm. Some caution is setting now as the markets have gained too much in a short span of time. The Sensex up over 15% since Sept. 1," says local dealer. Adds, some local institutions booking profits to participate in $3.5 billion \uc2 Coal India\uc2  IPO, opening for subscriptions Monday. Expects 16%-18% growth for Sensex companies - excluding oil & gas plays. Tips Sensex in 20,600-20,800 range. \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +3.0% at INR656.90, \uc2 Infosys\uc2  +1.5% at INR3,198. \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +1.2% at INR1,190, RIL (500325.BY) +0.4% at INR1,076.60. Larsen (500510.BY) down 2.5% at INR2,027, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) down 0.9% at INR1,149.60. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0818 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungarian forint firms as the USD weakens sharply and also as the market now regards meeting this year's budget deficit target as a fact in the wake of the extra taxes announced by the Prime Minister Wednesday, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. EUR/HUF is at 271.30 versus 273.20 Wednesday afternoon. If EUR/USD can massively break 1.41, the EUR/HUF could approach 270 or sink to levels even lower than that, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  adds. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0815 GMT [Dow Jones] USD tumbles after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore surprises financial markets with an effective monetary tightening by widening and raising its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading band. Its seen as a vote of confidence in the Asian economic recovery which, along with recent good earnings data, helps lift interest in riskier assets. Nikkei gained 1.9%, AUD rose to a new 28-year high near parity, dollar fell to a new record low at CHF0.9480 and a new 15-month low close to Y81.00 and EUR soared over $1.4100 for the first time since January. USD at Y81.12, EUR at $1.4114. Looming US data later this week and speech by Bernanke intensifies QE speculation. An expected widening in US trade gap in data due Thursday could increase pressure on the US to cut a deal with China over CNY. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)0-\par \par \uc2 0813 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower late at 3.0820 vs 3.0915 yesterday, tracking broad USD weakness vs regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on prospect of Fed resuming quantitative easing, while surprise monetary tightening by Singapore's MAS also weigh on greenback. 2 dealers say Bank Negara was suspected of buying at MYR3.0820 to slow MYR's rise. Tips next support at 3.0810 (Sept 29 low), stronger support at 3.0800; dealers think \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to let pair breach that level (3.0800) in near term.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0806 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary imposing extra taxes on telecommunication and energy companies and large supermarket chains "introduces further volatility to the business environment without significantly reducing the tax burden and as such is likely to further discourage investment," says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . Regards the composition of the budget measures as "far from ideal" and "the government's strategy once these tax hikes and pension changes expire is unclear." Reckons the measures won't boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's confidence to cut rates, at least not in the near term. Adds that the looming issue of rising government external debt payment obligations over 2011-2012 remains an issue. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish Riksbank's Deputy Governor Barbro Wickman-Parak reiterated her backing for the monetary policy committee's current repo rate forecast and the decision to raise the repo rate to 0.75% in September in a speech Thursday, showing she belongs to the "hawkish" part of the Riksbank's Executive Board, says Nordea. "There is little doubt that Wickman-Parak is prepared to vote for rate hikes at the next few meetings," Nordea notes, adding the speech shows a belief that the Swedish economy is strong, which justifies a less expansionary monetary policy. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0753 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CHF just printed a fresh all-time low of 0.9463, however, the downside is getting limited, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones. Price action is completely dominated by the 4-month downtrend channel that has its parameters as 0.98 and 0.9441 Thursday. Jones sees risk to the channel base but below here has 0.9350 as the maximum downside target. USD/CHF now at 0.9473. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT [Dow Jones] So why did the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore moves have such a big impact? \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  suggests two reason. First, Singapore is seen as a barometer of Asian economic growth and the MAS tightening was made into strength. "Stronger emerging market Asian economic growth, despite a weak US economy, continues to feed the flow of investment funds into EM Asia and countries with strong links to EM Asia [such as Australia] and away from the US," the bank says. Second, BarCap adds, "investors may read the MAS's move as a sign of greater willingness on the part of other EM Asian central banks to allow further USD depreciation." (nick.hastings@dowjones.com) [ 10-14-10 0426ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0006p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
