\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Czech Unemployment, Ruble In Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By William Mauldin   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 05:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par    Local/GMT \par    0900/0700 Czech ministry releases September jobless data \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Czech unemployment is expected to remain high at 8.5% in September, although a touch lower from the 8.6% recorded in August, as employers are wary about creating new jobs. Prospects also remain hazy due to the planned layoffs among public servants by the Czech government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "The situation on the job market will likely continue to worsen with payroll cuts by national and local government offices and agencies," Pavel Mertlik, the chief economist at Raiffeisenbank in Prague, said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 24.5020-20 24.5570-70 -0.22  24.5640 24.5160  -7.15 \par EUR/HUF 274.07-57  274.12-42  -0.02  274.32  274.46   +1.29 \par EUR/PLN 3.9763-13  3.9715-47  +0.12  3.9791  3.9740   -3.15 \par USD/RUB 29.806-26  29.790-96  +0.05  29.862  29.755   -1.67 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The zloty should oscillate tightly around the 3.97 level against the euro Friday, a dealer said. In the mid-term, the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is being underpinned by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Treasury Ministry\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's plans to sell a 10% stake in power utility PGE, as well as smaller peer Enea.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech koruna is expected to continue to trade at its current range just above 25.500 against the euro as investors look for the new direction on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Some traders don't rule out the koruna to weaken in the medium-term, largely on profit-taking after solid recent gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the Hungarian forint's softening against the euro Thursday, investor sentiment toward Hungary and the central and Eastern European region has been positive, Raiffeisen Bank said. The outlook for the forint is seen as bright as long as the 2011 budget draft will prove that the government plans to offset planned tax cuts with expenditure cuts and other measures, Raiffeisen added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ruble may strengthen gradually toward the level of 34 to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dollar-euro basket, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland Group PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  said, at which point the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's progress will probably be checked by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Other banks warned that problems in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's current-account balance and trade balance may weigh on the ruble in the short term, as momentum from a central banker's bullish comments this week wears off.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    FIXED INCOME \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS--The three-year bond yield was at 6.4% Thursday afternoon from 6.35% late Wednesday. The five-year was at 6.55% from 6.45%, and the 10-year was at 6.72% from 6.60%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BOND YIELDS--The average yield on two-year bonds was unchanged late Wednesday at 4.61%, the five-year yield was at 5.03% compared with 5.02%, and the 10-year was at 5.45% from 5.44%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BOND YIELD--The yield on the benchmark 10-year paper was at 3.21% late Thursday from 3.20% late Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Polish bond market should bound back Friday, as long as core bond markets rise, a dealer says. He added that comments by Polish monetary policy council members which clear up the mixed messages the council has been sending thus far is something investors are eagerly awaiting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian bond yields will likely remain trading within a tight range of 30 to 35 basis points until the government publishes the long-awaited 2011 budget draft and longer-term economic program. Bond yields could weaken somewhat in the coming days, bouncing back from the bottom of this range Thursday morning, but after about 15 to 20 basis points of weakening they'll once again move down, one trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                2626.43   loss     37.67    dn  1.4% \par BUX                 23536.46   gain     151.27   up  0.7% \par PX                   1145.7    gain      2.6     up  0.2% \par RTS                  1584.29   gain      6.0     up  0.4% \par MICEX                1468.38   loss      6.8     dn  0.5% \par Stoxx EU Enlarged 15 2273.22   loss      5.22    dn  0.5% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: The WIG20, Warsaw's large-cap index, is likely to edge down Friday, as it continues to be weighed by the 10% stake in PGE that Poland's Treasury is in the process of selling, a dealer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Czech stocks are seen mixed Friday but New World Resources may face sell off pressure, hurting the overall markets, on signs that investors at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lubelski Wegiel Bogdanka SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  view NWR's offer for the Polish miner as too low. Selloffs in NWR shares may accelerate if its takeover bid for Bogdanka stalls or collapses, some traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: The Hungarian stock market is seen trading range-bound Friday. Market participants are eagerly awaiting news from the government on economy policy or further extraordinary taxes on the telecommunications and energy sectors, mentioned earlier by the cabinet but wrapped in silence for a while.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN MOSCOW: Russian shares may be little changed Friday while oil traders, and the Moscow equity investors who depend on them, await U.S. employment data. Still, the RTS is coming close to the psychologically important 1600 level, which could prompt a true rally if crossed, a local broker said. Gold miners \f28 \uc2 \f2 Polyus Gold\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Petropavlovsk will be in the spotlight again after erratic moves Thursday. [ 10-07-10 2303ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: French President Nicolas Sarkozy complained to his Russian counterpart Dmitry Medvedev on Thursday that health checks on French livestock and beef imports into Russia were disadvantaging French farmers. Sarkozy said Russian import authorities enforced tough vaccination rules on French livestock which discouraged Russian farmers\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Russian President Dmitry Medvedev on Thursday renewed his country's proposal for a new European Security Pact to replace existing security and problem-solving mechanisms in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: GE's (NYSE: GE) multi-megawatt wind turbine technology has entered commercial service in Poland and is helping the country meet its renewable energy requirements, GE announced at the official opening of the Nekla windpark. With Poland's high wind potential and large agricultural areas, wind technology will play a key role to help the country meet its target to have 15 percent of its total energy supplied by renewable resources by the year 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mid Europa Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  Thursday said it has bought 100% of Poland's medical diagnostic company, Med-Sport, as part of its commitment to the Polish healthcare market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Czech Energeticky a Prumyslovy Holding, or EP Holding, has offered the highest price among four bidders for the majority stake in Poland's state-controlled power utility \f28 \uc2 \f2 Enea SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ENA.WA), two people familiar with the situation told Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Dead fish floated in the Danube Thursday after a toxic sludge spill that killed four people in Hungary reached Europe's second longest river, threatening to decimate marine life, officials said. Blood-red sludge reached the main branch of the Danube at around midday (1000 GMT), after wiping out all life in the smaller Marcal tributary, said Tibor Dobson, the regional chief of Hungary's disaster relief services. "I can confirm that we have seen sporadic losses of fish in the main branch of the Danube," Dobson said. "The fish have been sighted at the confluence of the Raba with the Danube," where water samples had shown a pH value of 9.1, he said. "In order to save the river's ecosystem, the pH level must be brought down below 8," Dobson said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: The toxic sludge that poisoned waterways in western Hungary will have a limited impact on the country's main river, the Danube, an official said on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: MAL, the company at the center of the massive toxic spill that has devastated parts of western Hungary and poisoned rivers, offered Thursday to pay compensation to families hit by the disaster. MAL Hungarian Aluminium Production and Trade Company--whose owners are among the richest individuals in the country--plans to pay "rapid aid" of EUR360 to each family, or 110,000 euros in total, said Karoly Tili, the mayor of one of the hardest-hit villages.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romania has asked Hungary for more information on the toxic sludge spill that reached the Danube Thursday, while gearing up for the risk of drinking water contamination in towns along the river.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romanian President Traian Basescu on Thursday asked parliament to amend pension reform legislation, arguing women should be able to retire at 63, two years earlier than men.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Several thousand protesters rallied outside Bulgaria's parliament Thursday against planned pension system reforms that might see people having to work longer before they retire.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By William Mauldin, Dow Jones Newswires, +7 495 232 9194;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 william.mauldin@dowjones.com [ 10-07-10 2304ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a800024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
