\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bk To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  If Ruble Strengthens\b0\par\par\uc2 981 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 16:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1418 GMT [Dow Jones] The Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , buying foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , if the ruble were to strengthen much beyond RUB34 to its dollar-euro basket, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Despite Russia's declining current account surplus, estimated at $60 billion to $75 billion this year and some $50 billion next year, the ruble will probably tend to appreciate, assuming there is no jump in global risk aversion, broker says. Ruble on Thursday strengthens slightly, to 35.05 to the basket, versus 35.07 late Wednesday. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1414 GMT [Dow Jones] The five-year Treasury note's yield hits a record low of 1.119%, smashing the previos record low of 1.122% just hit Wednesday. Five-year notes are considered the biggest beneficiaries on Fed QE2 as the yield is closely linked to home refinancings. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1410 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazil will likely see inflationary pressures continue in the short-term, but the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably just keep a watchful eye on prices until early next year, says economist Flavio Serrano of BES Investimentos. BES expects a 1.5-percentage-point hike, starting in the first quarter of 2011, to force consumer prices to converge on the government's year-end 2011 target of 4.5%, Serrano says.(Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1407 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD hovering near loss on day as US stocks hold lower. Pair at 1.3933 from 1.3929 late Wed, according to EBS via CQG. EUR/USD had gained to 1.4030, but investors are not likely to want to push the pair dramatically higher--and might even shed some risk--ahead of Fri's US non-farm payroll numbers, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 's Amelia Bourdeau.\par \par \uc2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1406 GMT [Dow Jones] The 2-yr note's yield keeps its slide toward zero. The yield just hit a fresh record low of 0.355%, not far away from the fed-funds target rate of zero-0.25% range.(min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1406 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar-weakening trend witnessed over the last few weeks is entering into its "final phase" says Nomura. Anticipation that the Fed will embark on more QE has been behind the move, the bank says, adding that at this stage USD "may already embed the bulk of the impact from further QE." Also, the bank notes positioning has moved toward dollar shorts in the past two weeks. EUR/USD trades at 1.3943 from a previous close at 1.3917.(clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1405 GMT [Dow Jones] The job opening rate remained unchanged in August at 2.4%, according the Labor Department's so-called JOLTS survey. There were 3.2 million jobs available in August and the hires rate of 3.2% and separations rate of 3.2% were also steady. "Although the month-to-month change is small, the number of job openings has risen by 863,000 (37 percent) since the most recent series trough of 2.3 million in July 2009," the government said. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1404 GMT [Dow Jones] The 30-year bond is losing ground ahead of supply news while other maturities rise on QE2 bets, pushing the yield between the 10-yr and 30-yr to record high of 131 bps. The trade also reflects wagers that Fed's QE2 is likely to benefit shorter-dated notes more than the long bond.\par \par \uc2 (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1356 GMT [Dow Jones] Polish bond prices edge down on the longer end of the curve as investors wait for more Polish rate-setters to comment about when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should begin its tightening cycle, a dealer says. He added he expects prices to rebound Friday if core bond markets rise. Average yield on 2-year bonds is unchanged from late Wednesday at 4.61%, 5-year at 5.03% from 5.02%, 10-year at 5.45% from 5.44%. EUR/PLN trades at 3.9639 from 3.9505 late Wednesday, USD/PLN at 2.8396 from 2.8460. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1355 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar fell earlier to Y82.10, its current low for the downtrend beginning at the August 1998 high of Y147.71. See chart at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4700"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4700 }}}\par \par \uc2 This long-term downtrend is targeting initial support at Y78.75. But a move above Y83.24 would be the signal for an uptrend to Y85.49.\par \par \uc2 (stephen.cox@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1353 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD giving up substantial gains above 1.40 to emerge only modestly higher on day, at 1.3957 from 1.3929 late Wed, according to EBS via CQG, as US stocks take turn into the red, leading investors out of riskier assets. DJIA down 0.13%. AUD/USD also off highest level since 1983, trading recently at 0.9853 from 0.9774 late Wed. Pair had ticked as high as 0.9918 in wake of much better-than-expected Australian jobs data. Despite brief respite for USD selling, major trend is USD-negative, says Standard Bank's Steven Barrow. (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1350 GMT [Dow Jones] Riksbank First Deputy Governor Svante Oberg sticks to his message of gradual rate hikes and unchanged rate path in a speech, SEB notes. "There are no signs that Oberg is presently willing to revise down path due to global worries or SEK strengthening in a situation where the Swedish economy is growing strongly and resource utilisation increasing," SEB says. EUR/SEK trades at 9.2845 from 9.317 before the speech.(ian.edmondson@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 1018ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a7000a3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
