\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Russia cbank calm on rouble, inflation, rates\b0\par\par\uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 13:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Chances of rouble appreciation greater than depreciation\par \par \uc2 * Inflation mostly driven by food, cbank official says\par \par \uc2 * No plans to hike rates or reserve requirements for now\par \par \uc2 * Economists wary after rouble losses, Sept inflation spike\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details, background, Kudrin)\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Oct 5 (Reuters) - Russian inflation is benign, the rouble may appreciate soon and there is no need to hike reserve requirements or rates for now, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Tuesday, moving to dispel concerns about the economy.\par \par \uc2 "(In) the short-term perspective, the chances of rouble appreciation are greater than of depreciation. In the medium term they are equal," the bank's first deputy chairman Alexei Ulyukayev told reporters on the sidelines of a conference.\par \par \uc2 In September, the rouble tumbled more than 3.3 percent against its euro-dollar basket <RUS=MCX>, losing almost all its 2010 gains in less than two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The move was caused by strong demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which eclipsed a positive background of strong oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Inflation also surged as the impact of a severe summer drought filtered through to non-food prices, data showed on Monday, raising expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to keep price pressures in check. [ID:nLDE6931JS]\par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate is set by the balance between supply and demand," Ulyukayev earlier told VTB Capital's \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Calling forum, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  played a marginal role, with sales of around $1.3 billion in the whole of September.\par \par \uc2 That compares to daily turnover of around $3-4 billion on the MICEX exchange.\par \par \uc2 'LOCALISED SHOCK'\par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev said the spike in inflation was largely driven by the impact of the drought on food costs and thus the long-term outlook on prices remains comfortable.\par \par \uc2 "It is not the result of fundamentals but of a localised shock on the food market due to weather conditions. This... should not last for more than six to nine months, especially as global prices are not rising," Ulyukayev said.\par \par \uc2 "For now we think the situation is under control."\par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev reiterated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mantra from its Sept. 28 meeting that interest rates would likely remain on hold at record lows in coming months.\par \par \uc2 Most analysts expect rate hikes from early 2011 but do not rule out other measures -- such as hikes in banks' reserve requirements -- before the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 "There is a danger of falling into a big macroeconomic trap, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Alexander Morozov said recently.\par \par \uc2 "By not influencing inflationary expectations, the risk is growing that such expectations will start to rise and companies will increase prices rather than production, thus further suppressing demand in the economy."\par \par \uc2 But Ulyukayev also played down the possibility of other monetary tightening moves.\par \par \uc2 "Today we do not see the need for (reserve requirement hikes). We are not worried by capital inflows," he said.\par \par \uc2 "We expect that in the fourth quarter there will be a small capital inflow. For the year as a whole there will be (an outflow) of under $12 billion, even less than that. It will be an insignificant number."\par \par \uc2 Russia saw record outflows of $134 billion in 2008, followed by drain of $57 billion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Alexei Kudrin, speaking at the same conference, forecast that inflows will soon resume, boosted by foreign direct investment. He sees FDI topping $40 billion this year after $36 billion in 2009 and reaching $50 billion annually in the next two years.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Yelena Fabrichnaya and Darya Korsunskaya; Writing by Toni Vorobyova; Editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-CBANK/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101005e6a5000q6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
