\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: No Central Bk Move To Stem RUB Weakness -RenCap\b0\par\par\uc2 1,245 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 10:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones]--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the ruble, but the wave of stop loss selling seems to have passed, says Anton Nikitin of Rennaisance Capital. "Still, the market is cautious and wary of strengthening," he says. Ruble is trading at 35.48 against the dollar-euro basket. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY turns lower as strong EUR, gains in most regional stocks support risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including CNY. On OTC, USD/CNY hits low at 6.7240 vs 6.7322 intraday high, 6.7248 last close; pair last at 6.7248. "The renewed yuan strength is all driven by the euro's move," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. "I think it's likely the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to set the dollar-yuan central parity at fresh record low next week, though the magnitude of the decline will be small." Next support tipped at 6.7200; "exporters' demand to exchange dollars into yuan in the deliverable forwards market has been decreasing in the past 1-2 months, indicating their orders may not be that strong." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0832 GMT [Dow Jones] Safe haven Treasurys are down in London as risk sentiment improves, with equities firm in Europe and the EUR higher across the board. Recent economic data in the US have also tempered worries over a slide back in to recession, easing talk of additional asset purchases by the Fed. Friday's data releases include CPI data at 1230 GMT and the University of Michigan consumer survey at 1355 GMT. The 2-year note is lower at 99-23/32 to yield 0.517%, the 10-year note is down at 98-17/32 to yield 2.795%. The 30-year bond is down at 98-26+/32, yielding 3.941%. December bunds are down 0.19 at 129.20. December gilts are 0.17 lower at 122.17. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR is up as risk sentiment improves. Apart from Spain's and France's successful bond auctions on Thursday, the mood is being lifted by the lack of any disappointing economic data and a general rally in global equities. Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the AUD, are major gainers too, helped by the rise in global liquidity from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the prospect of more QE from the Fed. The JPY is fairly stable against the USD as the market waits to see if the BOJ will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  any more. The USD is up a little at Y85.88 while the EUR is up at $1.3146. The GBP is up at $1.5717 despite disappointing UK data on Thursday. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower late at 3.1040 vs Wednesday's 3.1160, tracking broad USD weakness vs regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , EUR, says local dealer. Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not spotted today but players think \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could buy "at the MYR3.1000 level and that forms a psychological barrier," dealer says. Expects support to stay firm today but MYR likely well bid in near term. Tips 3.1000-3.1200 range for Monday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT [Dow Jones] STI +5.9% so far this year but upside would have been more if not for dismal performance by key stocks like banks, developers, rig builders, telcos, says DMG. Notes index's year-to-date gains driven mainly by 5 stocks: Genting Singapore (G13.SG), \uc2 Jardine Strategic\uc2  (J36.SG), \uc2 Jardine Matheson\uc2  (J37.SG), Jardine C&C (C07.SG), \uc2 Hongkong Land\uc2  (H78.SG); "if we exclude these five stocks, the STI would only have risen 0.9% year to date, suggesting lacklustre performance from other key STI stocks." Notes bullish investors hopeful core index components will perform well to lift STI over next few months, but "we believe the STI could trend down" given net interest margin squeeze on banks, fallout from recent housing-market curbs, depleting orderbooks of rig builders. Suggests defensive strategy, with top picks being ComfortDelgro (C52.SG), \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG), M1 (B2F.SG). STI +0.5% at 3080.96. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0809 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty is mixed Friday, down against the euro and up against the dollar. One dealer sees the EUR/PLN pair remaining in the 3.92-3.96 range, where it has stayed in recent days. "We'll likely test 3.96, but should remain below it," he adds. EUR/PLN is at 3.9547 from 3.9467 late Thursday, USD/PLN at 3.0057 from 3.0144. Average yield of 2-year bonds is flat since Wednesday afternoon at 4.63%, 5-year 5.12% from 5.10% late Thursday, 10-year at 5.48% from 5.45%. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0807 GMT [Dow Jones] The upside squeeze in EUR/CHF has further to run says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  with today's break above 1.3340 pointing to at least 1.3480. The bank adds a higher close Friday would open the topside toward 1.39 and also give reason to change its medium-term outlook on the cross to more neutral/bullish from the current bearish stance. EUR/CHF now at 1.3372. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0802 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong jobs and house price data may have vindicated the Riksbank's rate hike and helped the SEK. But, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 , Sunday's general election in Sweden might increase volatility. The bank is looking for EUR/SEK to find support at 9.18-9.20. The pair is now at 9.2245. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0800 GMT [Dow Jones] The increased global liquidity coming from Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and more QE from the Fed will help commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "We expect the JPY to remain under pressure allowing the commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in particular to push higher," the bank says, forecasting that the AUD/USD will rise further to the 0.9700 area and the AUD/JPY has the upside potential into the 84.00 area. AUD/USD now at 0.9472 and AUD/JPY is at 81.31. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0755 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR touches fresh near three-month low of 45.85 on anticipated foreign fund inflows after \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  takes aggressive rate action Thursday, says senior trader with private bank. Pair recently at 45.91 after rising to 46.12 in session, 46.15 late Thursday. Pair touched 45.88 on June 22. Trader says, selling pressure from NDF market driving USD down while gains in local stock markets support local unit. Adds, some amount of oil-related buying of greenback may help recoup losses partially. Sensex +0.9%. Trader tips pair in 45.80-45.95 band for session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF needs to clear the 1.3355 level before it can consolidate at higher levels, according to \uc2 Zuercher Kantonalbank\uc2 . "Our target on the upside is 1.3450 though we could see short-term consolidation down to 1.3250," says technical analyst Christian Eugster. EUR/CHF at 1.3373. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com) [ 09-17-10 0437ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100917e69h0005v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
