\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Ruble's Weakness Is Buying Opportunity\b0\par\par\uc2 1,219 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 16:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1430 GMT [Dow Jones] The ruble's current weakness should be viewed as a buying opportunity "to get in on the global growth trade at a cheap level," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. "If optimism regarding the global economy continues, higher oil prices should support the ruble. The ruble is the worst performing emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far in the third quarter, which we do not think is warranted given the improved global outlook." Additionally, the ruble may be helped by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which recently published a paper calling for "gradually changing" the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's floating corridor to increase exchange rate flexibility, the Brown Brother says. Ruble closes at 35.67 against the dollar-euro basket, its weakest levels since February. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1429 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts fall Thursday after demand at an auction fell for the 2nd day running as low yields deterred buyers. The DMO received a cover ratio of 1.68 at a sale of 10-year gilts while the yield tail also widened to 0.5bp vs a cover ratio of 2.45 and a tail of 0.2bp at the last sale. Safe haven bunds also fell after Spanish bond auctions attracted solid demand, amid signs appetite for low yielding government bonds is waning. "Gilts have had an exceptional run but given inflation above 3% and expectations that it'll remain sticky, 10-year yields around 3.10% do look a bit low," says a strategist. Data out earlier showed UK retail sales unexpectedly fell in August although UK's year ahead CPI expectations rose to 3.4% in Aug vs 3.3% in May, the BOE's quarterly poll showed. December gilts are down 0.78 at 122.20 and the 10-year gilt is down at 112.971 to yield 3.154%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1427 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD hits intraday high, at 1.3115 from 1.3012 late Wed, according to EBS via CQG, as investors continue to turn to common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in wake of week's successful euro-zone government debt auctions. Investors also might see EUR as a safety play, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's Alan Ruskin, at least as they wait for US economy to emerge from slow patch and await any further moves by Japan to temper yen strength after Wed's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1424 GMT [Dow Jones] Order books went subject on Ukraine's two-tranche bond deal. Guidance on the 5-year issue is 6 7/8%, while the 10-year bond is expected to price with a yield of 7 7/8%, said a person familiar with the debt. The offer comes two months after Ukraine delayed a bond sale due to financing made available by the IMF. Bookrunners are \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and VTB. The country's bonds are trading up on the grey market, said an investor, in a sign of confidence in the notes. Yet, back in July, several investors were hesitant to buy Ukrainian paper under 8%, given corporates regularly issue above that. The IMf deal is credited for the change. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1422 GMT [Dow Jones] After the Brazilian presidential election, looking at the 2011 budget will be a key indicator for how much more positive sentiment on Brazil is warranted, says Shelly Shetty, head of Fitch's LatAm sovereign team, speaking on a panel in NY. In particular, ratings agency looking to see how new govt will raise minimum wage, which inevitably will increase social security costs, to get a clue on their approach to spending. "The greater the signal they give early on, [the more] we would have faith on policy continuity," Shetty says. (kejal.vyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1420 GMT [Dow Jones] Economic deterioration -- as revealed in negative Phila Fed business index -- fails to generate flight to safety bid in U.S. interest rate futures. Prices for quarterly Eurodollar futures hit intraday lows, with quarterly 2012-through-2013 contracts down 2.5 to 7.5 BP. Long-term Tsy futures fall to fresh intraday lows. Dec 10-yr Tsy notes sink to 123-22+, with support at Mon's 1 1/4-month low of 122-30. Dec 30-yr Tsy bonds drop to 129-23. Support at Mon's 1-month bottom of 129-05.(howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1419 GMT [Dow Jones] Canada's economy is coming off a "sugar high" from stimulus measures as well as the unleashing of pent-up consumer and business demand, and is now showing signs of a maturing recovery, says TD Economics. TD is forecasting GDP growth averaging 2% in '11, a cut of a half percentage point from its June Forecast. The economy's natural "cruising speed" has slowed from its traditional 3% to the 1.5% to 2.2% range in response to factors like changing demographics and poor productivity growth, TD says. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1413 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian investors were buying agency mortgage and debt securities in July, but they bought less than when fears of an European credit crisis were high. July purchases by foreign investors was $17.3B, lower than $18.2B in June, and a sharp drop from the $27.4B in May, according to TIC data. Central banks also sold $4.1B of these bonds, taking advantage of the high prices. Asian investors have been intermittent buyers of agency mortgage bonds in the past few weeks, as uncertainty about the pace of prepayment muddles the market. (prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1410 GMT [Dow Jones] Fitch Ratings says it expects to assign a long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating of triple-B-minus to upcoming 10-year USD subordinated fixed-rate notes planned by \uc2 Itau Unibanco Holding SA\uc2  (ITUB, ITUB4.BR). Proceeds will be used for general purposes, enabling the bank's new business generation, Fitch says. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1408 GMT [Dow Jones] Major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs were little changed in wake of slightly worse-than-expected Philla Fed survey, with EUR/USD remaining higher at 1.3064 from 1.3012 late Wed, according to EBS via CQG. USD/JPY holding onto gains after Wed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ticking slightly higher to 85.82 from 85.63. Investor eyes on Treasury's Geithner, who was scheduled to begin testimony to Congress at 10 am ET. Geithner expected to comment on China \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy; after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , strategists waiting to see if he also comments on Japan's policy. (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1406 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys hold losses, showing muted response to the Phila Fed report so far. The data show Sept index is -0.7, a bit worse than the forecast of 0, but it is big improvement from -7.7 in August. 10-year note off 9/32 to yield 2.759%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1406 GMT [Dow Jones] The five-year Treasury notes are hard to borrow in overnight repos, with a rate of zero as traders scramble for the notes to adjust trading bets. Other maturities are trading normally. (min.zeng@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 1030ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g000ap\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
