\par\par \b \uc2 Russia bought gold for reserves in conservative '09\b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2010\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, May 17 (Reuters) - Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stuck to a conservative investment policy for its reserves in 2009, buying gold and reducing holdings of non-sovereign bonds, according to its annual report submitted to parliament on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The report also appeared to show that Russia had increased the share of dollars in its reserves at the expense of euros as of end-2009, but the data excludes conversion transactions, which have yet to be calculated, and the regulator has said that the nominal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  structure remains unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Excluding the not-yet accounted conversion transactions, Russia's reserve assets were held 44.5 percent in dollars, 43.8 percent in euros, 10.4 percent in sterling and 1.3 percent in yen. That compared to 41.5 percent dollars, 47.5 percent euros, 9.7 percent sterling and 1.3 percent yen at end-2008.\par \par \uc2 The figures include Russia's gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as well as assets against which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has obligation. \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, mostly in the dollar/rouble pair, and does not rebalance its reserves on a daily basis, meaning the reading for any particular day does not necessarily reflect the true structure of reserves.\par \par \uc2 Last year's annual report had prompted speculation that Russia was trying to limit its exposure to the dollar, but this was later disproved by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials. [ID:nLK576828]\par \par \uc2 Officials have said the nominal structure of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  part of Russia's reserves remains unchanged at 47 percent U.S. dollars, 41 percent euros and 10 percent sterling -- the same as it was a year ago.\par \par \uc2 The report also showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought 142.87 tonnes of gold, increasing its holdings of the metal by 30 percent during 2009. The non-gold part of reserves was spread between deposits and accounts at foreign banks' accounting for 5.4 percent, repo transaction with foreign counterparties at 1.4 percent and foreign bonds at 93.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Russia's reserves, the world's third largest, were worth $440.6 billion at end-2009. (Reporting by Yelena Fabrichnaya; Writing by Toni Vorobyova; editing by Jason Webb)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-CBANK/RESERVES|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|PCM   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100517e65h000u3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
