    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation has not\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 waived any of its sovereign\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 immunity, and has not submitted to\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the jurisdiction of any court, in\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 respect of its obligations under\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Listing and Admission to Trading....................                       \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Application has been made for the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bonds to be listed on the Official\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 List and admitted to trading on the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk Factors.......................................................... Risks associated with the Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 generally include: (1) there can be\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 no assurance that Russia's credit\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rating will not change; (2) the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 terms of the Bonds may be modified\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 pursuant to their\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 collective-action provisions; (3)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the Bonds may not be a suitable\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 investment for all investors; (4)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 legal investment considerations may\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 restrict certain investments; and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (5) the Bonds may have no\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 established trading market when\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 issued, and, if a market does\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 develop, it may not be liquid.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks associated with Russia\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 generally include: (1) any material\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 reduction in the price of crude oil\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 or natural gas may materially and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 adversely affect the revenues and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 financial condition of the Russian\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federation; (2) the Russian\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federation's economy, like many\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 economies, is vulnerable to\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 external shocks, and the current\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 global economic crisis, significant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 future economic difficulties of the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian Federation's major trading\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 partners, and more general\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "contagion" effects all could have\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 a material adverse effect on the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian Federation's economic\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 growth and the market for the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bonds; (3) Russia is a foreign\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 sovereign state, has not waived any\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rights to sovereign immunity it may\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 have in any jurisdiction and has\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 not submitted to the jurisdiction\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of any court and accordingly it may\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 be difficult to obtain or enforce\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                                                             judgments against it; and (4)\par                                                                             rouble depreciation risks.\par                                                                            \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These risk factors are described in\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                                                             greater detail under "Risk\par                                                                             Factors."\par                                  RISK FACTORS\par \par The Russian Federation believes that the factors described below\par represent the principal risks inherent in investing in the Bonds. All of these\par factors are contingencies, which may or may not occur, and the Russian\par Federation is not in a position to express a view on the likelihood that one\par or more of these contingencies will in fact occur.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation does not represent that the statements below are an exhaustive list of the risks inherent in investing in the Bonds, and the Russian Federation may be unable to pay amounts due on the Bonds for reasons not described below. Prospective investors should read the detailed information set out elsewhere in this Prospectus and reach their own views prior to making any investment decision.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks Relating to the Russian Federation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any material reduction in the price of crude oil or natural gas may materially adversely affect the revenues and financial condition of the Russian Federation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation's revenues are affected by international oil\par and natural gas prices, which have fluctuated widely in recent years in\par response to global supply and demand, general economic conditions, competition\par from other energy sources and other factors. In 2008, for example, the peak\par price of Urals oil was U.S.$137.40 per barrel and the average monthly price\par per barrel was U.S.$94.40, compared to a peak price of U.S.$78.10 and an\par average monthly price of U.S.$61.00 in 2009. The average price per barrel of\par Urals oil in the first two months of 2010 was U.S.$74.30, and the spot price\par of Urals oil was U.S.$81.03 on 1 April 2010. Russia's 2010 Budget Law assumes\par an average price per barrel of Urals oil of U.S.$58, increasing to U.S.$59 in\par 2011 and to U.S.$60 in 2012. See "Public Finance--2010 Federal Budget Law."\par Natural gas prices have likewise fluctuated significantly in recent years, in\par part because crude oil prices often provide a benchmark for natural gas\par prices. In 2008, the peak price of natural gas was U.S.$13.31 per mmbtu and\par the average monthly price was U.S.$8.85 per mmbtu, compared to a peak price of\par U.S.$6.14 and an average monthly price of U.S.$3.96 in 2009. While the\par existing price for Urals oil is substantially above the price assumed in the\par 2010 Budget Law for 2010, 2011 and 2012, any material reduction in the price\par of crude oil or natural gas will have a significant effect on Russia's\par budgetary revenues and foreign reserves, and may materially adversely affect\par the Russian Federation's financial condition, including its ability to make\par payments on the Bonds.\par \par The Russian Federation's economy, like many economies, is\par vulnerable to external shocks. The current global economic crisis, significant\par future economic difficulties of the Russian Federation's major trading\par partners, and more general "contagion" effects all could have a material\par adverse effect on the Russian Federation's economic growth and the market for\par the Bonds\par \par The Russian Federation's economy and finances have been adversely\par affected by the current global economic crisis. Real GDP, for example,\par declined by 7.9% in 2009 compared to 2008, and the average unemployment rate\par (using the definition of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Labour Organisation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "ILO"))\par increased from 7.1% in the fourth quarter of 2008 to 8.0% in the fourth\par quarter of 2009. The value of the rouble against the dollar also declined in\par 2009, by 12.2% in real terms, as did the value of the real effective exchange\par rate, which fell by 5.6%.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation's economy remains vulnerable to further external shocks, including the ongoing global economic crisis. A significant decline in the economic growth of the EU or any of the Russian Federation's other major trading partners could have a material adverse effect on the Russian Federation's balance of trade and adversely affect the Russian Federation's economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Events occurring in one geographic or financial market sometimes\par result in an entire region or class of investments being disfavoured by\par international investors--so-called "contagion effects." The Russian Federation\par has been adversely affected by contagion effects in the past, and it is\par possible that the market for Russian investments, including the Bonds, will be\par similarly affected in the future by negative economic or financial\par developments in countries whose economies or credit ratings are similar to\par those of the Russian Federation.\par \par There can be no assurance that the current economic crisis, or a\par future external economic crisis, will not have a negative effect on investors'\par confidence in Russia's markets or on the Russian Federation's economy or\par ability to raise capital in the international debt markets, all of which could\par have a material adverse effect on the trading price of the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No waiver of immunity; Enforcement of liabilities\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation is a sovereign state and has not waived any\par rights to sovereign immunity it may have in any jurisdiction. Accordingly, the\par Russian Federation may be entitled to immunity from suit in any action or\par proceeding in any jurisdiction arising out of the Bonds, and the Russian\par Federation and its assets, properties and revenues may be entitled to immunity\par in any related enforcement action. The Russian Federation has also not\par submitted to the jurisdiction of any court, agreed that any disputes may be\par resolved in any forum or appointed any agent for service of process in any\par jurisdiction, in connection with any action or proceeding arising out of the\par Bonds. As a result of the foregoing, it may be difficult or impossible for an\par investor to obtain a judgment against the Russian Federation in a foreign\par court and/or have such judgment recognised and/or enforced in any\par jurisdiction.\par \par A final judgment rendered by a foreign court will generally be\par recognised and enforced in the Russian Federation if there is an international\par treaty in effect between the Russian Federation and the country where the\par judgment is rendered providing for the mutual recognition and enforcement of\par judgments. There are no international treaties in effect today providing for\par the mutual recognition and enforcement of foreign judgments rendered by courts\par in the Russian Federation and courts in most of the countries where Bond\par investors are likely to reside, including the United States and the United\par Kingdom.\par \par In the absence of an applicable treaty, enforcement of a final\par judgment rendered by a foreign court may still be recognized by a Russian\par court on the basis of reciprocity, if courts of the country where the foreign\par judgment is rendered have previously enforced judgments issued by Russian\par courts. While Russian courts have recently recognized and enforced English and\par Dutch court judgments on these grounds, the existence of reciprocity must be\par established at the time the recognition and enforcement of a foreign judgment\par is sought, and it is not possible to predict whether a Russian court will in\par the future recognize and enforce on the basis of reciprocity a judgment issued\par by a foreign court, including an English or Dutch court.\par \par Even if an applicable international treaty is in effect or a\par foreign judgment might otherwise be recognised and enforced on the basis of\par reciprocity, the recognition and enforcement of a foreign judgment will in all\par events be subject to exceptions and limitations provided for in Russian law.\par For example, a Russian court may refuse to recognise or enforce a foreign\par judgment if its recognition or enforcement would contradict Russian public\par policy.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a result, it may be difficult to obtain recognition or enforcement in the Russian Federation of a foreign judgment in respect of the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Exchange rate risks\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The depreciation of the rouble against the dollar or other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may adversely affect the financial condition of the Russian Federation, as well as the Russian Federation's ability to repay its debt denominated in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than the rouble, including amounts due under the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Risks Relating to the Bonds\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit ratings may not reflect all risks\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation has been assigned sovereign credit ratings\par of Baa1 (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  ), BBB (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) and BBB (Fitch). Each of these\par ratings has a stable outlook. A credit rating is not a recommendation to buy,\par sell or hold the Bonds, and is subject to revision or withdrawal at any time\par by the assigning rating agency. No assurance can be given that the Russian\par Federation's current or future sovereign ratings will not be downgraded or\par withdrawn entirely, if circumstances in the future so warrant in the judgment\par of the assigning rating agency. The Russian Federation has no obligation to\par inform Bondholders of any revision, downgrade or withdrawal of its current or\par future sovereign credit ratings. A suspension, downgrade or withdrawal at any\par time of a credit rating assigned to the Russian Federation may adversely\par affect the market price of the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Modification\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each of the 2015 Bonds and the 2020 Bonds may generally be modified\par with the consent of persons holding or representing at least 662/3 per cent.\par of the votes cast at a meeting of Bondholders called for that purpose, so long\par as the meeting is attended by two or more persons holding or representing a\par clear majority of the aggregate principal amount of such outstanding Bonds (or\par by two or more persons being or\par \par representing Bondholders in the case of an adjourned meeting). The\par maturity, principal amount, interest rate and other payment terms of the 2015\par Bonds and the 2020 Bonds may be modified with the consent of persons holding\par or representing at least 75 per cent. of the votes cast at a meeting of\par Bondholders called for that purpose, so long as the meeting is attended by\par persons holding or representing at least 75 per cent. of the aggregate\par principal amount of such outstanding Bonds (or by persons holding or\par representing at least 25 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of such\par outstanding Bonds in the case of an adjourned meeting). Under these\par collective-action provisions, modifications to the terms of the Bonds that\par are so approved are binding on all Bondholders, including Bondholders who did\par not attend or vote at the Bondholders' meeting or who voted against the\par approved modifications.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bonds may not be a suitable investment for all investors\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Potential investors should determine the suitability of an investment in the Bonds in light of their own circumstances. In particular, investors should:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - understand thoroughly the terms of the Bonds;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - have sufficient knowledge and experience to make a meaningful evaluation of the Bonds, the information contained in this Prospectus, and the merits and risks of investing in the Bonds;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - have access to, and knowledge of, appropriate analytical tools to evaluate, in the context of their particular financial situation, an investment in the Bonds and the effect that investment will have on their overall investment portfolio;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - have sufficient financial resources and liquidity to bear all of the risks of an investment in the Bonds; and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - be able to evaluate (either alone or with the help of a financial advisor) how the Bonds will perform under changing conditions as well as possible economic, interest rate and other developments that may affect their investment and their ability to bear all of the risks of that investment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Legal investment considerations may restrict certain investments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The investment activities of certain investors are subject to\par investment laws and regulations, or review or regulation by applicable\par authorities. Potential investors should consult their legal advisors to\par determine whether and to what extent (1) the Bonds are legal investments for\par them, (2) the Bonds can be used as collateral for various types of borrowing\par and (3) other restrictions apply to their purchase or pledge of any Bonds.\par Financial institutions should consult their legal advisors or the appropriate\par regulators to determine the appropriate treatment of the Bonds under any\par applicable risk-based capital or similar rules.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The secondary market generally\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bonds may have no established trading market when issued, and one may never develop. If a market does develop, it may not be liquid. Therefore, investors may not be able to sell their Bonds easily or at prices that will provide them with a yield comparable to similar investments that have a developed secondary market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                     TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF THE 2015 BONDS\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following is the text of the terms and conditions of the 2015 Bonds which, subject to amendment, will be endorsed on each Bond Certificate (as defined below) and will be attached and (subject to the provisions thereof) apply to each Global Bond representing the 2015 Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The U.S.$2,000,000,000 3.625 per cent. Bonds due 2015 (the "Bonds")\par (which expression includes any further Bonds issued pursuant to Condition 13\par and forming a single series with the Bonds) of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the\par Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ") acting on behalf of the Russian\par Federation (the "Russian Federation") were authorised pursuant to the\par provisions of Federal Law No. 136-FZ of 29 July 1998 (as amended) "On the\par Specifics of Issuance and Circulation of State and Municipal Securities;"\par Federal Law No. 308-FZ of 2 December 2009 (as amended) "On the Federal\par Budget for 2010 and 2011-2012 (forecast);" Resolution of the Government of\par the Russian Federation dated 8 April 2010 No. 509-r; Resolution of the\par Government of the Russian Federation dated 8 April 2010 No. 217; Order of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 15 April 2010 No. 31n; and Order of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 23 April 2010 No. 244. The fiscal agency agreement dated 29\par April 2010 (the "Fiscal Agency Agreement") has been entered into in relation\par to the Bonds by the Russian Federation, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., London Branch, as\par fiscal and principal paying agent (the "Fiscal Agent") and as registrar (the\par "Registrar"), the other paying agents named therein (together with the Fiscal\par Agent and the Registrar, the "Paying Agents") and the transfer agents named\par therein (the "Transfer Agents").\par \par In these Conditions, "Fiscal Agent," "Registrar," "Paying Agent"\par and "Transfer Agent" shall include any successors appointed from time to time\par in accordance with the provisions of the Fiscal Agency Agreement for the\par Bonds, and any reference to an "Agent" or "Agents" shall mean any or all (as\par applicable) of such persons.\par \par Copies of the Fiscal Agency Agreement are available for inspection\par during usual business hours at the principal office of the Fiscal Agent\par (currently 14th Floor, Citigroup  Centre, Canada Square, London E14 5LB) and at\par the specified offices of each of the other Agents. The Bondholders (as defined\par in Condition 1(b)) are bound by, and are deemed to have notice of, the\par provisions of the Fiscal Agency Agreement for the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Form, Denomination and Title\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Form and Denomination\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bonds are in definitive fully registered form, without interest coupons attached, in the denomination of U.S. $100,000 (the "authorised denomination"). A certificate (each a "Bond Certificate") will be issued to each Bondholder in respect of its registered holding or holdings of Bonds. Each Bond Certificate will be numbered serially with an identifying number which will be recorded in the register (the "Register") which the Russian Federation shall procure to be kept by the Registrar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Title\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Title to the Bonds will pass by and upon registration in the\par Register. In these Conditions, "Bondholder" and "holder" mean the person in\par whose name a Bond is registered in the Register (or, in the case of joint\par holders, the first-named thereof). The holder of any Bond will (except as\par otherwise requested by such holder in writing, or as otherwise ordered by a\par court of competent jurisdiction or required by law) be treated as its absolute\par owner for all purposes, whether or not it is overdue and regardless of any\par notice of ownership, trust or any interest therein, any writing thereon by any\par person (other than a duly executed transfer thereof in the form endorsed\par thereon) or any notice of any previous theft or loss thereof, and no person\par will be liable for so treating the holder.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Transfer of Bonds and Issue of Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Transfer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Subject to Condition 2(d), a Bond may be transferred in whole or in\par part in the authorised denomination upon the surrender of the Bond Certificate\par representing that Bond, together with the form of transfer (including any\par certification as to compliance with restrictions on transfer included in such\par form of transfer) endorsed thereon (the "Transfer Form") duly completed and\par executed, at the specified office of the Registrar or any Transfer Agent,\par together with such evidence as the Registrar or, as the case may be, such\par Transfer Agent may reasonably require to prove the title of the transferor and\par the authority of the\par \par persons who have executed the Transfer Form. In the case of a\par transfer of part only of the Bonds represented by a Bond Certificate, neither\par the part transferred nor the balance not transferred may be less than the\par authorised denomination and a new Bond Certificate in respect of the balance\par not so transferred will be issued to the transferor.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Delivery\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each new Bond Certificate to be issued upon a transfer of any Bonds\par will, within five business days of the request for transfer being duly made,\par be delivered at the specified office of the Registrar or, as the case may be,\par any Transfer Agent or (at the request and the risk of such transferee) be\par mailed by uninsured post to such address as the transferee entitled to the\par Bonds represented by such Bond Certificate may have specified. In this\par Condition 2(b), "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or Sunday)\par on which commercial banks are open for business (including dealings in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) in the cities in which the Registrar and any such Transfer Agent\par have their respective specified offices.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c) No Charge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Registration or transfer of Bonds will be effected without charge\par to the holder or transferee thereof, but upon payment (or against such\par indemnity from the holder or the transferee thereof as the Registrar or the\par relevant Transfer Agent may reasonably require) in respect of any tax or other\par duty of whatever nature which may be levied or imposed in connection with such\par registration or transfer.\par \par (d) Closed Periods\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No Bondholder may require the transfer of a Bond to be registered during the period of 15 calendar days ending on the due date for any payment of principal in respect of such Bond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e) Regulations Concerning Transfer and Registration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par All transfers of Bonds and entries on the Register will be made\par subject to the detailed regulations concerning transfer of Bonds scheduled to\par the Fiscal Agency Agreement. The regulations may be changed by the Russian\par Federation in a manner which is reasonably required by the Russian Federation\par (after consultation with the Registrar) to reflect changes in legal\par requirements or in any other manner which is not prejudicial to the interests\par of the Bondholders. A copy of the current regulations will be sent by the\par Registrar to any Bondholder who so requests.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Status\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bonds constitute direct, unconditional, unsecured and\par unsubordinated obligations of the Russian Federation and the full faith and\par credit of the Russian Federation is pledged for the due and punctual payment\par of principal of, and interest on, the Bonds and for the performance of all\par other obligations of the Russian Federation pursuant to the Bonds. The Bonds\par shall at all times rank pari passu without any preference among themselves and\par pari passu in all respects with all other present and future unsecured and\par unsubordinated obligations of the Russian Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Negative Pledge and Covenant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Negative Pledge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as any of the Bonds remains outstanding (as defined in the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement) the Russian Federation will not create or permit to\par subsist any Lien upon the whole or any part of the International Monetary\par Assets to secure any Public External Indebtedness unless, at the same time or\par prior thereto, the obligations of the Russian Federation under the Bonds are\par secured equally and rateably therewith.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Covenant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as any Bond remains outstanding the Russian Federation\par shall obtain, and do or cause to be done all things necessary to ensure the\par continuance of, all consents, licences, approvals and authorisations, and make\par or cause to be made all registrations, recordings and filings, which may at\par any time be required to be obtained or made in the Russian Federation for the\par execution, delivery or performance of the Bonds or for the validity or\par enforceability thereof.\par \par (c) Definitions\par \par In these Conditions:\par \par "Excluded Indebtedness" means any obligation of the Russian\par Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   of the U.S.S.R. (now known as state corporation "Bank for\par Development and Foreign Economic Affairs (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  )") or any other\par person that may arise from time to time representing restructured indebtedness\par originally incurred prior to 1 January 1992 by the government of the former\par Soviet Union or any of its legally authorised entities, other than the Russian\par Federation U.S. Dollar Denominated Bonds due 2007 to 2030.\par \par "External Indebtedness" means Indebtedness (i) which is not\par Excluded Indebtedness, (ii) which is denominated or payable, or at the option\par of the relevant creditor or holder thereof may be payable, in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other\par than the lawful \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation and (iii) which was not\par originally incurred or assumed under an agreement or instrument made with or\par issued to creditors substantially all of whom were residents of the Russian\par Federation or entities having their head office or principal place of business\par within the territory of the Russian Federation. For the avoidance of doubt,\par External Indebtedness does not include Internal Government Hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Bonds\par known as "OVVZs," "Taiga" bonds or "MinFins" or any bonds representing\par restructured internal hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds of the Government of the Russian\par Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Government of the Russian Federation" means the Government of the Russian Federation as provided in Article 110 of the Constitution of the Russian Federation, or any successor article, from time to time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "IMF" means the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par "Indebtedness" means any legal obligation or any obligation\par intended by its terms to be a legal obligation (whether present or future,\par actual or contingent, secured or unsecured, incurred as principal, surety,\par guarantor or otherwise) for the payment or repayment of borrowed money created\par (or intended by its terms to have been created) under an agreement or\par instrument in which the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian\par Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is designated as the obligor, or which\par by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Russian law constitutes a legal obligation of the Russian\par Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (it being understood that neither the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian\par Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , nor any political subdivision, regional or municipal government,\par ministry (other than the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ), department, authority or\par statutory corporation of the Russian Federation nor any joint stock company,\par enterprise or other entity organised or existing under the laws or regulations\par of the Russian Federation or any of the above, is considered to be part of the\par Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for purposes hereof).\par \par "International Monetary Assets" means all official holdings of\par gold, Special Drawing Rights, Reserve Positions in the Fund and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign\par Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or the Government of the Russian\par Federation from time to time (but not, for the avoidance of doubt, any such\par assets of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  at any time), and the\par terms "Special Drawing Rights," "Reserve Positions in the Fund" and "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign\par Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " have, as to the types of assets included, the meanings given to them\par in the IMF's publication entitled "International Financial Statistics" or such\par other meanings as shall be formally adopted by the IMF from time to time.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Lien" means any lien, pledge, hypothecation, mortgage, security interest, deed of trust, charge or any other encumbrance arising under a security agreement or arrangement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par "Public External Indebtedness" means External Indebtedness which\par (i) is in the form of, or represented by, bonds, notes or other securities or\par any guarantee thereof and (ii) is, or may be, quoted, listed or ordinarily\par purchased and sold on any stock exchange, automated trading system or\par over-the-counter or other securities market (including, without prejudice to\par the generality of the foregoing, the market for securities eligible for resale\par pursuant to Rule 144A under the United States Securities Act of 1933, as\par amended (the "Securities Act")).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each Bond bears interest on its outstanding principal amount from\par 29 April 2010 at 3.625 per cent. per annum, payable semi-annually in arrear on\par 29 April and 29 October in each year until maturity, commencing on 29 October\par 2010 (each, an "Interest Payment Date"). Interest will be paid subject to and\par in accordance with the provisions of Condition 7. The period beginning on and\par including 29 April 2010 and\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ending on but excluding the first Interest Payment Date and each successive period beginning on and including an Interest Payment Date and ending on but excluding the next successive Interest Payment Date is called an "Interest Period."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each Bond will cease to bear interest from the due date for\par redemption unless, after surrender of such Bond, payment of principal is\par improperly withheld or refused, in which case the outstanding principal amount\par of such Bond will continue to bear interest at the rate specified above (after\par as well as before judgment) until whichever is the earlier of (a) the day on\par which all sums due in respect of such Bond up to that day are received by or\par on behalf of the relevant Bondholder and (b) the day which is seven days after\par notice has been given to the Bondholders that the Fiscal Agent has received\par all sums due in respect of the Bonds up to such seventh day (except, in the\par case of payment to the Fiscal Agent, to the extent that there is any\par subsequent default in payment in accordance with these Conditions).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If interest is required to be calculated for a period of less than a complete Interest Period, it will be calculated on the basis of a year of 360 days consisting of 12 months of 30 days each and, in the case of an incomplete month, the actual number of days elapsed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6 Redemption, Purchase and Cancellation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Redemption\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unless previously redeemed, or purchased and cancelled, each Bond will be redeemed at its principal amount on 29 April 2015 (the "Maturity Date") subject as provided in Condition 7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Purchase and Cancellation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation and its affiliates may at any time purchase\par Bonds in the open market or otherwise at any price. Any Bonds so purchased may\par be cancelled or held and resold (provided that such resale is outside the\par United States, as defined in Regulation S under the Securities Act). Any Bonds\par so cancelled will not be reissued.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7 Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Principal\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Payments of principal in respect of each Bond will be made by\par transfer to a U.S. dollar account maintained by the Bondholder with a bank in\par New York City or (i) if it does not have such a U.S. dollar account or (ii) if\par the principal amount of Bonds held by such person is less than U.S.$300,000,\par by a U.S. dollar cheque drawn on a bank in New York City mailed to the\par registered address of the Bondholder by uninsured mail at the risk of the\par Bondholder. Such payment will be made only upon presentation and surrender of\par the relevant Bond Certificate at the specified office of any of the Paying\par Agents and will be rounded downwards, if necessary, to the nearest cent. For\par the purposes of this Condition 7(a) the holder of such Bond will be deemed to\par be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of joint holders) on\par the Register at the Registrar's close of business on the fifteenth day before\par the due date for such payment of principal and the outstanding amount of each\par Bondholder's registered holding will be deemed to be the amount shown as such\par on the Register for such Bondholder at the same time on that date.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Payments of interest (other than interest due on redemption) in\par respect of each Bond will be made by U.S. dollar cheque drawn on a bank in New\par York City and mailed to the holder (or to the first-named of joint holders)\par of such Bond at the address appearing in the Register not later than the due\par date for such payment and will be rounded downwards, if necessary, to the\par nearest cent. For the purposes of this Condition 7(b) the holder of such Bond\par will be deemed to be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of\par joint holders) on the Register on the fifteenth day before the due date for\par such payment of interest.\par \par Upon application by a Bondholder with respect to Bonds having a\par principal amount of U.S.$300,000 or more to the specified office of the\par Registrar not less than 15 days before the due date for the payment of any\par interest (other than interest due on redemption) in respect of such Bonds,\par such payment will be made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account maintained by\par the payee with a bank in New York City. Any such application for transfer to a\par U.S. dollar account shall be deemed to relate to all future payments of\par interest (other than interest due on redemption) in respect of the Bonds which\par become payable to the Bondholder\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 who has made the initial application until such time as the Registrar is notified in writing to the contrary by such Bondholder.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payment of interest due on redemption will be made in the same manner as payment of the principal of a Bond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c) Payments Subject to Fiscal Laws\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds are subject in all cases to any applicable fiscal or other laws and regulations, but without prejudice to the provisions of Condition 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d) Commissions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No commissions or expenses shall be charged to the Bondholders in respect of any payments of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e) Payments on Business Days\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Where payment is to be made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account,\par payment instructions (for value the due date or, if that is not a business\par day, for value the first following day which is a business day) will be\par initiated on the due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal\par and interest due on redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond\par Certificate is presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the\par Paying Agents.\par \par Where payment is to be made by cheque, the cheque will be mailed on\par the due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal and interest\par due on redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond Certificate\par is presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the Paying\par Agents.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In these Conditions, "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or Sunday) on which commercial banks are open for business in New York City and, in the case of the surrender of a Bond Certificate, in the place where the Bond Certificate is surrendered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (f) Delay in Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Bondholders will not be entitled to any interest or other payment\par for any delay after the due date in receiving any amount due in respect of any\par Bond as a result of (i) the due date not being a business day, (ii) the\par Bondholder being late in presenting or surrendering its Bond Certificate (if\par required to do so) or (iii) a cheque mailed in accordance with this Condition\par 7 arriving after the due date for payment or being lost in the mail.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (g) Partial Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If at any time a partial payment is made in respect of any Bond, the Registrar shall endorse the Register with a statement indicating the amount and date of such payment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (h) Agents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The initial Agents and their initial specified offices are listed\par below. Any of the Agents may resign in accordance with the provisions of the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement and the Russian Federation reserves the right at any\par time to vary or terminate the appointment of any Agent and appoint additional\par or other Agents, provided that while the Bonds are outstanding it will\par maintain (i) a Fiscal Agent, (ii) a Registrar and (iii) a Paying Agent and a\par Transfer Agent having a specified office in a major European city which will\par be in London, so long as the Bonds are listed on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   and\par the rules of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   so require. Notice of any change in the\par Agents or their specified offices will promptly be given to the Bondholders in\par accordance with Condition 14.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8 Taxation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds by\par the Russian Federation shall be made free and clear of, and without\par withholding or deduction for, any taxes, duties, assessments or governmental\par charges of whatever nature imposed, levied, collected, withheld or assessed by\par the Russian Federation or any political subdivision or any authority thereof\par or therein having power to tax (together, "Taxes"), unless such withholding or\par deduction is required by law. If at any time any Taxes are withheld or\par deducted, the Russian Federation shall increase the payment of principal or\par interest, as the case may be, to\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 such amount as will result in the receipt by the Bondholders of such amounts as would have been received by them had no such withholding or deduction been required, except that no such increased amount shall be payable:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, if such holder is liable to such Taxes by reason of having some connection with the Russian Federation other than the mere holding of a Bond or the receipt of payments thereunder;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (b) if the Bond Certificate representing a Bond is presented and\par surrendered for payment more than 30 days after the Relevant Date, except to\par the extent that the holder would have been entitled to such additional amounts\par on presentation and surrender of such Bond Certificate for payment on the last\par day of such period of 30 days;\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c) where such withholding or deduction is imposed on a payment to an individual and is required to be made pursuant to European Council Directive 2003/48/EC or any other Directive implementing the conclusions of the ECOFIN Council meeting of 26-27 November 2000 on the taxation of savings income or any law implementing or complying with, or introduced in order to conform to, such Directive; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, who would have been able to avoid such withholding or deduction by presenting the relevant Bond Certificate representing a Bond to another Paying Agent in a Member State of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If, in relation to any payment under the Bonds (a "Payment") any\par Bondholder has received any increased amount as provided in this Condition 8,\par the Russian Federation shall have the right to retain any refund of any amount\par withheld or deducted in respect of such Payment, which refund may be available\par under a tax treaty or otherwise to such Bondholder. Notwithstanding the\par foregoing, no Bondholder makes any representation or warranty that the Russian\par Federation will be entitled to any such refund (or to make any claim in\par respect thereof) and no Bondholder shall incur any obligation with respect\par thereto (including, without limitation, incurring any expense or liability in\par connection therewith).\par \par For the avoidance of doubt, the Russian Federation acknowledges\par that it shall have no right to make any deduction or withholding in respect of\par (nor will it purport to set off any amount equal to) any such refund from any\par payment in respect of any Bond.\par \par In these Conditions, "Relevant Date" means whichever is the later\par of (i) the date on which the payment in question first becomes due and (ii) if\par the full amount payable has not been received in New York City by the Fiscal\par Agent on or prior to such due date, the date on which (the full amount having\par been so received) notice to that effect has been given to the Bondholders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any reference in these Conditions to principal or interest in respect of the Bonds shall be deemed to include any increased amounts which may be payable under this Condition 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9 Events of Default\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If any of the following occurs and is continuing (each an "Event of Default") in respect of the Bonds, as applicable:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Non-payment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation fails to pay any amount of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds, as the case may be, when due and such failure continues for a period of 30 calendar days; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Breach of other obligations or undertakings\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation defaults in performance or observance of or\par compliance with any of its other obligations or undertakings in respect of the\par Bonds, as the case may be, which default (if capable of remedy) is not\par remedied within 60 days after written notice of such default shall have been\par given to the Russian Federation by any holder of the Bonds, as the case may\par be, it being understood that a default in respect of the undertaking set forth\par in Condition 4(a) shall be deemed capable of remedy for purposes hereof; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c) Cross-acceleration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Any other Public External Indebtedness shall become due and payable\par prior to the stated maturity thereof otherwise than at the option of the\par debtor following a default or the Russian Federation, the Government of the\par Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall fail to make the final\par payment of principal in respect of any Public External Indebtedness on the\par date on which such final payment is due and payable or at the expiration of\par any grace period originally applicable thereto or, in the case of any\par guarantee which constitutes Public External Indebtedness, the underlying\par obligation in respect of which such guarantee has been given shall have become\par due and payable prior to the stated maturity thereof otherwise than at the\par option of the debtor following a default or the debtor shall have failed to\par make the final payment of principal in respect of such underlying obligation\par on the date on which such final payment is due and payable or at the\par expiration of any grace period originally applicable thereto and the guarantee\par shall not be honoured when due and called upon; provided that the aggregate\par amount of the relevant Public External Indebtedness in respect of which one or\par more of the events mentioned in this Condition 9(c) shall have occurred equals\par or exceeds U.S.$75,000,000 (or its equivalent in any other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ); and provided, further, that any secured Public External\par Indebtedness that by its terms is fully non-recourse to the Russian\par Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall not be counted as Public External Indebtedness for purposes of\par this Condition 9(c); or\par \par (d) Moratorium\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A moratorium on the payment of principal of, or interest on, all or any part of Public External Indebtedness; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e) Unlawfulness or Invalidity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The validity of the Bonds is contested by the Russian Federation or\par any agency or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation or the Russian\par Federation or any agency or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation\par shall deny any of the Russian Federation's obligations under the Bonds or it\par is or will become unlawful for the Russian Federation to perform or comply\par with any of its obligations under or in respect of the Bonds or under or in\par respect of the Fiscal Agency Agreement or any of such obligations shall be or\par become unenforceable or invalid; or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (f) Consents etc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Any regulation, decree, consent, approval, licence or other\par authority necessary to enable the Russian Federation to enter into or perform\par its obligations under the Bonds or under the Fiscal Agency Agreement or for\par the validity or enforceability thereof shall expire or be withheld, revoked or\par terminated or otherwise be void or cease to remain in full force and effect or\par shall be modified in a manner which adversely affects any rights or claims of\par any of the holders of the Bonds, as the case may be,\par \par then holders of 25 per cent. or more in aggregate outstanding\par principal amount of the Bonds may declare the Bonds to be immediately due and\par payable whereupon the Bonds shall become immediately due and payable at their\par principal amount, together with accrued interest, without any further\par formality.\par \par If the Russian Federation receives notice in writing from holders\par of at least 50 per cent. in aggregate principal amount of the outstanding\par Bonds to the effect that the Event of Default or Events of Default giving rise\par to any above mentioned declaration of acceleration is or are cured following\par any such declaration and that such holders wish the relevant declaration to be\par withdrawn, the Russian Federation shall give notice thereof to the Bondholders\par (with a copy to the Fiscal Agent), whereupon the relevant declaration shall be\par withdrawn and shall have no further effect, but without prejudice to any\par rights or obligations which may have arisen before the Russian Federation\par gives such notice (whether pursuant to these Conditions or otherwise). No such\par withdrawal shall affect any other or any subsequent Event of Default or any\par right of any Bondholder in relation thereto.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Prescription\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Claims against the Russian Federation in respect of principal and interest shall become void unless made within a period of 10 years, in the case of principal, and five years, in the case of interest, from the appropriate Relevant Date (as defined in Condition 8).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11 Replacement of Bond Certificates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If any Bond Certificate is lost, stolen, mutilated, defaced or\par destroyed, it may be replaced at the specified office of the Registrar or the\par Transfer Agent with its specified office in London subject to all applicable\par laws and stock exchange requirements, upon payment by the claimant of the\par expenses incurred in connection with such replacement and on such terms as to\par evidence, security, indemnity and otherwise as the Russian Federation may\par reasonably require. Mutilated or defaced Bond Certificates must be surrendered\par before replacements will be issued.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions; Modification and Waiver\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions for convening\par meetings of Bondholders to consider any matter affecting their interests,\par including the modification by Extraordinary Resolution of these Conditions.\par The quorum at any such meeting for passing an Extraordinary Resolution shall\par (subject as provided in the Fiscal Agency Agreement in the event that all\par outstanding Bonds are held by one person) be two or more persons holding or\par representing a clear majority of the aggregate principal amount of the\par outstanding Bonds, or at any meeting that is adjourned two or more persons\par being or representing Bondholders (whatever the principal amount of the\par outstanding Bonds so held or represented), and the majority for passing an\par Extraordinary Resolution at any such meeting shall be at least 662/3 per cent.\par of the votes cast except that at any meeting the business of which includes\par consideration of proposals on Reserved Matters, including, inter alia, (i) to\par modify the maturity of the Bonds, (ii) to reduce or cancel the principal\par amount of, or interest on, the Bonds, (iii) to change the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of payment\par of the Bonds, (iv) to modify the provisions concerning the quorum required at\par any meeting of Bondholders or the majority required to pass an Extraordinary\par Resolution or (v) to modify the percentage required to pass any resolution,\par the necessary quorum for passing an Extraordinary Resolution shall (subject as\par provided in the Fiscal Agency Agreement in the event that all outstanding\par Bonds are held by one person) be two or more persons holding or representing\par at least 75 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the outstanding\par Bonds, or at any meeting that is adjourned at least 25 per cent. of the\par aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, and the majority for\par passing an Extraordinary Resolution on a Reserved Matter at any such meeting\par shall be at least 75 per cent. of the votes cast. An Extraordinary Resolution\par duly passed at any meeting of Bondholders will be binding on all Bondholders\par whether or not they are present at the meeting.\par \par In addition, the Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions\par relating to Written Resolutions. A "Written Resolution" means a resolution in\par writing signed by or on behalf of holders of at least 75 per cent. of the\par aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, in the case of a Reserved\par Matter, or 662/3 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the\par outstanding Bonds, in the case of a matter other than a Reserved Matter. Any\par Written Resolution may be contained in one document or several documents in\par like form, each signed by or on behalf of one or more Bondholders. Any Written\par Resolution shall be binding on all of the Bondholders, whether or not signed\par by them.\par \par (b) Modification and waiver\par \par The parties to the Fiscal Agency Agreement may agree, without the\par consent of the Bondholders, to any modification of any provision of the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement or the Bonds which is of a formal, minor or technical nature\par or is made to correct a manifest error.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 Further Issues\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation shall be at liberty from time to time,\par without the consent of the Bondholders, to create and issue further Bonds\par ranking equally in all respects (or in all respects save for payments made\par prior to the issuance of such further Bonds and, if applicable, the date and\par amount of the first payment on such further Bonds) so that the same shall be\par consolidated and form a single series with the Bonds, provided that such\par further issuance constitutes a qualified reopening for U.S. federal income\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 tax\par purposes.\par \par 14 Notices\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notices to the Bondholders will be sent to them by mail (airmail if overseas) at their respective addresses on the Register, and will be deemed to have been given on the fourth day after the date of mailing. So long as the Bonds are listed on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the rules of the London Stock\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Exchange so require, notices with respect to the Bonds will also be\par published in a leading newspaper having general circulation in the United\par Kingdom (which is expected to be The Financial Times) or, if such publication\par is not practicable, in an English language newspaper having general\par circulation in Europe, and each such notice shall be deemed to have been given\par on the date of such publication, or if published more than once or on\par different dates, on the first date on which publication is made.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Indemnity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The U.S. dollar is the sole \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of account and payment for all\par sums payable by the Russian Federation under or in connection with the Bonds,\par including damages. Any amount received or recovered in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than\par the U.S. dollar (whether as a result of, or the enforcement of, a judgement or\par order of a court of any jurisdiction or otherwise) by any Bondholder in\par respect of any sum expressed to be due to it from the Russian Federation shall\par only constitute a discharge to the Russian Federation to the extent of the\par U.S. dollar amount which the recipient is able to purchase with the amount so\par received or recovered in that other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the date of that receipt or\par recovery (or, if it is not practicable to make that purchase on that date, on\par the first date on which it is practicable to do so). If that U.S. dollar\par amount is less than the U.S. dollar amount expressed to be due to the\par recipient under any Bond, the Russian Federation shall indemnify such\par recipient against any loss sustained by it as a result. In any event, the\par Russian Federation shall indemnify the recipient against the cost of making\par any such purchase. These indemnities constitute separate and independent\par obligations from the Russian Federation's other obligations, shall give rise\par to a separate and independent cause of action, shall apply irrespective of any\par indulgence granted by any Bondholder and shall continue in full force and\par effect despite any judgement, order, claim or proof for a liquidated amount in\par respect of any sum due under any Bond or any judgement or order.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No person shall have any right to enforce any term or condition of the Bonds under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17 Governing Law\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bonds and the Fiscal Agency Agreement and any non-contractual obligations arising out of or in connection with them shall be governed by and construed in accordance with the laws of England.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                     TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF THE 2020 BONDS\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following is the text of the terms and conditions of the 2020 Bonds which, subject to amendment, will be endorsed on each Bond Certificate (as defined below) and will be attached and (subject to the provisions thereof) apply to each Global Bond respresenting the 2020 Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The U.S.$3,500,000,000 5 per cent. Bonds due 2020 (the "Bonds`")\par (which expression includes any further Bonds issued pursuant to Condition 13\par and forming a single series with the Bonds) of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the\par Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ") acting on behalf of the Russian\par Federation (the "Russian Federation") were authorised pursuant to the\par provisions of Federal Law No. 136-FZ of 29 July 1998 (as amended) "On the\par Specifics of Issuance and Circulation of State and Municipal Securities;"\par Federal Law No. 308-FZ of 2 December 2009 (as amended) "On the Federal\par Budget for 2010 and 2011-2012 (forecast);" Resolution of the Government of\par the Russian Federation dated 8 April 2010 No. 509-r; Resolution of the\par Government of the Russian Federation dated 8 April 2010 No. 217; Order of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 15 April 2010 No. 31n; and Order of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 23 April 2010 No. 243. The fiscal agency agreement dated 29\par April 2010 (the "Fiscal Agency Agreement") has been entered into in relation\par to the Bonds by the Russian Federation, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., London Branch, as\par fiscal and principal paying agent (the "Fiscal Agent") and as registrar (the\par "Registrar"), the other paying agents named therein (together with the Fiscal\par Agent and the Registrar, the "Paying Agents") and the transfer agents named\par therein (the "Transfer Agents").\par \par In these Conditions, "Fiscal Agent," "Registrar," "Paying Agent"\par and "Transfer Agent" shall include any successors appointed from time to time\par in accordance with the provisions of the Fiscal Agency Agreement for the\par Bonds, and any reference to an "Agent" or "Agents" shall mean any or all (as\par applicable) of such persons.\par \par Copies of the Fiscal Agency Agreement are available for inspection\par during usual business hours at the principal office of the Fiscal Agent\par (currently 14th Floor, Citigroup  Centre, Canada Square, London E14 5LB) and at\par the specified offices of each of the other Agents. The Bondholders (as defined\par in Condition 1(b)) are bound by, and are deemed to have notice of, the\par provisions of the Fiscal Agency Agreement for the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Form, Denomination and Title\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Form and Denomination\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bonds are in definitive fully registered form, without interest coupons attached, in the denomination of U.S. $100,000 (the "authorised denomination"). A certificate (each a "Bond Certificate") will be issued to each Bondholder in respect of its registered holding or holdings of Bonds. Each Bond Certificate will be numbered serially with an identifying number which will be recorded in the register (the "Register") which the Russian Federation shall procure to be kept by the Registrar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Title\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Title to the Bonds will pass by and upon registration in the\par Register. In these Conditions, "Bondholder" and "holder" mean the person in\par whose name a Bond is registered in the Register (or, in the case of joint\par holders, the first-named thereof). The holder of any Bond will (except as\par otherwise requested by such holder in writing, or as otherwise ordered by a\par court of competent jurisdiction or required by law) be treated as its absolute\par owner for all purposes, whether or not it is overdue and regardless of any\par notice of ownership, trust or any interest therein, any writing thereon by any\par person (other than a duly executed transfer thereof in the form endorsed\par thereon) or any notice of any previous theft or loss thereof, and no person\par will be liable for so treating the holder.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Transfer of Bonds and Issue of Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Transfer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Subject to Condition 2(d), a Bond may be transferred in whole or in\par part in the authorised denomination upon the surrender of the Bond Certificate\par representing that Bond, together with the form of transfer (including any\par certification as to compliance with restrictions on transfer included in such\par form of transfer) endorsed thereon (the "Transfer Form") duly completed and\par executed, at the specified office of the Registrar or any Transfer Agent,\par together with such evidence as the Registrar or, as the case may be, such\par Transfer Agent may reasonably require to prove the title of the transferor and\par the authority of the\par \par persons who have executed the Transfer Form. In the case of a\par transfer of part only of the Bonds represented by a Bond Certificate, neither\par the part transferred nor the balance not transferred may be less than the\par authorised denomination and a new Bond Certificate in respect of the balance\par not so transferred will be issued to the transferor.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Delivery\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each new Bond Certificate to be issued upon a transfer of any Bonds\par will, within five business days of the request for transfer being duly made,\par be delivered at the specified office of the Registrar or, as the case may be,\par any Transfer Agent or (at the request and the risk of such transferee) be\par mailed by uninsured post to such address as the transferee entitled to the\par Bonds represented by such Bond Certificate may have specified. In this\par Condition 2(b), "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or Sunday)\par on which commercial banks are open for business (including dealings in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) in the cities in which the Registrar and any such Transfer Agent\par have their respective specified offices.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c) No Charge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Registration or transfer of Bonds will be effected without charge\par to the holder or transferee thereof, but upon payment (or against such\par indemnity from the holder or the transferee thereof as the Registrar or the\par relevant Transfer Agent may reasonably require) in respect of any tax or other\par duty of whatever nature which may be levied or imposed in connection with such\par registration or transfer.\par \par (d) Closed Periods\par No Bondholder may require the transfer of a Bond to be registered\par during the period of 15 calendar days ending on the due date for any payment\par of principal in respect of such Bond.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e) Regulations Concerning Transfer and Registration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par All transfers of Bonds and entries on the Register will be made\par subject to the detailed regulations concerning transfer of Bonds scheduled to\par the Fiscal Agency Agreement. The regulations may be changed by the Russian\par Federation in a manner which is reasonably required by the Russian Federation\par (after consultation with the Registrar) to reflect changes in legal\par requirements or in any other manner which is not prejudicial to the interests\par of the Bondholders. A copy of the current regulations will be sent by the\par Registrar to any Bondholder who so requests.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Status\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bonds constitute direct, unconditional, unsecured and\par unsubordinated obligations of the Russian Federation and the full faith and\par credit of the Russian Federation is pledged for the due and punctual payment\par of principal of, and interest on, the Bonds and for the performance of all\par other obligations of the Russian Federation pursuant to the Bonds. The Bonds\par shall at all times rank pari passu without any preference among themselves and\par pari passu in all respects with all other present and future unsecured and\par unsubordinated obligations of the Russian Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Negative Pledge and Covenant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Negative Pledge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as any of the Bonds remains outstanding (as defined in the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement) the Russian Federation will not create or permit to\par subsist any Lien upon the whole or any part of the International Monetary\par Assets to secure any Public External Indebtedness unless, at the same time or\par prior thereto, the obligations of the Russian Federation under the Bonds are\par secured equally and rateably therewith.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Covenant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as any Bond remains outstanding the Russian Federation\par shall obtain, and do or cause to be done all things necessary to ensure the\par continuance of, all consents, licences, approvals and authorisations, and make\par or cause to be made all registrations, recordings and filings, which may at\par any time be required to be obtained or made in the Russian Federation for the\par execution, delivery or performance of the Bonds or for the validity or\par enforceability thereof.\par \par (c) Definitions\par \par In these Conditions:\par \par "Excluded Indebtedness" means any obligation of the Russian\par Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   of the U.S.S.R. (now known as state corporation "Bank for\par Development and Foreign Economic Affairs (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  )") or any other\par person that may arise from time to time representing restructured indebtedness\par originally incurred prior to 1 January 1992 by the government of the former\par Soviet Union or any of its legally authorised entities, other than the Russian\par Federation U.S. Dollar Denominated Bonds due 2007 to 2030.\par \par "External Indebtedness" means Indebtedness (i) which is not\par Excluded Indebtedness, (ii) which is denominated or payable, or at the option\par of the relevant creditor or holder thereof may be payable, in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other\par than the lawful \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation and (iii) which was not\par originally incurred or assumed under an agreement or instrument made with or\par issued to creditors substantially all of whom were residents of the Russian\par Federation or entities having their head office or principal place of business\par within the territory of the Russian Federation. For the avoidance of doubt,\par External Indebtedness does not include Internal Government Hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Bonds\par known as "OVVZs," "Taiga" bonds or "MinFins" or any bonds representing\par restructured internal hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds of the Government of the Russian\par Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Government of the Russian Federation" means the Government of the Russian Federation as provided in Article 110 of the Constitution of the Russian Federation, or any successor article, from time to time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "IMF" means the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par "Indebtedness" means any legal obligation or any obligation\par intended by its terms to be a legal obligation (whether present or future,\par actual or contingent, secured or unsecured, incurred as principal, surety,\par guarantor or otherwise) for the payment or repayment of borrowed money created\par (or intended by its terms to have been created) under an agreement or\par instrument in which the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian\par Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is designated as the obligor, or which\par by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Russian law constitutes a legal obligation of the Russian\par Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (it being understood that neither the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian\par Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , nor any political subdivision, regional or municipal government,\par ministry (other than the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ), department, authority or\par statutory corporation of the Russian Federation nor any joint stock company,\par enterprise or other entity organised or existing under the laws or regulations\par of the Russian Federation or any of the above, is considered to be part of the\par Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for purposes hereof).\par \par "International Monetary Assets" means all official holdings of\par gold, Special Drawing Rights, Reserve Positions in the Fund and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign\par Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or the Government of the Russian\par Federation from time to time (but not, for the avoidance of doubt, any such\par assets of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  at any time), and the\par terms "Special Drawing Rights," "Reserve Positions in the Fund" and "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign\par Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " have, as to the types of assets included, the meanings given to them\par in the IMF's publication entitled "International Financial Statistics" or such\par other meanings as shall be formally adopted by the IMF from time to time.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Lien" means any lien, pledge, hypothecation, mortgage, security interest, deed of trust, charge or any other encumbrance arising under a security agreement or arrangement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par "Public External Indebtedness" means External Indebtedness which\par (i) is in the form of, or represented by, bonds, notes or other securities or\par any guarantee thereof and (ii) is, or may be, quoted, listed or ordinarily\par purchased and sold on any stock exchange, automated trading system or\par over-the-counter or other securities market (including, without prejudice to\par the generality of the foregoing, the market for securities eligible for resale\par pursuant to Rule 144A under the United States Securities Act of 1933, as\par amended (the "Securities Act")).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each Bond bears interest on its outstanding principal amount from\par 29 April 2010 at 5.00 per cent. per annum, payable semi-annually in arrear on\par 29 April and 29 October in each year until maturity, commencing on 29 October\par 2010 (each, an "Interest Payment Date"). Interest will be paid subject to and\par in accordance with the provisions of Condition 7. The period beginning on and\par including 29 April 2010 and\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ending on but excluding the first Interest Payment Date and each successive period beginning on and including an Interest Payment Date and ending on but excluding the next successive Interest Payment Date is called an "Interest Period."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each Bond will cease to bear interest from the due date for\par redemption unless, after surrender of such Bond, payment of principal is\par improperly withheld or refused, in which case the outstanding principal amount\par of such Bond will continue to bear interest at the rate specified above (after\par as well as before judgment) until whichever is the earlier of (a) the day on\par which all sums due in respect of such Bond up to that day are received by or\par on behalf of the relevant Bondholder and (b) the day which is seven days after\par notice has been given to the Bondholders that the Fiscal Agent has received\par all sums due in respect of the Bonds up to such seventh day (except, in the\par case of payment to the Fiscal Agent, to the extent that there is any\par subsequent default in payment in accordance with these Conditions).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If interest is required to be calculated for a period of less than a complete Interest Period, it will be calculated on the basis of a year of 360 days consisting of 12 months of 30 days each and, in the case of an incomplete month, the actual number of days elapsed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6 Redemption, Purchase and Cancellation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Redemption\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unless previously redeemed, or purchased and cancelled, each Bond will be redeemed at its principal amount on 29 April 2020 (the "Maturity Date") subject as provided in Condition 7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Purchase and Cancellation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation and its affiliates may at any time purchase\par Bonds in the open market or otherwise at any price. Any Bonds so purchased may\par be cancelled or held and resold (provided that such resale is outside the\par United States, as defined in Regulation S under the Securities Act). Any Bonds\par so cancelled will not be reissued.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7 Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Principal\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Payments of principal in respect of each Bond will be made by\par transfer to a U.S. dollar account maintained by the Bondholder with a bank in\par New York City or (i) if it does not have such a U.S. dollar account or (ii) if\par the principal amount of Bonds held by such person is less than U.S.$300,000,\par by a U.S. dollar cheque drawn on a bank in New York City mailed to the\par registered address of the Bondholder by uninsured mail at the risk of the\par Bondholder. Such payment will be made only upon presentation and surrender of\par the relevant Bond Certificate at the specified office of any of the Paying\par Agents and will be rounded downwards, if necessary, to the nearest cent. For\par the purposes of this Condition 7(a) the holder of such Bond will be deemed to\par be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of joint holders) on\par the Register at the Registrar's close of business on the fifteenth day before\par the due date for such payment of principal and the outstanding amount of each\par Bondholder's registered holding will be deemed to be the amount shown as such\par on the Register for such Bondholder at the same time on that date.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Payments of interest (other than interest due on redemption) in\par respect of each Bond will be made by U.S. dollar cheque drawn on a bank in New\par York City and mailed to the holder (or to the first-named of joint holders)\par of such Bond at the address appearing in the Register not later than the due\par date for such payment and will be rounded downwards, if necessary, to the\par nearest cent. For the purposes of this Condition 7(b) the holder of such Bond\par will be deemed to be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of\par joint holders) on the Register on the fifteenth day before the due date for\par such payment of interest.\par \par Upon application by a Bondholder with respect to Bonds having a\par principal amount of U.S.$300,000 or more to the specified office of the\par Registrar not less than 15 days before the due date for the payment of any\par interest (other than interest due on redemption) in respect of such Bonds,\par such payment will be made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account maintained by\par the payee with a bank in New York City. Any such application for transfer to a\par U.S. dollar account shall be deemed to relate to all future payments of\par interest (other than interest due on redemption) in respect of the Bonds which\par become payable to the Bondholder\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 who has made the initial application until such time as the Registrar is notified in writing to the contrary by such Bondholder.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payment of interest due on redemption will be made in the same manner as payment of the principal of a Bond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c) Payments Subject to Fiscal Laws\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds are subject in all cases to any applicable fiscal or other laws and regulations, but without prejudice to the provisions of Condition 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d) Commissions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No commissions or expenses shall be charged to the Bondholders in respect of any payments of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e) Payments on Business Days\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Where payment is to be made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account,\par payment instructions (for value the due date or, if that is not a business\par day, for value the first following day which is a business day) will be\par initiated on the due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal\par and interest due on redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond\par Certificate is presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the\par Paying Agents.\par \par Where payment is to be made by cheque, the cheque will be mailed on\par the due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal and interest\par due on redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond Certificate\par is presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the Paying\par Agents.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In these Conditions, "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or Sunday) on which commercial banks are open for business in New York City and, in the case of the surrender of a Bond Certificate, in the place where the Bond Certificate is surrendered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (f) Delay in Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Bondholders will not be entitled to any interest or other payment\par for any delay after the due date in receiving any amount due in respect of any\par Bond as a result of (i) the due date not being a business day, (ii) the\par Bondholder being late in presenting or surrendering its Bond Certificate (if\par required to do so) or (iii) a cheque mailed in accordance with this Condition\par 7 arriving after the due date for payment or being lost in the mail.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (g) Partial Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If at any time a partial payment is made in respect of any Bond, the Registrar shall endorse the Register with a statement indicating the amount and date of such payment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (h) Agents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The initial Agents and their initial specified offices are listed\par below. Any of the Agents may resign in accordance with the provisions of the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement and the Russian Federation reserves the right at any\par time to vary or terminate the appointment of any Agent and appoint additional\par or other Agents, provided that while the Bonds are outstanding it will\par maintain (i) a Fiscal Agent, (ii) a Registrar and (iii) a Paying Agent and a\par Transfer Agent having a specified office in a major European city which will\par be in London, so long as the Bonds are listed on the London Stock Exchange  and\par the rules of the London Stock Exchange  so require. Notice of any change in the\par Agents or their specified offices will promptly be given to the Bondholders in\par accordance with Condition 14.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8 Taxation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds by\par the Russian Federation shall be made free and clear of, and without\par withholding or deduction for, any taxes, duties, assessments or governmental\par charges of whatever nature imposed, levied, collected, withheld or assessed by\par the Russian Federation or any political subdivision or any authority thereof\par or therein having power to tax (together, "Taxes"), unless such withholding or\par deduction is required by law. If at any time any Taxes are withheld or\par deducted, the Russian Federation shall increase the payment of principal or\par interest, as the case may be, to\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 such amount as will result in the receipt by the Bondholders of such amounts as would have been received by them had no such withholding or deduction been required, except that no such increased amount shall be payable:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, if such holder is liable to such Taxes by reason of having some connection with the Russian Federation other than the mere holding of a Bond or the receipt of payments thereunder;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (b) if the Bond Certificate representing a Bond is presented and\par surrendered for payment more than 30 days after the Relevant Date, except to\par the extent that the holder would have been entitled to such additional amounts\par on presentation and surrender of such Bond Certificate for payment on the last\par day of such period of 30 days;\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c) where such withholding or deduction is imposed on a payment to an individual and is required to be made pursuant to European Council Directive 2003/48/EC or any other Directive implementing the conclusions of the ECOFIN Council meeting of 26-27 November 2000 on the taxation of savings income or any law implementing or complying with, or introduced in order to conform to, such Directive; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, who would have been able to avoid such withholding or deduction by presenting the relevant Bond Certificate representing a Bond to another Paying Agent in a Member State of the European Union.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If, in relation to any payment under the Bonds (a "Payment") any\par Bondholder has received any increased amount as provided in this Condition 8,\par the Russian Federation shall have the right to retain any refund of any amount\par withheld or deducted in respect of such Payment, which refund may be available\par under a tax treaty or otherwise to such Bondholder. Notwithstanding the\par foregoing, no Bondholder makes any representation or warranty that the Russian\par Federation will be entitled to any such refund (or to make any claim in\par respect thereof) and no Bondholder shall incur any obligation with respect\par thereto (including, without limitation, incurring any expense or liability in\par connection therewith).\par \par For the avoidance of doubt, the Russian Federation acknowledges\par that it shall have no right to make any deduction or withholding in respect of\par (nor will it purport to set off any amount equal to) any such refund from any\par payment in respect of any Bond.\par \par In these Conditions, "Relevant Date" means whichever is the later\par of (i) the date on which the payment in question first becomes due and (ii) if\par the full amount payable has not been received in New York City by the Fiscal\par Agent on or prior to such due date, the date on which (the full amount having\par been so received) notice to that effect has been given to the Bondholders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any reference in these Conditions to principal or interest in respect of the Bonds shall be deemed to include any increased amounts which may be payable under this Condition 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9 Events of Default\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If any of the following occurs and is continuing (each an "Event of Default") in respect of the Bonds, as applicable:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Non-payment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation fails to pay any amount of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds, as the case may be, when due and such failure continues for a period of 30 calendar days; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Breach of other obligations or undertakings\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation defaults in performance or observance of or\par compliance with any of its other obligations or undertakings in respect of the\par Bonds, as the case may be, which default (if capable of remedy) is not\par remedied within 60 days after written notice of such default shall have been\par given to the Russian Federation by any holder of the Bonds, as the case may\par be, it being understood that a default in respect of the undertaking set forth\par in Condition 4(a) shall be deemed capable of remedy for purposes hereof; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c) Cross-acceleration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Any other Public External Indebtedness shall become due and payable\par prior to the stated maturity thereof otherwise than at the option of the\par debtor following a default or the Russian Federation, the Government of the\par Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall fail to make the final\par payment of principal in respect of any Public External Indebtedness on the\par date on which such final payment is due and payable or at the expiration of\par any grace period originally applicable thereto or, in the case of any\par guarantee which constitutes Public External Indebtedness, the underlying\par obligation in respect of which such guarantee has been given shall have become\par due and payable prior to the stated maturity thereof otherwise than at the\par option of the debtor following a default or the debtor shall have failed to\par make the final payment of principal in respect of such underlying obligation\par on the date on which such final payment is due and payable or at the\par expiration of any grace period originally applicable thereto and the guarantee\par shall not be honoured when due and called upon; provided that the aggregate\par amount of the relevant Public External Indebtedness in respect of which one or\par more of the events mentioned in this Condition 9(c) shall have occurred equals\par or exceeds U.S.$75,000,000 (or its equivalent in any other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ); and provided, further, that any secured Public External\par Indebtedness that by its terms is fully non-recourse to the Russian\par Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall not be counted as Public External Indebtedness for purposes of\par this Condition 9(c); or\par \par (d) Moratorium\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A moratorium on the payment of principal of, or interest on, all or any part of Public External Indebtedness; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e) Unlawfulness or Invalidity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The validity of the Bonds is contested by the Russian Federation or\par any agency or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation or the Russian\par Federation or any agency or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation\par shall deny any of the Russian Federation's obligations under the Bonds or it\par is or will become unlawful for the Russian Federation to perform or comply\par with any of its obligations under or in respect of the Bonds or under or in\par respect of the Fiscal Agency Agreement or any of such obligations shall be or\par become unenforceable or invalid; or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (f) Consents etc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Any regulation, decree, consent, approval, licence or other\par authority necessary to enable the Russian Federation to enter into or perform\par its obligations under the Bonds or under the Fiscal Agency Agreement or for\par the validity or enforceability thereof shall expire or be withheld, revoked or\par terminated or otherwise be void or cease to remain in full force and effect or\par shall be modified in a manner which adversely affects any rights or claims of\par any of the holders of the Bonds, as the case may be,\par \par then holders of 25 per cent. or more in aggregate outstanding\par principal amount of the Bonds may declare the Bonds to be immediately due and\par payable whereupon the Bonds shall become immediately due and payable at their\par principal amount, together with accrued interest, without any further\par formality.\par \par If the Russian Federation receives notice in writing from holders\par of at least 50 per cent. in aggregate principal amount of the outstanding\par Bonds to the effect that the Event of Default or Events of Default giving rise\par to any above mentioned declaration of acceleration is or are cured following\par any such declaration and that such holders wish the relevant declaration to be\par withdrawn, the Russian Federation shall give notice thereof to the Bondholders\par (with a copy to the Fiscal Agent), whereupon the relevant declaration shall be\par withdrawn and shall have no further effect, but without prejudice to any\par rights or obligations which may have arisen before the Russian Federation\par gives such notice (whether pursuant to these Conditions or otherwise). No such\par withdrawal shall affect any other or any subsequent Event of Default or any\par right of any Bondholder in relation thereto.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Prescription\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Claims against the Russian Federation in respect of principal and interest shall become void unless made within a period of 10 years, in the case of principal, and five years, in the case of interest, from the appropriate Relevant Date (as defined in Condition 8).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11 Replacement of Bond Certificates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If any Bond Certificate is lost, stolen, mutilated, defaced or\par destroyed, it may be replaced at the specified office of the Registrar or the\par Transfer Agent with its specified office in London subject to all applicable\par laws and stock exchange requirements, upon payment by the claimant of the\par expenses incurred in connection with such replacement and on such terms as to\par evidence, security, indemnity and otherwise as the Russian Federation may\par reasonably require. Mutilated or defaced Bond Certificates must be surrendered\par before replacements will be issued.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions; Modification and Waiver\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions for convening\par meetings of Bondholders to consider any matter affecting their interests,\par including the modification by Extraordinary Resolution of these Conditions.\par The quorum at any such meeting for passing an Extraordinary Resolution shall\par (subject as provided in the Fiscal Agency Agreement in the event that all\par outstanding Bonds are held by one person) be two or more persons holding or\par representing a clear majority of the aggregate principal amount of the\par outstanding Bonds, or at any meeting that is adjourned two or more persons\par being or representing Bondholders (whatever the principal amount of the\par outstanding Bonds so held or represented), and the majority for passing an\par Extraordinary Resolution at any such meeting shall be at least 662/3 per cent.\par of the votes cast except that at any meeting the business of which includes\par consideration of proposals on Reserved Matters, including, inter alia, (i) to\par modify the maturity of the Bonds, (ii) to reduce or cancel the principal\par amount of, or interest on, the Bonds, (iii) to change the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of payment\par of the Bonds, (iv) to modify the provisions concerning the quorum required at\par any meeting of Bondholders or the majority required to pass an Extraordinary\par Resolution or (v) to modify the percentage required to pass any resolution,\par the necessary quorum for passing an Extraordinary Resolution shall (subject as\par provided in the Fiscal Agency Agreement in the event that all outstanding\par Bonds are held by one person) be two or more persons holding or representing\par at least 75 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the outstanding\par Bonds, or at any meeting that is adjourned at least 25 per cent. of the\par aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, and the majority for\par passing an Extraordinary Resolution on a Reserved Matter at any such meeting\par shall be at least 75 per cent. of the votes cast. An Extraordinary Resolution\par duly passed at any meeting of Bondholders will be binding on all Bondholders\par whether or not they are present at the meeting.\par \par In addition, the Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions\par relating to Written Resolutions. A "Written Resolution" means a resolution in\par writing signed by or on behalf of holders of at least 75 per cent. of the\par aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, in the case of a Reserved\par Matter, or 662/3 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the\par outstanding Bonds, in the case of a matter other than a Reserved Matter. Any\par Written Resolution may be contained in one document or several documents in\par like form, each signed by or on behalf of one or more Bondholders. Any Written\par Resolution shall be binding on all of the Bondholders, whether or not signed\par by them.\par \par (b) Modification and waiver\par \par The parties to the Fiscal Agency Agreement may agree, without the\par consent of the Bondholders, to any modification of any provision of the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement or the Bonds which is of a formal, minor or technical nature\par or is made to correct a manifest error.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 Further Issues\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation shall be at liberty from time to time,\par without the consent of the Bondholders, to create and issue further Bonds\par ranking equally in all respects (or in all respects save for payments made\par prior to the issuance of such further Bonds and, if applicable, the date and\par amount of the first payment on such further Bonds) so that the same shall be\par consolidated and form a single series with the Bonds, provided that such\par further issuance constitutes a qualified reopening for U.S. federal income\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 tax\par purposes.\par \par 14 Notices\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notices to the Bondholders will be sent to them by mail (airmail if overseas) at their respective addresses on the Register, and will be deemed to have been given on the fourth day after the date of mailing. So long as the Bonds are listed on the London Stock Exchange and the rules of the London Stock\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Exchange so require, notices with respect to the Bonds will also be\par published in a leading newspaper having general circulation in the United\par Kingdom (which is expected to be The Financial Times) or, if such publication\par is not practicable, in an English language newspaper having general\par circulation in Europe, and each such notice shall be deemed to have been given\par on the date of such publication, or if published more than once or on\par different dates, on the first date on which publication is made.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Indemnity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The U.S. dollar is the sole \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of account and payment for all\par sums payable by the Russian Federation under or in connection with the Bonds,\par including damages. Any amount received or recovered in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than\par the U.S. dollar (whether as a result of, or the enforcement of, a judgement or\par order of a court of any jurisdiction or otherwise) by any Bondholder in\par respect of any sum expressed to be due to it from the Russian Federation shall\par only constitute a discharge to the Russian Federation to the extent of the\par U.S. dollar amount which the recipient is able to purchase with the amount so\par received or recovered in that other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the date of that receipt or\par recovery (or, if it is not practicable to make that purchase on that date, on\par the first date on which it is practicable to do so). If that U.S. dollar\par amount is less than the U.S. dollar amount expressed to be due to the\par recipient under any Bond, the Russian Federation shall indemnify such\par recipient against any loss sustained by it as a result. In any event, the\par Russian Federation shall indemnify the recipient against the cost of making\par any such purchase. These indemnities constitute separate and independent\par obligations from the Russian Federation's other obligations, shall give rise\par to a separate and independent cause of action, shall apply irrespective of any\par indulgence granted by any Bondholder and shall continue in full force and\par effect despite any judgement, order, claim or proof for a liquidated amount in\par respect of any sum due under any Bond or any judgement or order.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No person shall have any right to enforce any term or condition of the Bonds under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17 Governing Law\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bonds and the Fiscal Agency Agreement and any non-contractual obligations arising out of or in connection with them shall be governed by and construed in accordance with the laws of England.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                                USE OF PROCEEDS\par \par The proceeds of the issue of the 2015 Bonds (net of commissions but\par before deduction of expenses) are expected to amount to approximately\par U.S.$1,988,500,000, and the proceeds of the issue of the 2020 Bonds (net of\par commissions but before deduction of expenses) are expected to amount to\par approximately U.S.$3,475,955,000. The proceeds of each issue will be used for\par general governmental purposes. Total commissions and expenses payable by the\par Russian Federation in connection with the 2015 Bonds are expected to be\par approximately U.S.$1,350,000, and total commissions and expenses payable by\par the Russian Federation in connection with the 2020 Bonds are expected to be\par approximately U.S.$2,400,000.\par \par                               RUSSIAN FEDERATION\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Territory, Population and Natural Resources\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation, or Russia (Rossiya), is a sovereign and democratic federal state, consisting of 83 sub-federal political units, referred to as "Federation subjects." Russia is the largest country in the world by land mass, covering 17.1 million square kilometres, approximately one-eighth of the Earth's land area. Russia borders 16 countries and spans nine time zones, extending some 9,000 kilometres from the Baltic Sea in the west to the Pacific Ocean in the east and some 4,000 kilometres from its southern border on the Black and Caspian Seas to its northern limits on the Arctic Ocean. The European area of the country consists of a large plain west of the Ural Mountains, while the Asian portion is divided into Siberia, occupying the vast central region of the country, and the Russian Far East, which borders on the Pacific. Russia has the tallest mountain in Europe (Elbrus, in the Caucasus, at 5,642 metres), the largest lake in Europe (Ladoga, near St. Petersburg) and the world's deepest lake (Baikal, near Irkutsk).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia is rich in natural resources. It is the world's\par second-largest producer of natural gas, the world's leading producer of oil\par and a significant producer of coal, uranium, nickel, palladium and platinum.\par As of January 2009, Russia's A, B and C1 natural gas reserves were estimated\par at 55.2 trillion cubic metres. The country also has substantial gold deposits\par (located mainly in Eastern Siberia and the Russian Far East) as well as\par significant deposits of zinc, lead, tin, silver, other rare metals and\par precious and semiprecious stones. Russia is among the world's leading\par producers of electricity, steel, fertilisers, cotton textiles and other goods.\par Forests cover approximately 52% of Russia's total land area and Russia's\par timber reserves, the largest of any country, are estimated at 83.3 billion\par cubic metres.\par \par Russia is home to approximately 142 million people (2.1% of the\par world's population at the beginning of 2010), ranks ninth in the world by\par population and has a population density of approximately 8.3 persons per\par square kilometre. Roughly 73% of the population lives in European Russia, and\par approximately the same percentage of Russia's population lives in cities.\par Russia's capital and largest city is Moscow with 10.5 million inhabitants.\par Other major cities include St. Petersburg, Novosibirsk, Nizhny Novgorod,\par Kazan, Ekaterinburg and Samara. Eleven Russian cities have a population of\par more than one million residents, including two in the Ural Mountains and two\par in Siberia.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to the most recent (2002) general census, ethnic Russians are the largest demographic group and account for approximately 80% of the total population. Other ethnic groups include Tatars (approximately 3.8% of the total population), Ukrainians (2.0%), Bashkirs (1.2%) and Chuvashs (1.1%). No other ethnic group accounts for more than 1% of Russia's total population.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia has a well-developed system of education, with a literacy\par rate exceeding 99%, according to the 2002 census, and grammar and middle\par school education is compulsory. Russia had approximately 1,134 institutions of\par higher education, with 7.5 million students, during the 2008-09 educational\par year, and an estimated labour force of 76 million people in November 2008. As\par of November 2008, over 99% of the labour force had at least eight years of\par schooling, 95% had at least ten years, 27.9% had received higher education and\par 46% had received professional training.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia and the Dissolution of the Soviet Union\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par From 1922 to 1991, Russia was the largest republic in the Union of\par Soviet Socialist Republics (the "Soviet Union"). For most of this period, the\par politics and economy of the Soviet Union were centralised under Communist\par rule. Beginning in 1985, Mikhail Gorbachev, the General Secretary of the\par Communist Party of the Soviet Union, led a movement to reform the Soviet\par political and economic system through the policies of openness (glasnost) and\par restructuring (perestroika). As a result of these policies, the Soviet Union\par greatly improved its relations with the West, effectively ending the Cold War,\par and the Soviet press gained greater independence and public discussion became\par increasingly open and informed. At the same time, the leading role of the\par Communist Party as a matter of law was abolished.\par \par General Secretary Gorbachev's economic reforms were mostly\par unsuccessful, however. The aim of perestroika was to revive economic growth\par through the introduction of market incentives within the framework of a\par planned economy. Despite a number of initiatives, the economy continued to\par decline and economic conditions deteriorated significantly in 1990 and 1991.\par This decline was mainly due to increases in social expenditures enacted by the\par Supreme Soviet, the Soviet Union's legislative body, and a jurisdictional\par dispute between Soviet and Russian authorities over the collection of taxes,\par causing central government revenues to decline. The resulting budget deficit\par reached approximately 20% of GDP in 1991. To cover this deficit, the Soviet\par Union resorted to short-term and uncoordinated foreign borrowing, the\par \par sale of virtually all of its gold and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and\par the increased printing of roubles. The increase in the money supply, combined\par with price controls on most products, led to excess money holdings, acute\par shortages of consumer goods and significant inflationary pressure (prices rose\par by 160% in 1991). Over the same period, output contracted and the Soviet Union\par fell into recession.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 1990, Russia declared its sovereignty (though not its independence) and began to challenge the central Soviet government's authority. On 17 June 1991, Boris Yeltsin became Russia's first popularly elected president, and he and the leaders of eight other Soviet Republics reached agreement with the central authorities on a new power sharing arrangement, known as the New Union Treaty. In August 1991, the imminent signing of the New Union Treaty provoked a coup attempt by Soviet hardliners. In the wake of the coup's failure, President Yeltsin banned the Communist Party of the Soviet Union within Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par On 8 December 1991, the Soviet Union was formally dissolved by the\par leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus, the original signatories of the 1922\par Union Treaty that had constituted the Soviet Union, and Russia became a fully\par independent state.\par \par For some time after the dissolution of the Soviet Union, the\par division of authority between Russia's federal, executive and legislative\par branches was unclear. Beginning in the summer of 1992, the Supreme Soviet,\par elected during the Soviet Union, sought to assert its authority over Russia's\par economic policy, opposing the reform programme of the Russian Government.\par Support for the Government's programme was demonstrated in a national\par referendum in April 1993, in which a majority of voters voted in favour of\par continuing the reform process. In September 1993, relations between the\par Government and the Supreme Soviet reached an impasse. That month, President\par Yeltsin issued a decree dissolving the Supreme Soviet, prompting several of\par its members to occupy the Russian parliament building and call for an armed\par uprising. Within a short time, the occupation was ended with the assistance of\par Interior Ministry troops. A new Constitution was subsequently drafted by the\par office of the President and approved by a specially convened Constitutional\par Assembly. In December 1993, the new Constitution was adopted in a national\par referendum. On the same day, the first elections were held for the members of\par the State Duma, the lower house of Russia's legislature, known as the Federal\par Assembly.\par \par Political System\par \par Constitution\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Constitution of Russia provides for a tripartite governmental structure in which the power of the state is divided among the executive, legislative and judicial branches, each independent of the others. The Constitution also establishes a federal system, allocating responsibilities between federal, sub-federal and local authorities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Constitution protects certain fundamental "rights and freedoms of the person and the citizen," and charges the state with guaranteeing the equal treatment of people of all races, nationalities and beliefs. In contrast to the Soviet Union's constitution, which emphasised state property and the one-party system, under the Russian Constitution all forms of property (including private property) are equal before the law and ideological diversity and a multi-party system are expressly recognised.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In general, the Constitution may be amended through passage of a\par special federal constitutional law, and its ratification by the legislatures\par of at least two-thirds of the Federation subjects. See "--Federal Structure\par and Regional Issues" for more information on Federation subjects. Passage of\par such a law requires the vote of a two-thirds majority of the State Duma, a\par three- fourths majority of the Federation Council (the upper house of the\par Federal Assembly) and signature by the President. The provisions of the\par Constitution that govern the nature of the constitutional system, individual\par rights of citizens and the amendment process, however, can be changed only by\par convening a Constitutional Assembly. A proposed new constitution may be\par adopted either by the vote of a two-thirds majority of the Constitutional\par Assembly or by a simple majority in a national referendum in which more than\par half of the eligible voters participate.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Executive Branch\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The President of the Russian Federation is Dmitry Medvedev, who was elected President in March 2008, receiving approximately 70.28% of the vote. See "--Political Parties, Recent Elections and Political Developments."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The President is the Head of State and the Supreme Commander of the\par Armed Forces. The President has broad powers, including the authority to\par declare a state of emergency, subject to approval of the Federation Council,\par the ability to commence military engagements, subject to immediate\par notification of the State Duma and Federation Council, the power to issue\par decrees and orders that have the force of law (although these may not\par contravene the Constitution or federal legislation), to suspend acts of\par sub-federal and local executive authorities, and to call national referenda.\par In addition, the President is empowered to arbitrate disputes between the\par federal authorities and the Federation subjects. The President is also\par responsible for foreign policy.\par \par The President has the power to veto bills passed by the Federal\par Assembly and, under certain circumstances, to dissolve the State Duma. The\par President may dissolve the State Duma if (i) the State Duma fails to accept\par the President's proposed candidate for Prime Minister in three successive\par votes, (ii) the State Duma twice within three months passes a motion of no\par confidence in the Government or (iii) the Government loses a confidence motion\par put before the State Duma by the Prime Minister. The State Duma may not,\par however, be dissolved at any time during the last six months of a presidential\par term, during the period between passage by the State Duma of an accusation\par initiating impeachment proceedings against the President and action by the\par Federation Council on such accusation, while a state of emergency covering all\par of Russia is in effect, or, in the case of (ii) or (iii) above, within the\par first year after State Duma elections. In the event the State Duma is\par dissolved, the President must schedule elections and a newly elected State\par Duma must be convened within four months.\par \par The President has significant appointment powers, including the\par power to appoint the Prime Minister (with the consent of the State Duma) and\par the other members of the Government (upon the nomination of the Prime\par Minister). The President may also dismiss deputy prime ministers and federal\par ministers at any time and may dismiss the Prime Minister, which would\par simultaneously lead to a dismissal of the Government. In addition, the\par President nominates candidates for Governor of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (for\par appointment by the State Duma), Prosecutor General (for appointment by the\par Federation Council) and judges to the Constitutional Court, the Supreme Court\par and the Supreme Arbitration Court (for appointment by the Federation Council).\par The President also has the power to dismiss the legislative and executive\par authorities of Federation subjects under certain circumstances. Beginning in\par December 2004, the President's powers were expanded to include nominating the\par heads of Federation subjects, who are now appointed by the legislatures of the\par relevant Federation subject and were previously elected by the residents of\par the relevant region. See "--Federal Structure and Regional Issues."\par \par The President is elected in a national election for a term of four\par years. Beginning with the next Presidential election in 2012, the President\par will be elected to a six-year term. Under Russia's Constitution, the President\par may not serve more than two consecutive terms. The Constitution also provides\par for the early termination of the President's term of office in the event of\par the President's death, resignation, impeachment, or persistent inability to\par exercise his powers for health reasons. New presidential elections must be\par held within three months of an early termination. Impeachment of the President\par requires an accusation supported by the vote of a two-thirds majority of the\par State Duma, followed by a vote in favour of impeachment by a two-thirds\par majority of the Federation Council, with the subsequent confirmation by the\par Supreme Court of the legality of the accusation and by the Constitutional\par Court of the observance of due process. Under the Constitution, whenever the\par President is incapable for any reason of carrying out his duties, the\par obligations of the office are temporarily assumed by the Prime Minister,\par except that the Prime Minister, as acting President, may not dissolve the\par State Duma or propose any national referendum or changes to the Constitution.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government consists of the Prime Minister, deputy prime ministers and federal ministers, all of whom are appointed by the President as described above. The Government is automatically dissolved after each presidential election in order to permit the President to form a new Government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Government is responsible for implementing federal laws,\par presidential decrees and international agreements. In particular, the\par Government is responsible for preparing and implementing the federal budget,\par establishing a unified financial, credit and monetary policy, carrying out\par social policy, preserving public order and defending the rights and freedoms\par of citizens.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Prime Minister of the Russian Federation is Vladimir Putin, who took office in May 2008 following his nomination by President Medvedev. Before becoming Prime Minister, Mr. Putin was President of the Russian Federation. Mr. Putin was initially appointed acting President upon the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par resignation of Boris Yeltsin in January 2000, was subsequently\par elected President in March 2000, receiving approximately 53% of the vote, and\par was then re-elected in March 2004, when he received approximately 71% of the\par vote. See "--Political Parties, Recent Elections and Political Developments."\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Legislative Branch\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The legislative branch consists of the Federal Assembly, which comprises a lower chamber, known as the State Duma, and an upper chamber, known as the Federation Council.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The State Duma consists of 450 deputies, elected for a four-year\par term by a system of proportional representation. Beginning with the December\par 2011 elections, deputies to the State Duma will be elected to five-year terms.\par In accordance with the Federal Law on the Elections of Deputies of the State\par Duma, which became effective in December 2006, deputies are chosen from "party\par lists" on the basis of a nationwide election, with seats allocated in\par proportion to the number of votes received by the party. Only parties\par receiving 7% or more of the vote qualify for "party list" seats. Deputies are\par not able to change party affiliation during their term of office without\par surrendering their seat. No person may simultaneously serve as a State Duma\par deputy and hold a position in the Government.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federation Council, with 166 deputies, represents Russia's 83 Federation subjects. See "--Federal Structure and Regional Issues." Each Federation subject appoints two members of the Federation Council, one chosen by the legislative body of the Federation subject and the other by its executive body. The members of the Federation Council have to work on a full-time basis and cannot occupy any other office.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par For a bill to become federal law, it must first be passed by a\par majority vote in the State Duma, then be approved by a majority vote in the\par Federation Council and finally be signed by the President. Rejection of a bill\par by the Federation Council can be overridden by a two-thirds majority vote in\par the State Duma. Rejection of a bill by the President can be overridden by a\par two-thirds majority of each of the Federation Council and the State Duma.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Judicial Branch\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia has three courts of final appeal. The Constitutional Court\par has jurisdiction over matters relating to the interpretation of the\par Constitution, including the constitutionality of federal laws, decrees of the\par President, resolutions of the Government, resolutions of the State Duma and\par the Federation Council, laws and legal documents of Federation subjects and\par agreements between federal and sub-federal authorities. The Supreme\par Arbitration Court and lower arbitration courts have jurisdiction over economic\par disputes. Judicial authority is otherwise vested in the Supreme Court and\par lower courts of general jurisdiction over civil, criminal, administrative and\par other matters.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Judges of the Constitutional Court, Supreme Court and Supreme Arbitration Court are nominated by the President and appointed by the Federation Council. Judges of lower federal courts are appointed by the President in accordance with procedures established by federal legislation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Political Parties, Recent Elections and Political Developments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under federal law, all Russian citizens who are at least 18 years old have the right to vote in presidential and State Duma elections, regional and local elections and national, regional and local referenda.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russian legislation contains a number of provisions designed to\par encourage the development of a stable party system. First, candidates must\par collect a specified number of signatures to qualify for elections. In the case\par of presidential elections, a candidate must collect at least two million\par signatures in order to be placed on the ballot. In order to participate in\par elections to the State Duma, a political party must also obtain a specified\par number of signatures supporting its party-list (150 thousand signatures for\par the next election, in 2011, and 120 thousand signatures for elections\par thereafter). Second, political parties must garner at least 7% of the votes in\par party-list elections to qualify for seats allocated on the basis of\par proportional representation in the State Duma. Political parties that garner\par between 6% and 7% of votes are allocated two seats in the State Duma, and\par parties that collect between 5% and 6% of votes are allocated one seat in the\par State Duma. Third, the rules for presidential elections, which call for a\par run-off election between the first and second place candidates if no candidate\par wins a majority in the initial round of voting, discourage fragmentation of\par the vote.\par \par In the most recent State Duma election, held in December 2007, the\par pro-Kremlin party Edinaya Rossiya (United Russia), which is currently led by\par Prime Minister Putin and is the product of the 2001 merger of two large\par centrist parties, Yedinstvo (Unity) and Otechestvo-Vsya Rossiya\par (Fatherland-All Russia), received a majority of the votes cast (64.3%) and\par currently holds 315 State Duma seats. The Communist Party received 11.6% of\par the votes cast in 2007 (down from 12.6% in 2003 and 24.3% in 1999) and now\par holds 57 State Duma seats. Two other parties are also represented in the State\par Duma: the Liberal Democratic Party (8.1% of the votes cast and a total of 40\par seats) and Fair Russia (7.7% of the votes cast and a total of 38 seats).\par Parties that received votes in the December 2007 election, but did not pass\par the 7% threshold required to seat members in the Duma include the Agrarian\par Party of Russia (2.3% of the votes cast); the Russian Democratic Party\par "Yabloko" (1.6% of the votes cast); "Civil Strength" (1.1% of the votes cast);\par and several other parties, none of which received more than 1% of the vote.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Structure and Regional Issues\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia consists of 83 Federation subjects, comprising 21 republics, nine krais, 46 oblasts, two cities of federal importance ( Moscow and St. Petersburg), one autonomous oblast and four autonomous okrugs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In an effort to accelerate socio-economic development through the\par combination of resources and the reduction of duplicative services, several\par Federation subjects have recently merged. On 1 December 2005, Perm Oblast and\par Komi-Permiatsky Autonomous Okrug merged into a new, single sub-federal unit,\par known as Perm Krai, and on 1 January 2007, Krasnoyarsk Krai, Evenki Autonomous\par Okrug and Taimyr Autonomous Okrug merged into an enlarged Krasnoyarsk Krai. In\par referenda held on 23 October 2005, residents of the Kamchatka Oblast and the\par Koryak Autonomous Okrug approved the merger of these territories into\par Kamchatka Krai, effective 1 July 2007, following the Duma's June 2006 approval\par of a Federal Constitutional Law authorising the creation of an enlarged\par Kamchatka Krai. On 16 April 2006, residents of Irkutsk Oblast and the Ust-Orda\par Buryat Autonomous Okrug approved by separate referenda the merger of these two\par regions into an enlarged Irkutsk Oblast, effective 1 January 2008, and, on 11\par March 2007, residents of Chita Oblast and Agin-Buryat Autonomous Okrug\par approved by separate referenda the merger of these two regions into an\par enlarged Zabaykalsky Krai, with effect from 1 March 2008.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many of the republics and autonomous regions contain a substantial number of ethnic non-Russians, with non-Russians constituting a majority in certain regions (e.g., the Republic of Tatarstan and the Republic of Kalmykia).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federation subjects form the intermediate level of government, and are further subdivided into approximately 12,000 municipalities, comprising cities or geographically separated county territories (raions). Lower-level governments at the county (raion), city and village levels are subordinate to their respective Federation subjects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Throughout its history, Russia has generally been a unitary state\par with a single central government. Since the dissolution of the Soviet Union,\par however, Federation subjects have obtained greater decision-making authority\par and financial autonomy, and Russia is now a federal state.\par \par The Federation Treaty of 31 March 1992, signed by 87 Federation\par subjects (all but the Chechen Republic and the Republic of Tatarstan),\par initially granted each Federation subject a measure of control over budgetary\par and external policy, as well as local natural resources. The Constitution\par subsequently refined the division of authority between Russia and the\par Federation subjects, expressly incorporating the provisions of the Federation\par Treaty to the extent they were not inconsistent with other provisions of the\par Constitution. Certain areas of governance are reserved by the Constitution\par exclusively to the federal authorities, including management of federal\par property, the issuance of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulation, customs policy,\par foreign relations, military defence, atomic energy and space exploration.\par Joint federal-regional authority is prescribed in a number of other areas,\par including tax administration, ownership and use of land and natural resources,\par environmental protection, social assistance, education, health, science and\par cultural facilities, and the selection of certain court and law enforcement\par officials. Responsibility for all matters not reserved to the federal\par authorities or to joint federal-regional competence is reserved for\par Federation subjects. The Constitution prohibits any sub-federal barriers to\par the free movement of goods, capital and labour throughout Russia.\par \par In May 2000, President Putin signed a presidential decree creating\par seven federal circuits, each comprising several Federation subjects, and\par authorising the President to appoint a representative to each federal circuit.\par The representatives' responsibilities include facilitating the implementation\par of the\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President's domestic and foreign policies, supervising the implementation of federal decisions in the Federation subjects and reporting to the President on the state of affairs in the Federation subjects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In August 2000, a law aimed at improving management of the\par Federation subjects and lower-level authorities came into effect. The law\par grants the President the power to dismiss the chief legislative and executive\par authorities of a Federation subject if the authorities refuse to repeal local\par legislation held by a court to be contrary to the Constitution or federal law\par and harmful to the fundamental rights and freedoms of Russian citizens or that\par prevent federal authorities from performing their statutory functions.\par \par A new law abolishing the direct election of executives of the\par Federation subjects was adopted in December 2004. Under this law, the\par President may nominate and dismiss the executives of a Federation subject, but\par Presidential nominations must still be approved by the Federation subject's\par legislative body. This law was intended to strengthen central control of the\par country and to facilitate the federal Government's efforts in combating\par domestic terrorism.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In general, disputes between the federal authorities and Federation subjects have been resolved peacefully through the political process, with the notable exception of the military confrontation in the Chechen Republic and periodic unrest in the other republics of the North Caucasus, including Daghestan and Ingushetia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 International Relations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's Position in the International Community\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia has been recognised by the international community as the\par successor to the Soviet Union and maintains diplomatic relations with 190\par countries. Russia is currently a member of many international organisations,\par including the United Nations, where it is a permanent member of the Security\par Council and, accordingly, plays an active role in maintaining international\par peace and security.\par \par Russia is a member of the G-8 group of industrial nations, having\par chaired that group in 2006, and the G-20. Since 1992, Russia has been a\par member of the International Monetary Fund ("IMF") and the International Bank\par for Reconstruction and Development (the "World Bank"). See "Public\par Debt--Relations with International Financial Institutions." Russia is also a\par member of the International Finance Corporation  and participates as a donor in\par the International Development Association. In December 1996, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par of the Russian Federation (the "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ") became a member of the Bank for\par International Settlements, and in September 1997 Russia became a member of the\par Paris Club of creditor nations.\par \par In June 1993, Russia applied for membership in the General\par Agreement on Tariffs and Trade, which later became the World Trade\par Organisation (the "WTO"). The Government has placed a high priority on joining\par the WTO, in part because entry into the WTO would allow Russia to use the\par organisation's procedures to combat discrimination against Russian producers\par and exporters, which the Government believes has had a negative effect on\par Russian exports. Russia has concluded bilateral agreements on its accession to\par the WTO with most of the members of the working group established to consider\par Russia's membership. Before it can join the WTO, Russia must reach agreement\par with the United States and Georgia on several outstanding trade issues, as\par well as ensure that Russia's laws conform with WTO standards in five key\par areas: access to the market for goods, intellectual property rights,\par agricultural tariffs, legislative and institutional framework and access to\par the market for services. In 2002, both the European Union (the "EU") and the\par United States recognised Russia as a "market economy."\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 1994, Russia concluded a Declaration on Cooperation with the Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (the "OECD"), and, in May 1996, Russia formally applied for membership in the OECD. In 2007, the OECD on a ministerial level made the decision to invite Russia to begin negotiations regarding its accession. Such negotiations are currently underway. Russia is currently participating on a permanent basis in the activities of more than 20 working groups of the OECD.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 1997, President Yeltsin and the leaders of 16 NATO nations\par signed the "Founding Act on Mutual Cooperation," establishing new principles\par to govern their relationship and providing for consultation between Russia and\par NATO. In December 2001, Russia and NATO agreed upon a process aimed at\par enhancing future cooperation and in May 2002 agreed to work together to fight\par terrorism and to curtail the spread of nuclear, chemical and biological\par weapons. In May 2002, a NATO-Russia Council was established, under which more\par than 25 working groups have been created to enhance political contacts and\par develop\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 cooperation between Russia and NATO members on issues of mutual interest, including terrorism, missile defence, arms control, peacekeeping and civil defence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia and Regional Cooperation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia has wide-ranging contacts with the EU, its most significant\par trading partner. The Agreement on Partnership and Cooperation between Russia\par and the EU, in effect since December 1997, was amended in April 2004 and March\par 2008 to allow new EU members to accede to it. The agreement establishes a\par framework for the parties' economic and political relationship.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strategic cooperation between Russia and the EU is realised through political dialogue and collaboration. Since May 2003, the EU and Russia have arranged 24 Russia-EU summits to discuss, among other issues, integration and cooperation in five areas of common interest: economy; freedom, security and justice; foreign policy and security; and research, education and culture. At the May 2005 summit, a roadmap for each area of common interest was developed setting out the parties' plan for further integration. Both sides have created working groups to fulfil the roadmap objectives. EU- Russia summits take place yearly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On 15 June 2001, Russia, China, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and Uzbekistan signed a declaration on the creation of the Shanghai Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Organisation (SCO). Russia chaired the SCO in 2008-2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia is a member of the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (the "EBRD") and the Black Sea Bank for Trade and Development. Russia also participates in a number of regional cooperation forums, including the Barents Euro-Arctic Council, the Council of Baltic Sea States and the Organisation of the Black Sea Economic Cooperation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia has been a partner of the Association of South-East Asian\par Nations ("ASEAN") since 1996. The first Russia-ASEAN summit took place in\par 2005, where both sides issued a joint declaration on cooperation through 2015.\par The second summit is expected to take place in the fall of 2010. Since 1998,\par Russia has been a member of the Asian Pacific Economic Cooperation Forum,\par which is expected to hold a summit in 2012 in Vladivostok. Since 2003, Russia\par has been developing regional cooperation within the Russia-India-China\par format. Since 1999, Russia has been participating in the activities of the\par Conference on Interaction and Confidence Building Measures in Asia. Russia is\par expected to participate in the organisation's third summit in June 2010. In\par 2005, Russia joined the Asia Cooperation Dialogue. In October 2010, Russia\par expects to join the Asia-Europe Forum.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia, the Former Soviet Union and the CIS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par After the dissolution of the Soviet Union, Russia concluded\par separate agreements ("zero-option agreements") with all of the other republics\par of the former Soviet Union except the three Baltic republics. All of the\par zero-option agreements have been mutually ratified, except the agreement with\par Ukraine, which has not yet been ratified by Ukraine. Each zero-option\par agreement provides that, as between Russia and the other former Soviet\par republic, Russia is responsible for virtually all the external debt contracted\par on behalf of the authorities of the former Soviet Union and receives in return\par all claims on former Soviet Union assets located outside the territory of the\par other former Soviet republic. The Government regularised its relations with\par virtually all the external creditors of the former Soviet Union and agreed to\par a schedule for the repayment of former Soviet Union debt.\par \par In April 1996, the Government entered into an agreement\par rescheduling approximately U.S.$33 billion of indebtedness to the Paris Club\par of official creditors. In August 1999, the Government concluded a further\par rescheduling agreement providing for the deferral of approximately U.S.$8.3\par billion of indebtedness to Paris Club creditors. In the summer of 2005, Russia\par prepaid U.S.$15 billion of its Paris Club indebtedness. In August 2006, Russia\par prepaid the remaining balance of approximately U.S.$21.6 billion owed to its\par Paris Club creditors.\par \par In December 1997, Vnesheconombank  restructured the Soviet-era debt\par owed to the London Club of commercial creditors. In the wake of the events of\par 17 August 1998, when the Government announced that it would not meet its\par obligations under its short-term rouble-denominated treasury bills, i.e.,\par GKOs and OFZs, Russia, Vnesheconombank  and a committee of London Club\par creditors agreed, in February 2000, on terms for the further restructuring of\par Vnesheconombank 's London Club debt. The agreed restructuring terms have been\par implemented through a series of exchange offers. At 1 January 2010, U.S.$33.1\par million had still not been tendered for exchange and remains outstanding. In\par December 2002, December 2006 and\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 2009, Russia restructured most of the Soviet-era debt owed to uninsured trade creditors, and in March 2004 Russia restructured most of the Soviet-era debts owed by Vnesheconombank to the International Bank for Economic Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ("IBEC") and to the International Investment Bank ("IIB"). See "Public Debt--External Debt Restructuring" for a further discussion of Russia's indebtedness to the Paris Club, the London Club, Soviet-era uninsured trade creditors, IBEC and the IIB.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia is a member of the Commonwealth of Independent States (the\par "CIS"), which was founded in December 1991 to promote mutual cooperation among\par its members. In 1993, an Agreement on the Establishment of an Economic Union\par was signed by the CIS countries, and the Interstate Bank was established by\par the CIS member governments and Central banks. As of 1 October 2009, the\par Interstate Bank had assets of five billion roubles. The main goals of the\par Interstate Bank include facilitating multilateral settlements among CIS\par national central banks and providing financing for interstate projects.\par Currently, 11 of the 15 former Soviet Republics are members of the CIS. The\par three Baltic states never joined the organisation, Turkmenistan changed its\par status to associate member in 2005 and Georgia withdrew from the organisation,\par effective August 2009.\par \par In 1995, Russia and Belarus agreed to form a customs union. Russia\par and Belarus, together with Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan, signed a\par treaty in 1999 to create a customs union and a common economic territory and a\par treaty in 2000 concerning the creation of the Eurasian Economic Community.\par Belarus, Kazakhstan and Russia are currently carrying out work on creating the\par customs union and a single economic space. In November 2009, Russia, Belarus\par and Kazakhstan concluded several agreements unifying customs tariff and\par non-tariff regulations among the three nations, which came into force on 1\par January 2010, and establishing a customs code that will harmonise customs\par procedures, which is expected to come into force on 1 July 2010.\par \par Since 1995, Russia and Belarus have pursued closer economic and\par political integration. In December 1999, Russia and Belarus signed a treaty on\par the creation of a unified state and a programme for its implementation. Under\par the treaty, Russia and Belarus are to preserve their independence and\par sovereignty, while developing a single economic space, joint social policies,\par coordinated foreign and defence policies and integrated military structures.\par Amendments to the laws of Russia or Belarus may be necessary to fully\par implement this treaty. A draft constitution for the two countries was approved\par in April 2003, which envisaged the unification of Belarus' and Russia's tax\par and social welfare systems, and provided Belarus the option to adopt the\par Russian rouble. In February 2009, Russia and Belarus initiated the first stage\par of joint military officer training programmes designed to integrate the\par military structures of both countries.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia is also a member of the Collective Security Treaty Organisation, founded in 2002 on the basis of the CIS Collective Security Treaty, to promote collective security among its members, which include Armenia, Belarus, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and Uzbekistan, in addition to Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In August 2008, Russia and Georgia fought a brief war over the\par territories of South Ossetia and Abkhazia. Both territories are now de facto\par independent from Georgia and have been legally recognised by Russia,\par Nicaragua, Venezuela and the Pacific island state of Nauru. In the aftermath\par of the August 2008 hostilities, Georgia severed diplomatic relations with\par Russia, and Russian troops are currently stationed in both South Ossetia and\par Abkhazia.\par \par On several occasions since the collapse of the Soviet Union,\par disputes have arisen with Ukraine and Belarus concerning the supply and\par transport of natural gas. Russia is dependent on pipelines in Ukraine and\par Belarus to deliver a significant portion of the natural gas it exports to\par western Europe (Russia supplies approximately one-quarter of the natural gas\par consumed in the EU), and Ukraine and Belarus are, in turn, dependent on\par Russian gas to meet their domestic requirements. At the end of 2008, Russia\par and Ukraine failed to sign the necessary contracts for the supply of Russian\par natural gas to Ukraine. As a result, Russia reduced its supplies to Ukraine,\par while at the same time preserved its supplies to European customers. Following\par Ukraine's reported diversion of Russian transit gas for its own use, Russia\par completely halted shipments of gas to Ukraine. On 19 January 2009, both\par countries agreed to a 10-year transit and supply contract. Under this\par agreement, prices for gas supplies are set pursuant to a European pricing\par formula. Similar disagreements over the transit and supply of oil have\par periodically arisen with Belarus.\par \par                              THE RUSSIAN ECONOMY\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's initial economic reform programme, first outlined by President Yeltsin in October 1991, set out to achieve three principal goals: liberalisation, privatisation and financial stabilisation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Liberalisation of the Russian economy required a fundamental\par redefinition of the role of the state, which had in the Soviet era regulated\par virtually all economic and financial activities. The subsequent liberalisation\par of most prices has permitted market forces to determine prices to a\par significant extent and to play an increased role in the allocation of\par resources and output. At present, only certain public services and\par legislatively defined "natural monopolies" (for example, pipeline transport of\par oil and gas, electricity transmission and production, railway transport,\par terminal, port and airport services, and postal services) are subject to price\par regulation. Internationally, liberalisation of Russia's trade regime has\par helped redirect trade flows away from historically important former socialist\par markets towards OECD countries and developing nations and contributed to an\par overall rise in exports. See "Balance of Payments and Foreign Trade--Foreign\par Trade."\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beginning in January 1992, the Government implemented a privatisation programme, made possible by the legal recognition of private property and the creation of an economic and legal environment more conducive to the development of private economic activity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia's economic programme came under severe pressure in the\par second half of 1997 and the first half of 1998, following the Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par and financial crisis and a sharp fall in world prices for oil and other\par commodities, which adversely affected the Government's ability to continue to\par finance its budget deficit and to maintain the value of the rouble against the\par dollar. In 1999, Russia was assigned sovereign credit ratings of CCC (Fitch),\par B3 (Moody's ) and CCC\u226\'00\'E2\u710\'02\'C6\u8217\'20\'19 (Standard & Poor's ).\par \par In 2004, the responsibilities of Federal Governmental structures\par were reformed. A new three-tiered system of federal executive bodies was\par established, which assigned responsibility to federal ministries for the\par elaboration of state policy and legal regulation, to federal services for the\par supervision, control and protection of state security and state borders and to\par federal agencies for the provision of public services and the management of\par state property and law enforcement (other than supervision and control).\par \par In January 2006, the Government adopted a Medium-Term Program for\par the period 2006-2008. The principal priorities of the program were improving\par the investment climate, diversifying the economy, modernising the industrial\par sector, reducing Government involvement in the economy, stimulating Russian\par innovation, developing the housing and mortgage markets and improving the\par quality of the healthcare and education systems.\par \par In November 2008, the Government adopted a Long-Term Program for\par Social and Economic Development for the period through 2020. The primary\par objectives of this program are to stimulate Russian innovation, including in\par high-technology industries, diversify the economy, promote regional economic\par development and reform the country's natural monopolies.\par \par In response to the global financial crisis, Russia has enacted a\par set of measures designed to restore investor confidence and support the\par medium-term economic growth of the country. Many of these measures have been\par aimed at bolstering the liquidity of the Russian banking sector and\par facilitating the flow of credit within the economy. See "Monetary and\par Financial System--Banking--Anti-Crisis Measures" for a discussion of the\par policies specifically related to the banking sector. However, the Government\par has also implemented policies to support the real sector of the economy, the\par labour market as well as households.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In April 2009, the Government adopted its Anti-Crisis Programme for 2009, which, in addition to policies aimed at the banking sector, included the following measures:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The allocation of 300 billion roubles for federal budget loans and transfers to sub-federal governments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Commitment to support approximately 300 key enterprises with supplemental recapitalisations, state loan guarantees (for which the Government has allocated 300 billion roubles) and direct state support.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The increase of federal support for the defence-industrial complex, including (i) the allocation of (x) up to 100 billion roubles in State guarantees of previously issued loans to companies in the defence industry, and (y) three billion roubles to certain strategic companies of the defence-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 industrial complex; and (ii) equity injections into certain key companies, including 15 billion roubles into Russian Aircraft Company "MIG" and eight billion roubles into the Khrunichev Space Center.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - To support development of the agricultural and fishing industries, the allocation of over 200 billion roubles. An additional 95 billion roubles from regional budgets have been earmarked for the same purpose.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The allocation of 39 billion roubles to support the automotive industry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par - To support the transport industry, the allocation of (i) 560\par billion roubles for general development, including approximately 342 billion\par roubles to improve roads; and (ii) 50 billion roubles to subsidise losses\par incurred by Open Joint Stock Company "Russian Railways " in connection with the\par reduction in tariffs for railway shipments in 2009. In 2009, 570 billion\par roubles were spent on general development of the transport industry, including\par 194 billion roubles to improve roads, and 40.7 billion roubles were spent to\par subsidise losses incurred as a result of tariff reductions. Within the\par framework of the Anti-Crisis Programme for 2009, Russian Railways  carried out\par the issuance and placement of 145 billion roubles of infrastructure bonds in\par 2009.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In December 2009, the Government passed its Anti-Crisis Programme for 2010, the key elements of which include the following measures:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Various measures to support the labour market (approximately 36 billion roubles, which amount was subsequently increased to 40.5 billion roubles as a result of a February 2010 Government resolution).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Support for certain strategic enterprises ( 40 billion roubles).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Various investments in the automotive industry, including: (i) subsidies to trade organisations for losses incurred in Russia's "cash for clunkers" programme ( 11 billion roubles); and (ii) purchase by the federal authorities of various automotive products and machinery for road repairs ( 20 billion roubles).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Various investments in the housing market ( 15 billion roubles).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Establishment of a fund to carry out investment projects in single- industry towns ( 10 billion roubles).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Increase of the charter capital of Open Joint Stock Company "Unified Aviation Construction Corporation" in order to facilitate debt repayments ( 10.7 billion roubles).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Civil and Commercial Law\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Since 1992, Russia has sought to establish a legal framework for\par economic relations between independent legal persons and entities. The rule of\par law in Russia nonetheless continues to be undermined by persistent gaps in\par legislation, inconsistencies between legal norms at the federal level as well\par as between norms at the federal and regional levels, the significant degree of\par discretion given to state officials in many areas and the inexperience of some\par Russian judges and their susceptibility to outside influences, especially at\par the regional and local levels.\par \par The Constitution protects the right of Russian citizens to hold\par private property. Property rights and basic rules for commercial relations are\par set out in Russia's Civil Code (the "Civil Code"). Part I of the Civil Code,\par passed in 1994, establishes the principles of contract and property law. The\par Civil Code also specifies the forms that private enterprises may take,\par including corporations, partnerships and limited liability companies. Part II\par of the Civil Code, passed in 1996, regulates particular types of contractual\par relationships, including sales contracts, leases, credit agreements and\par insurance policies. Part III of the Civil Code came into effect in 2002 and\par covers inheritance law and private international law. The fourth and final\par part of the Civil Code, which came into effect on 1 January 2008, codifies\par rules on intellectual property matters.\par \par The Federal Law on the Enactment of the Land Code (the "Land\par Code"), enacted in 2001, regulates the ownership of non-agricultural real\par estate and establishes general principles of private land ownership. The law\par in this area remains incomplete, however, and there are many local rules and\par standards that limit the purchase and ownership of land. As a result, private\par ownership of land is not widespread in Russia, and a land market does not\par exist to the same extent as is common in countries with more developed market\par economies. According to the Land Code, legal entities that are currently using\par their land based on the right of permanent (perpetual) use are required to\par purchase or lease their land before 1 January 2012. The Land\par \par Code does not cover agricultural land, which is instead regulated\par by the Federal Law on the Circulation of Agricultural Land. This law creates a\par unified market framework at the federal level for the purchase and sale of\par farmland. Under the agricultural law, foreigners are not allowed to purchase\par farmland.\par \par The Law on Privatisation of State and Municipal Property dated 21\par December 2001 (the "Privatisation Law") sets forth the procedures that govern\par the privatisation of state and municipal property, including the requirements\par for potential buyers and other related matters. Under the Privatisation Law, a\par privatisation program, including a list of enterprises to be sold, is adopted\par annually. In November 2009, the Government published the list of enterprises\par expected to be privatised in 2010, which includes enterprises in the\par electricity, construction, insurance, agricultural, transportation and machine\par building industries, among others.\par \par The Law on Joint-Stock Companies dated 26 December 1995 came into\par effect in 1996 and was subsequently amended on a yearly basis between 1999 and\par 2009. The Law on Joint-Stock Companies sets forth the basic framework for\par corporate organisation and corporate governance, including the formation of\par companies, shareholder rights and liabilities (including the ability to enter\par into shareholder agreements), the role of directors, interested party\par transactions, mergers and acquisitions, shareholder buy-outs and share capital\par and dividends. The Law on the Securities Market, adopted in 1996 and\par subsequently amended, regulates the issuance and circulation of securities.\par Pursuant to this law, companies that issue securities must, with limited\par exceptions, register the issuance by filing a prospectus and publicly\par reporting certain information on a quarterly basis. The law also regulates the\par activities of professional participants in the securities market, aims to\par strengthen the protection of investors by imposing rules on market\par professionals and contains framework provisions on the listing and public\par trading of securities of foreign issuers in Russia.\par \par The Law on Limited Liability Companies dated 8 February 1998, as\par amended, sets forth the basic framework for the organisation and governance of\par limited liability companies. On 1 July 2009, substantial amendments to this\par law came into effect, the primary purposes of which were to streamline\par procedures for establishing limited liability companies, to improve the\par regulation of the purchase and sale of interests in limited liability\par companies and to make the ownership structure of limited liability companies\par more transparent.\par \par The current bankruptcy law, which replaced the prior law adopted in\par 1998, came into effect in November 2002 and was subsequently amended. The\par bankruptcy law provides basic rules for declaring an enterprise bankrupt, for\par managing and liquidating an enterprise after it has been declared bankrupt,\par for satisfying creditors' claims and for the bankruptcy process generally. The\par bankruptcy law permits an enterprise to be declared bankrupt if it is unable\par to make payments to its creditors (including tax authorities) in excess of\par 100,000 roubles within three months of such payments becoming due. The\par bankruptcy law also expands the types of legal entities that are subject to\par the bankruptcy law and clarifies the procedure for appointing managers of\par bankrupt enterprises and for initiating insolvency proceedings.\par \par The legislative framework for the restructuring of banks is set out\par in the Law on Insolvency (Bankruptcy) of Credit Institutions, which was\par adopted in February 1999 and subsequently amended between 2000 and 2009. This\par legislation details the standards for the appointment by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par temporary bank administrators, provides an expedited liquidation procedure for\par banks and gives the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the power to accredit the receivers of\par bankrupt banks and to propose receivers for approval by arbitration courts.\par \par Russia's Strategic Investments Law, which came into force in May\par 2008, regulates foreign investments (whether direct or indirect) in Russian\par businesses having strategic importance in matters of state defence and\par security and, inter alia, requires foreign investors to receive prior consent\par of a special government commission before acquiring control (including through\par the purchase of certain percentages of voting shares or certain management\par rights) of such strategic companies.\par \par Under Russia's Foreign Investment Law, adopted in July 1999,\par foreign investors are ensured equal treatment with domestic investors, with\par some exceptions in the area of land ownership and leasing, access to natural\par resources and participation in privatisations. The law provides protections\par against nationalisation and expropriation without compensation, ensures the\par free transfer of investment proceeds in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the reinvestment\par of profits in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and provides recourse to Russian courts. The\par Foreign Investment Law provides that any acquisition (whether direct or\par indirect) by a foreign state or international organisation or entities\par controlled by them of (i) more than 25% of the voting shares of a Russian\par company; or (ii) any power to block decisions of the management bodies of a\par Russian\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 company requires prior approval of the special government commission established by the Strategic Investments Law.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia's Federal Law "On Protection of Competition" (the\par "Competition Law") came into force in October 2006. The Competition Law\par generally prohibits any concerted action, agreement or coordination of\par business activity that results or may result in the restriction of\par competition, including (a) price fixing; (b) coordination of auction bids; (c)\par partition of a commodity market by territory, volume of sales or purchases,\par types of goods, customers or suppliers; (d) fixing of disparate prices for the\par same goods for reasons other than economic or technical reasons; and (e)\par creation of barriers to entering or exiting a market. The Competition Law also\par regulates persons (or a group of affiliated persons) who have a dominant\par position in a particular market. A dominant position may arise in\par circumstances when (a) the person has a market share in a particular market in\par excess of 50%, unless it is specifically established that the person does not\par have a dominant position; (b) the person has a market share in a particular\par market in excess of 35% but less than 50%, and it is specifically established\par by the Federal Antimonopoly Service (the "FAS") that the person has a dominant\par position based on certain factors; or (c) in certain circumstances, the person\par has a market share in a particular market less than 35% but exceeding shares\par of competitors in the same market, which can have a crucial impact on general\par conditions of commodity circulation in the market. The FAS is authorised to\par employ a variety of means to restrain anti-competitive behaviour and can in\par extreme cases compel the break-up of enterprises. In addition, the Competition\par Law establishes a merger control regime, pursuant to which FAS consent is\par required prior to closing in particular circumstances, including the\par following: (a) acquisitions of voting rights in a joint stock company or\par assets of a person above a certain threshold; (b) mergers and consolidations\par that result in an aggregate asset value or aggregate annual revenues above a\par certain amount; (c) creation of a company, if its share capital or the\par contributors of such share capital meet certain specifications. The\par Competition Law also provides for a mandatory post-transactional notification\par (within 45 days of the closing) to FAS in certain circumstances.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A new Customs Code took effect in January 2004. The Customs Code seeks to reduce the volume of required documentation and to shorten customs clearance times, reflecting a shift in policy to a focus on developing foreign economic activity. This law strives to bring current legislation into line with international standards.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In an attempt to maximise value and increase the efficiency of\par state assets, a law on concession agreements, enacted in July 2005,\par establishes a framework for the Government to transfer the management of\par state-owned property to private companies for a term provided for in the\par relevant concession agreement. Under the law, the private company is entitled\par to retain the profits generated from the property in exchange for investing in\par and managing it.\par \par A law on special economic zones in the Russian Federation, adopted\par in July 2005, aims to create technical, industrial, production, tourist and\par health resort zones. Commercial and industrial residents of these zones enjoy\par reductions in property and land tax and import duties. They also benefit from\par greater depreciation allowances (except with respect to technical zones) and\par are permitted to write-off research and development costs within the periods\par when such costs were incurred without limitation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gross Domestic Product\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth certain information regarding Russia's gross domestic product ("GDP") for the years 2005 to 2009:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                           Gross Domestic Product(1)\par \par                                                                                    2005    2006    2007    2008    2009\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nominal GDP (billions of roubles)                                                21,625  26,903  33,258  41,446  39,064\par Real GDP (in 2003 prices) (%)                                                     114.0   122.7   132.6   140.1   129.1\par Real GDP growth/(decline) (%, period-on-period)                                     6.4     7.7     8.1     5.6   (7.9)\par Nominal GDP per capita (roubles)                                                151,106 188,813 234,023 291,959 275,272\par Real GDP per capita (in 2003 prices) (%)                                          115.2   124.5   134.9   142.7   131.5\par Real GDP per capita growth/(decline) (%, period-on-period)                          6.9     8.1     8.3     5.7   (7.9)\par GDP deflator (%, period-on-period)                                                 19.2    15.5    13.8    18.0     2.7\par Memo:\par \par Total population, millions (end of period)                                 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2       142.7   142.2     142   141.9   141.9\par -------------------------\par \par Note:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data due to regular revisions by Rosstat. The nominal GDP figures in this table are current as of 31 March 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Rosstat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Between 2005 and 2009, real GDP grew by 20.5%. Russia experienced\par significant economic growth from 2005 through the first three quarters of\par 2008. Real GDP growth from 2005 through 2008 amounted to 30.8%, including 6.4%\par growth in 2005, 7.7% growth in 2006 and 8.1% growth in 2007. Despite\par substantial growth in the first nine months of 2008 (9.3%, 7.7% and 6.6% in\par the first, second and third quarters, respectively, against the relevant\par periods in the previous year), the effects of the global financial crisis\par contributed to a slowdown in overall growth in 2008 (5.6%), the slowest rate\par of growth recorded since 2002. As a result of the global financial crisis, the\par economy contracted by 7.9% in 2009 compared to 2008.\par \par Economic growth between 2005 and 2008 resulted mainly from improved\par labour productivity due to substantial increases in investment and improved\par management (average annual productivity growth from 2005 to 2008 was 6.2%) and\par from a rapid expansion in private consumption. The surge in investments and\par private consumption was, in turn, fuelled by high commodity prices and a\par significant expansion in credit. Overall consumption as a percentage of GDP\par remained roughly constant during this period because of relatively flat growth\par in public consumption, itself caused by strong fiscal discipline on the part\par of the Government.\par \par Strong real appreciation of the rouble and production growth\par contributed to an aggregate increase in the dollar value of Russia's GDP\par between 2005 and 2008. From 1999 through 2008, Russia registered a compounded\par annualised GDP growth rate of 6%. In 2008, based on purchasing power parity,\par Russia's economy was the world's sixth largest.\par \par Russia experienced particularly adverse economic and financial\par effects as a result of the global economic crisis. In the fourth quarter of\par 2008, due to a decline in gross investments and net exports and despite a\par nominal increase in both private and public consumption, real GDP growth\par dropped sharply to 1.2% relative to the fourth quarter of 2007. In 2009, real\par GDP decreased by 7.9% compared to 2008, primarily because of a contraction in\par private-sector consumption and gross investments. The decline in real GDP in\par 2009 was slightly offset by a rise in net exports that was, in turn, caused by\par a disproportionate drop in imports, a result of falling domestic demand and\par depreciation of the rouble (the average rouble rate fell by 27.4% in 2009\par compared to 2008). The rouble depreciation as well as the overall decline in\par the Russian economy led to a decrease in the dollar value of Russia's GDP in\par 2009 relative to 2008.\par \par GDP by Output\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table illustrates Russia's GDP by output for the years 2005 to 2009:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Gross Domestic Product by Output(1)\par \par                                                2005             2006             2007             2008             2009\par \par                                                   %                %                %                %                \par                                    (%)(2) change(3) (%)(2) change(3) (%)(2)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 change(3) (%)(2) change(3) (%)(2) change(3)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GDP total.............................100 6.4 100 7.7 100\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2        8.1    100       5.6    100     (7.9)\par Agriculture(4)........................5.1       1.1    4.7       3.8    4.2       2.5    4.2       7.2    4.5       0.3\par Fishery...............................0.3       2.8    0.3       4.4    0.2     (0.1)    0.2       6.4    0.2       1.5\par Mining(5)............................11.0       0.5   10.7     (3.3)   10.1     (2.8)    9.3       1.3    9.1     (3.1)\par Manufacturing........................18.6       6.0   18.1       7.3   17.6       7.9   17.4     (0.7)   15.0    (15.3)\par Electricity, water and gas production and\par distribution..........................3.3       1.2    3.2       5.7    3.0       0.1    2.9       0.5    3.2     (7.9)\par Construction..........................5.3      10.5    5.2      11.8    5.7      13.7    6.3      11.2    5.5    (17.0)\par Trade(6).............................19.3       9.4   20.1      14.1   20.3      12.1   20.9       9.6   19.7     (8.6)\par Hotels and restaurants............... 0.9       9.7    0.9       8.5    1.0      14.8    1.0       9.6    1.0    (16.3)\par Transportation and communications....10.1       6.2    9.7       9.7    9.7       5.4    9.1       6.0    9.5     (3.0)\par Financial services..................  4.0      11.9    4.5      10.3    4.4      12.5    4.4      14.1    5.0     (2.4)\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with real estate, \par including rent and\par services............................. 9.7      12.5    9.8      10.2   10.9      19.8   11.2      11.7   12.6     (3.4)\par Public administration, defence and \par mandatory social\par security............................  5.1     (3.1)    5.1       2.6    5.1      13.9    4.6       2.9    6.1       2.1\par Education.............................2.6       0.4    2.6       0.5    2.7       1.2    2.4     (0.1)    3.1     (1.5)\par Public health and social services.... 3.0       1.7    3.3       1.5    3.3       1.2    2.9     (0.2)    3.8       0.4\par Communal and other services.......... 1.7       2.4    1.8       8.7    1.8\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2       8.0    1.5     (2.6)    1.7    (18.7)\par \par -------------------------\par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data due to regular revisions by Rosstat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (2) Sectoral contributions are calculated pursuant to gross added\par value, which does not take into account taxes on products, but does take into\par account subsidies on products. For 2005 and 2006, gross added value also did\par not include indirectly measured financial intermediation services. For this\par reason, sectoral contributions based on gross added value do not total 100%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of\par GDP.\par \par (3) In constant prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Includes hunting and forestry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (5) Includes extraction of oil, gas, coal, metal ores and other minerals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (6) Includes wholesale and retail trade and repairs of motor vehicles and personal and household goods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Rosstat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par From 2005 through 2009, the major trend in the composition of GDP\par by output has been an increase in the contribution of real estate and a\par decrease in the share of the mining and manufacturing sectors. As a share of\par GDP, real estate rose from 9.7% of GDP in 2005 to 12.6% of GDP in 2009. Mining\par decreased from 11.0% of GDP in 2005 to 9.1% of GDP in 2009, and manufacturing\par declined from 18.6% of GDP in 2005 to 15.0% of GDP in 2009. Construction,\par hotels and restaurants, financial services, public administration, education\par and public health also increased their contribution to overall GDP during this\par period.\par \par These shifts in the composition of Russia's GDP resulted from\par changes in performance in real terms and from changes in relative prices.\par Between 2005 and 2009, the fastest rate of growth among Russia's large sectors\par was recorded in real estate (60.3%), financial services (54.6%), trade\par (40.2%), construction (29.6%) and transportation and communications (26.3%),\par all of which exceeded the GDP growth rate during this period of 20.5%. Buoyant\par economic growth and the real appreciation of the rouble between 2005 and 2008\par were the principal reasons for the improvement in these sectors. From 2005\par through 2009, growth was recorded in all sectors except for mining, i.e.,\par extraction, which experienced the largest decline\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (7.3%), communal and other services (4.8%) and the production and distribution of electricity, water and gas (0.9%). Extraction declined, in part, due to a slowdown in oil and gas production in 2006 and 2007 and to lower demand for Russian commodities as a result of the global financial crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Of the country's largest sectors, construction, manufacturing,\par trade, real estate, mining and transport and communications, were the worst\par affected by the global financial crisis in 2009. Compared to 2008,\par construction declined by 17.0%, manufacturing by 15.3%, trade by 8.6%, real\par estate by 3.4%, mining by 3.1% and transport and communications by 3.0%. Every\par industry with the exception of agriculture, fishery, public administration and\par public health and social services experienced a decline in 2009 compared to\par 2008.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GDP by Use\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The following table illustrates Russia's GDP by use for the years\par 2005 to 2009:\par \par                        Gross Domestic Product by Use(1)\par \par                                       2005      %     2006      %      2007      %      2008      %      2009      %\par                                        (%)  change(2)  (%)  change(2)  (%)   change(2)  (%)   change(2)  (%)   change(2)\par \par GDP                                     100       6.4   100       7.7    100       8.1    100       5.6    100     (7.9)\par Consumption                            66.1       8.8  65.7       9.0   66.7      11.1   65.7       8.6   74.1     (5.1)\par Private                                49.0      11.8  48.0      11.4   48.7      13.9   48.0      10.8   53.6     (7.7)\par Public                                 16.6       1.3  17.2       2.4   17.5       3.7   17.2       2.9   20.0       2.0\par Non-profit institutions                 0.5    (18.6)   0.5     (1.8)    0.5       2.4    0.5     (1.4)    0.5     (1.4)\par Gross investment                       20.2       9.6  21.5      18.5   24.5      21.0   25.2      10.5   18.6    (37.4)\par Fixed assets                           17.8      10.6  18.6      18.0   21.3      21.1   22.0      10.5   21.3    (15.7)\par Inventory accumulation                  2.4       1.5   2.9      22.9    3.2      20.3    3.2      11.5  (2.7)        --\par Net exports                            13.7    (11.9)  12.8    (14.7)    8.8    (27.2)    9.1    (35.5)    7.3      56.8\par Exports                                35.2       6.5  33.9       7.3   30.5       6.3   30.9       0.6   27.5     (4.7)\par Imports                                21.5      16.6  21.1      21.3   21.7      26.6   21.8      15.2   20.2    (30.4)\par Statistical discrepancy                 0.0        --   0.4        --    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1.5 -- (0.4) -- 0.1 --\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -------------------------\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data due to regular revisions by Rosstat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) In constant prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Rosstat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The composition of GDP by use was affected by the overall condition\par of the Russian economy--from its growth between 2005 and 2008, on the back of\par high commodity prices, to its contraction, particularly in 2009, as a result\par of the global financial crisis. As a percentage of GDP, domestic demand (i.e.,\par consumption and gross investment) increased from 86.3% in 2005 to 92.7% in\par 2009 and net exports decreased from 13.7% in 2005 to 7.3% in 2009.\par \par Domestic consumption's contribution to GDP decreased slightly in\par 2006 compared to 2005, increased slightly in 2007 compared to 2006 and\par decreased again in 2008, until expanding by 13.7% in 2009 compared to 2008.\par Between 2005 and 2009, overall private consumption rose in real terms by\par 45.1%, outpacing the overall growth rate of GDP during the period, whereas\par public consumption increased in real terms by only 12.9%, which reflected the\par country's relatively conservative fiscal policy during the period. Between\par 2005 and 2008, private consumption increased in real terms by 57.2% due to the\par overall strength of the Russian economy during that period, including high oil\par prices and a rapid expansion of available credit. However, as a result of the\par global financial crisis and its negative effects on Russian consumers, private\par consumption contracted by 7.7% in 2009 compared to 2008. The contribution to\par GDP made by private consumption decreased from 49.0% of GDP in 2005 to 48.0%\par in 2008 before increasing to 53.6% of GDP in 2009. The contribution to GDP\par made by public consumption increased slightly between 2005 and 2008 (16.6% of\par GDP in 2005 compared to 17.2% of GDP in 2008), and then significantly more so\par in 2009, when its share rose by nearly 17% compared to 2008. The rise in the\par share of public consumption as a percentage of GDP in 2009 was mainly a\par function of the Government's fiscal stimulus package.\par \par Between 2005 and 2009, gross investment grew by 8.7% in real terms.\par The 8.7% growth represents a significant slowdown from the 73.7% increase in\par gross investments that occurred between 2005 and 2008. This deterioration in\par growth was primarily the result of the 37.4% contraction in gross investments\par in 2009\par \par compared to 2008, which was caused by the adverse effects of the\par global financial crisis on the Russian economy. Between 2005 and 2009,\par investments in fixed assets grew by 47.7% in real terms, which includes an\par 15.7% contraction in 2009 compared to 2008. The contribution of fixed asset\par investments to GDP increased from 17.8% in 2005 to 22.0% in 2008 before\par declining to 21.3% in 2009. Inventory accumulation increased by 67.3% in real\par terms between 2005 and 2008, only to experience a depletion of inventories in\par 2009 compared to 2008.\par \par Between 2005 and 2009, real growth of exports grew at a slower pace\par than overall GDP, while imports accelerated at a faster rate than GDP. During\par this period, the real value of exports grew by 16.5%, and the real value of\par imports grew by 43.6%. Exports as a share of GDP, however, contracted between\par 2005 and 2009. From 2005 through 2008, this contraction (from 35.2% of GDP in\par 2005 to 30.9% of GDP in 2008) was due to the rapid expansion of domestic\par demand, partially reflected in the rise of gross investments, and was\par supported by the real appreciation of the rouble. Exports declined in 2009 by\par 4.7% compared to 2008 and, as a share of GDP, from 30.9% in 2008 to 27.5% in\par 2009, due mainly to lower commodity prices and reduced global demand for\par Russian exports. The high growth in imports between 2005 and 2008 was a\par result, generally, of high rates of economic growth in Russia during this\par period, and, in particular, of rising consumer incomes. Imports as a share of\par GDP remained generally constant between 2005 and 2008 (21.5%, 21.1%, 21.7% and\par 21.8% of GDP in 2005, 2006, 2007 and 2008, respectively). The contraction in\par imports in 2009 compared to 2008 (30.4%) as well as the decline in imports as\par a share of GDP (from 21.8% in 2008 to 20.2% in 2009) was mainly due to the\par negative effects of the global financial crisis on the Russian economy. Net\par exports decreased in real terms by 44.7% between 2005 and 2009 and decreased\par as a share of GDP from 13.7% of GDP to 7.3% of GDP.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Principal Sectors of the Economy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industry\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia is highly industrialised, with a large share of its\par industrial activity concentrated in heavy industry. Russia is the world leader\par in oil extraction, ranks second in the world in natural gas extraction and\par produces significant amounts of electricity, iron, steel, rolled products,\par mineral fertilisers and coal. Manufacturing activity is heavily concentrated\par in machine building, including power equipment, transportation vehicles, oil\par and mining equipment and food processing.\par \par The following table illustrates the structure of industrial output\par and period-on-period changes in real industrial output by sector for the years\par 2005 to 2009:\par \par                          Industrial Output by Sector\par \par                                          2005              2006             2007             2008           2009(1)\par                                                                 %                %                %                %\par                                      %   % change(2)   %    change(2)   %    change(2)   %    change(2)   %    change(2)\par \par Total Industrial Output            100.0         5.1  100.0       6.3  100.0       6.3  100.0       2.1  100.0    (10.8)\par Extraction                          22.5         1.4   21.8       2.5   21.8       1.9   21.3       0.2   23.0     (1.2)\par Of which\par Fuels                               19.7         2.0   19.3       2.5   19.3       1.9   18.8       0.2   20.5     (0.6)\par Other minerals                       2.7         2.3    2.5       2.5    2.4       1.6    2.5       1.5    2.4     (6.2)\par Manufacturing                       65.1         7.6   65.5       8.3   67.8       9.5   68.2       3.2   63.4    (16.0)\par Of which\par Foods                               11.0         6.6   10.1       7.0   10.3       6.1   10.7       1.1   12.9     (0.5)\par Paper products                       2.3         3.6    2.3       7.0    2.4       9.1    2.2       0.8    2.4    (12.5)\par Coke and petrochemicals             10.5         4.4   11.7       7.1   11.0       2.9   12.1       2.7   10.5     (0.6)\par Chemicals                            5.3         4.1    4.5       4.8    4.6       6.0    5.3     (4.2)    4.8     (5.4)\par Metallurgy                          14.0         7.0   14.1       9.8   14.3       2.1   13.3     (0.2)   10.9    (13.9)\par Machines and equipment               3.5       (0.3)    3.6       9.4    3.9      19.1    4.0       4.0    3.4    (28.4)\par Electrical equipment                 3.3        33.2    3.5      16.3    4.0      11.8    3.7     (7.9)    3.8    (31.6)\par Transport vehicles and equipment     6.1         7.1    6.1       3.9    6.2      15.3    6.1       9.5    5.1    (38.0)\par Textiles and clothing                0.7         3.6    0.7      12.1    0.6       1.3    0.6       4.5    0.6    (15.8)\par Leather and footwear                 0.1         0.2    0.1      22.2    0.1       0.0    0.1       1.7    0.1     (2.0)\par Wood products                        1.1         7.1    0.1       3.6    1.1       6.2    0.1       1.4    0.9    (17.7)\par Rubber and plastics                  1.5        16.4    1.5      21.7    1.7      22.1    1.6      12.5    1.6    (12.2)\par Other non-metal mineral products     3.1         4.9    3.3      15.7    4.1      10.3    4.1       0.9    3.1    (24.1)\par Other                                1.6         8.7    1.6      12.2    1.8       5.1    1.8       4.6    1.3    (17.8)\par Electricity, water and gas\par production and distribution         12.4         0.9   12.6       4.9   10.4     (0.2)   10.4       1.4   13.5     (4.8)\par \par -------------------------\par \par Notes:\par \par (1) Preliminary data.\par \par (2) In constant prices.\par \par Source: Rosstat.\par \par Industrial output grew in each year between 2005 and 2008, with\par overall real output growth equalling 21.3% over the period. This increase was\par driven mainly by growth in manufacturing, which, in turn, was the result of\par improved competitiveness of Russian products in both domestic and\par international markets, improved management, growth in private investment and\par strong global commodity prices. Growth in industrial production decelerated in\par 2008 because of lower commodity prices and the onset of the global financial\par crisis, which led to a contraction in industrial output in the fourth quarter\par of the year (a contraction of 6.1% compared to the fourth quarter of 2007) and\par reduced investment.\par \par Between 2005 and 2008, growth was recorded in all major industrial\par sectors. The greatest growth over the period was recorded in electrical\par equipment (59.5%), transport vehicles and equipment (40.5%) and machines and\par equipment (35.1%), as these industries benefited from high levels of\par investment and external demand. At 0.2%, growth in the extraction industry\par remained flat during this period as a result of production bottlenecks.\par Electricity, water and gas production and distribution also exhibited a weak\par growth rate (1%) between 2005 and 2008, reflecting government-mandated price\par caps.\par \par In 2008, Russia's total industrial output decelerated, increasing\par by 2.1% in real terms compared to 2007. Overall annual growth was recorded in\par most industries, including rubber and plastics (16.4%), transport vehicles and\par equipment (9.5%), textiles and clothing (4.6%), machines and equipment (4.0%)\par and coke and petrochemicals (2.7%). Output contracted in 2008 in electrical\par equipment (7.9%), chemicals (4.2%) and metallurgy (0.2%). Overall industrial\par growth decelerated in 2008 due to the global economic crisis, which began to\par have a significant negative impact on Russia's real economy in the fourth\par quarter of 2008. While industrial output grew in each of the first three\par quarters of 2008 (6.2%, 5.5% and 4.7%, respectively), output contracted in the\par fourth quarter by 6.1%.\par \par In 2009, Russia's total industrial output decreased by 10.8% in\par real terms compared to 2008. Industrial output contracted in all major\par industries, with transport vehicles and equipment (38.0%), electrical\par equipment (31.6%) and machinery (28.4%) experiencing the sharpest declines.\par The overall contraction was due to the effects of the global financial crisis,\par including a substantial decline in the overall demand for industrial products\par and a decrease in global commodity prices.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Energy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2009, energy (including oil, petroleum products, natural gas, coal and electricity) accounted for approximately 79.5% of Russia's exports. In the aggregate, approximately 22.9% of the oil, natural gas, coal and electric power produced by Russia was exported in 2009. In 2009, oil and petroleum products accounted for 49.3% of total exports, while natural gas accounted for 13.9%, coal for 2.4% and electricity approximately 0.3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Domestic energy prices were heavily subsidised in the Soviet Union\par (amounting to only 10% to 20% of world market prices in 1992). By 1996,\par however, domestic energy prices had risen relative to other prices as a result\par of export liberalisation and the real appreciation of the rouble against\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The devaluation of the rouble in 1998 led to a sharp fall\par in domestic energy prices in dollar terms. Despite a recovery in prices in\par subsequent years, domestic prices for gas, electricity and crude oil remain\par lower than international prices. Domestic prices for petroleum products,\par however, are more similar to international prices after deducting export\par duties and transportation costs. Federal budget revenues and Russia's balance\par of payments are affected to a significant extent by world prices for oil and\par gas. See "Balance of Payments and Foreign Trade" and "Public Finance."\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil and Natural Gas\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russian methodologies for calculating oil and gas reserves and\par Russian reserves classifications differ from standard international\par methodologies and classifications, in particular with respect to the manner\par and extent to which commercial factors affecting exploration, development and\par production are taken into account. Generally, Russian methodologies classify\par oil and gas deposits as reserves if such deposits are technically recoverable,\par even if the recovery of a portion of such reserves, using currently available\par technology, is uneconomic. In contrast, most international methodologies\par classify oil and gas deposits as reserves only if such deposits are\par economically extractable on the basis of existing technologies, prices and\par costs. Accordingly, the gas reserves information contained in this Prospectus\par is not comparable, and cannot be made comparable, to gas reserves information\par provided on the basis of standard international methodologies and\par classifications. The gas reserves information contained in this Prospectus\par represents total "explored" reserves, consisting of gas reserves in categories\par A, B and C1 of the Russian classification system.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Oil output declined significantly following the dissolution of the\par Soviet Union but has been growing in recent years. Output in 2009 was 478\par million tonnes (approximately 13% of world output), an increase of 1.4% over\par production in 2008 and 1.0% over production in 2007. Output in 2009 was 14.4%\par lower than the peak output of 559 million tonnes recorded in 1987. Between\par 2005 and 2009, overall oil output increased by 5.6% (from 470.2 million tonnes\par in 2005 to 494.2 million tonnes in 2009). In 2008 and 2009, oil exports to\par non-CIS countries were 204.9 million tonnes and 211.9 million tonnes,\par respectively, while exports to the CIS were 38.2 million tonnes and 36.5\par million tonnes, respectively.\par \par Beginning in 1993, the state oil industry was split into several\par holding companies and operating subsidiaries. This resulted in the creation of\par a number of large vertically integrated oil groups, with each holding company\par managing and controlling a stake in many operating subsidiaries. The\par Government privatised its shareholdings in Slavneft (75%) and Lukoil (13.5%)\par between 2002 and 2003. In December 2004, a 76.79% stake in Yuganskneftegaz ,\par the main production subsidiary of what was then Russia's largest oil company,\par Yukos, was sold at auction for U.S.$9.35 billion, in partial settlement of\par Yukos' tax obligations, to Baikalfinancegroup, which was in turn acquired by\par Rosneft, then a wholly state-owned entity. In July 2006, the Government\par privatised approximately 15% of its shareholdings in Rosneft as part of the\par company's initial public offering.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2005, three Yukos shareholders commenced three related arbitrations under the Energy Charter Treaty, seeking in the aggregate approximately U.S. $33.1 billion as compensation for the Russian Federation's alleged breach of its treaty obligations. Other Yukos-related claims have also been asserted\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 before the European Court of Human Rights and under the Russian Federation's bilateral investment treaties with Spain and the United Kingdom.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Natural Gas\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia's A, B and C1 natural gas reserves were estimated at 55.2\par trillion cubic metres as of January 2009. Natural gas output stabilised after\par declining immediately following the dissolution of the Soviet Union. Between\par 2005 and 2008, natural gas output increased by 3.6% (from 641 billion cubic\par metres in 2005 to 664 billion cubic metres in 2008). Then, in 2009, output\par decreased to 582.4 billion cubic metres, or by 12.3% compared to 2008. In 2008\par and 2009, natural gas exports to non-CIS countries were approximately 158.4\par billion cubic metres and 119.5 billion cubic metres, respectively, while\par exports to the CIS were 37 billion cubic metres and 47.5 billion cubic metres,\par respectively.\par \par Russia's natural gas industry is dominated by Gazprom , which has an\par effective monopoly on gas transmission, storage and export, and accounts for\par most of Russia's natural gas reserves and production. Approximately 38.4% of\par Gazprom 's shares are owned directly by the Russian Government, although\par through its ownership of other Gazprom  shareholders, the Government controls a\par majority stake in the company. Gazprom  holds production licences with respect\par to approximately 70% of Russia's natural gas reserves, accounted for 83% of\par Russia's natural gas production in 2008 and owns and operates the Unified Gas\par Supply System, which includes approximately 160,000 kilometres of high\par pressure pipelines.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coal\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia had approximately 190 billion tonnes of coal reserves as at\par 1 January 2009. After the dissolution of the Soviet Union, coal production was\par negatively affected by a decrease in overall demand, a reduction in budgetary\par subsidies and under-investment. As a result, many mines are today\par inefficient. Russia adopted a four-stage rationalisation plan, supported by\par World Bank lending, for the restructuring of the coal industry and the gradual\par closure of inefficient and subsidised mines. These measures, together with\par growing demand, resulted in a 10.1% growth of coal production between 2005 and\par 2008. In 2009, coal production returned to 2005 levels. Approximately 300.6\par million tonnes of coal were produced in Russia in 2009, a decrease of\par approximately 8.6% compared to 2008. In 2009, coal exports amounted to\par approximately 98.1 million tonnes, a slight increase compared to 2008, with\par the greatest volumes sold to customers the United Kingdom, China, Turkey,\par Poland and Japan.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Electricity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's electricity output was 992 billion kWh in 2009, representing an increase of 6.5% compared to 2005 and a decrease of 4.6% compared with 2008. Of the total output in 2009, thermal power plants accounted for 652 billion kWh (65.7%), hydrogenation for 176 billion kWh (17.7%) and nuclear generation for 164 billion kWh (16.5%). As at 1 January 2009, total Russian generating capacity was 225 GW, approximately 68.4% of which was attributable to thermal power plants, 21% to hydro power plants and 10.6% to nuclear power plants. Electricity is exported primarily to CIS countries, China, Finland, Latvia, Lithuania and Norway.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the collapse of the Soviet Union, in 1992, the Russian electricity industry was initially restructured and split into RAO Unified Energy Systems ("UES"), 73 regional energy companies (energos), and a separate nuclear power industry operated by State-owned Rosenergoatom (re-named the Energoatom Concern in 2008).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until the restructuring of UES, described below, UES was the largest producer of electricity in Russia. At year-end 2007, UES owned more than 72.2% of Russia's generating capacity and was the monopoly high voltage distributor and wholesale purchaser and seller of electricity. Until its sell-off, the Russian Government owned approximately 53% of UES.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In recent years, the Russian electricity industry has undergone a\par major restructuring, including the break-up of almost all\par vertically-integrated power companies controlled by UES and the formation of\par new mono-profile companies with the following activity types: generation,\par transmission, distribution, retail sales and repairs and servicing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par The restructuring process culminated on 1 July 2008 in the de-merging of UES\par into more than 20 independent companies each engaged in either the competitive\par sector (power generation) or the non-competitive sector (transmission and\par distribution).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The electricity generation sector is currently principally comprised of thermal power plants, hydro power plants, nuclear generation complexes and other power units, including renewable energy plants.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The thermal power plants, which are powered by fossil fuels, are\par owned primarily by OGKs (wholesale generating companies, of which there are\par six), TGKs (regional generating companies, of which there are 14) and several\par other energy companies, such as OJSC Bashkirenergo  and OJSC Irkutskenergo . The\par OGKs are the largest generators in the wholesale electricity (capacity) market\par except for RusHydro  and Mosenergo . Each OGK owns plants in different regions\par in order to minimise competition abuse. With the exception of Mosenergo , the\par TGKs are smaller than the OGKs (as measured by capacity and output) and\par operate principally on a regional level.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 State-controlled RusHydro owns more than 50% of the installed capacity of hydro power plants, with the remainder owned by several TGKs and other regional energy companies. Energoatom Concern , a wholly State-owned company, operates all of Russia's nuclear plants.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's transmission network of high-voltage power lines is operated by the Federal Grid Company, which is 79.11% owned by the State. Most of the country's medium- and low-voltage distribution networks are consolidated into 11 Inter-Regional Distribution Companies, which are indirectly controlled by the Russian Federation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retail electricity sales are made by the electricity companies that were spun-off from the energos and sold by UES in public auctions and other independent electricity retail companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2004, all dispatching functions of the Russian electricity sector were transferred from the energos to the System Operator of the Unified Energy System, which is 100% State-owned.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian electricity market is divided into a wholesale\par electricity market, a capacity market and a retail market. On 1 September\par 2006, new rules governing the wholesale electricity market took effect,\par pending full liberalisation of the sector, which is anticipated to occur in\par 2011. Under these rules, trading in electricity is conducted on the basis of\par the following mechanisms: regulated contracts; unregulated bilateral\par contracts; the "one-day-ahead" market; and the balancing market. Regulated\par contracts are effectively take-or-pay obligations at a regulated tariff for\par electricity and capacity volumes. Each generator (except those facilities\par commissioned after 2007) is required to enter into regulated contracts\par covering a certain percentage of its planned annual output. This percentage,\par which is reduced semi-annually (and is expected to continue decreasing until\par full liberalisation), is 35-40% as of 1 January 2010. The volumes of planned\par generation that are not traded under regulated contracts may be sold pursuant\par to unregulated bilateral contracts or traded on the one-day-ahead market or\par balancing market. The one-day-ahead market is effectively a spot market for\par those who wish to trade electricity at unregulated prices. Participants on the\par one-day-ahead market submit bids for the purchase and sale of electricity for\par a certain volume and price for each hour of the following day. The balancing\par market was established as a real-time platform to allow the trading of\par electricity in order to cover deviations on the one-day-ahead market between\par planned power volumes and volumes actually generated or consumed.\par \par Since 1 September 2006, electricity capacity has been sold\par separately from electricity. Currently, capacity is sold on the wholesale\par market pursuant to both regulated and unregulated contracts. Beginning in\par 2008, there has been a gradual liberalisation of the capacity market similar\par to the liberalisation underway on the wholesale electricity market. From 1\par July to 31 December 2009, 45-50% of a generator's capacity had to be sold\par under regulated contracts. The remaining capacity (and any capacity from power\par plants commissioned in 2008 or later) could be sold at unregulated prices.\par From 1 January 2010 to 30 June 2010, 35-40% of a generator's capacity must be\par sold under regulated contracts, with the remaining capacity available for sale\par at unregulated prices. From 1 July 2010 to 31 December 2010, it is expected\par that 15-20% of capacity must be sold under regulated contracts. Full\par liberalisation of the capacity market is expected to occur in 2011.\par \par New rules governing the retail electricity market also came into\par effect on 1 September 2006. Purchasers on the retail market include (i) large\par industrial consumers, which pay for electricity supplies pursuant to a formula\par that takes into account the cost of electricity in the competitive wholesale\par electricity market; and (ii) households, which until at least 2011, pay\par according to below-market tariffs set by a state regulator. Therefore, until\par full liberalisation of the retail market, which is expected to occur in\par conjunction with deregulation of the wholesale market, industrial consumers\par will continue to, in effect, subsidise retail consumers.\par \par In August 2009, an accident occurred at the Sayano-Shushenskaya\par Hydroelectric Power Plant, the largest hydro power plant in Russia in terms of\par installed capacity, when water from the Yenisei River flooded the turbine and\par transformer rooms at the power plant's dam, killing more than 70 people and\par causing billions of roubles in damage. As a result of the accident, the plant\par halted power production, which\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 led to severe power shortages for both residential and industrial consumers. Of the nine hydro units functioning before the accident, two currently are in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Agriculture\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's agricultural sector has faced significant challenges in recent years, as major structural changes have been implemented under difficult conditions. Since the dissolution of the Soviet Union, Government subsidies to the agricultural sector have decreased significantly, the state food purchasing monopoly has been replaced by a decentralised market system and the share of agricultural output purchased by the state has fallen steadily.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The agricultural sector consists primarily of large agricultural\par concerns (estimated to account for approximately 66% of arable land and 45.9%\par of agricultural output in monetary terms) and private plots, including both\par small private farms and individual farms (estimated to account for 34% of\par arable land and 54.1% of agricultural output in monetary terms). The Law on\par the Circulation of Agricultural Land, which came into effect in January 2003,\par created a unified market framework at the federal level for the purchase and\par sale of farmland. It also prohibits the non-agricultural use of farmland,\par regulates the amount of farmland any one owner may hold and prohibits foreign\par ownership of farmland.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Agricultural output grew by 15.7% between 2005 and 2009. While Russia still imports substantial amounts of food and agricultural products, foodstuffs and agricultural products amounted to 3.3% of total exports in 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Construction\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The construction sector accounted for approximately 6.3% of GDP in\par 2008 and 5.5% of GDP in 2009. Following a steep decline after the dissolution\par of the Soviet Union and until the onset of the global financial crisis, output\par in the construction industry grew significantly. Overall growth in\par construction between 2005 and 2009 amounted to 29.6%, compared to overall\par growth between 2005 and 2008 of 56.2%. The deceleration was caused by a 17.0%\par decline in construction sector output in 2009 compared to 2008. New housing\par construction amounted to 59.8 million square metres in 2009, compared to 64.1\par million square metres in 2008.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Transport and Communications\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia has a railway network, a large merchant fleet, a large\par number of airports, a developed system of municipal transport, a comprehensive\par road network, modern telecommunications infrastructure and a major space\par industry. The market for the provision of transport and communication services\par has been liberalised and restructured, particularly in air transport, water\par transport and communications. Despite a 3.0% decline in 2009 compared to 2008,\par the transportation and communications sector grew by 26.3% between 2005 and\par 2009. From 2005 through 2008, investment in transport as a share of total\par fixed investment in the Russian economy increased from 19.1% to 21.5%.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Railways\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par At year-end 2008, there were approximately 86,000 kilometres of\par railways in Russia for general passenger and freight transportation. Railways\par accounted for 85.0% of all freight transport in tonne-kilometres (excluding\par pipeline transport) and 33.7% of all passenger journeys in\par passenger-kilometres in 2009, making it the country's most important form of\par transport.\par \par Tariffs for passenger transport are currently subsidised from the\par railway freight profits of Russian Railways  and from the federal budget.\par Russian Railways , which is wholly-State owned and was founded in 2003, plays\par a significant role in the Russian railway sector. Among other activities, it\par is the owner and operator of the Russian Federation's integrated passenger and\par freight network and related infrastructure, owns and operates nearly all of\par the locomotives in Russia, is the largest Russian owner, operator and lessor\par of freight rolling stock, is the largest Russian freight rail operator and\par carries nearly all suburban and long-haul rail passengers. Substantial funding\par has been allocated to the renovation and maintenance of railway track and\par locomotive parks, as well as to the replacement of rolling stock.\par \par The Government has developed a multi-stage programme of\par structural reforms in the railway sector. Key aspects of the reforms include\par (i) increasing the safety of railway transport as well as the quality of\par services offered by the railway sector; (ii) meeting the increasing demand for\par railway transport; (iii) enhancing the overall efficiency of the railway\par sector. As part of these reforms, and in an effort to\par \par foster competition and increase private investment in the industry,\par control and management functions of the railway sector were separated,\par including through the establishment of Russian Railways , the spin-off of\par non-core assets of Russian Railways  and the creation of new independent\par operating companies. Currently, 65% of freight cars are under the control of\par such private operating companies. The Government has also taken steps toward\par reducing or partially eliminating cross-subsidies. The third stage of the\par reform programme, currently underway, envisages the privatisation of\par subsidiaries of Russian Railways , the creation of a Federal passenger company,\par approval of a market model for freight transport through 2015 and the\par transition to a new system of tariff regulation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Roads\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par There are approximately 1,212,883 kilometres of roads in Russia (as\par of 1 January 2009), 76% of which have hard surfaces. Some 86% of the\par hard-surface roads are in public use, over 60% of which are under regional or\par municipal ownership, 15% of which are under local ownership and some 6% of\par which are under federal control. 14%, or approximately 124,771 kilometres, of\par the hard-surface roads are not in public use, are controlled by legal entities\par or physical persons and generally are used to connect with the public road\par network. Roads falling under federal jurisdiction are renovated and maintained\par by means of the federal budget, while roads falling under the jurisdiction of\par Federation subjects are renovated and maintained by the Federation subjects in\par which they are located through the respective regional and local budget as\par well as transfers from the federal budget.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ports and Shipping\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2009, Russia had 64 seaports, approximately half of which\par serviced foreign trade and half of which serviced regional or local\par activities, and 705 freight complexes with a total capacity of approximately\par 603 million tonnes per year. In 2009, freight turnover through Russian\par seaports equalled 97.5 billion tonne-kilometres, representing 4.6% of\par Russia's total freight turnover (excluding pipeline transport). This\par represents an increase of 15.5% compared to the 84.6 billion\par tonne-kilometres of freight transported in 2008. In 2009, more than 77% of\par freight transported by sea from Russian ports was transported to international\par destinations, and approximately 82% of Russia's foreign trade freight was\par shipped through its seaports. In early 2006, a new federal law took effect\par that regulates the activities of seaports and intends to promote the growth of\par commercial transport by sea. Substantial resources are being invested to\par improve the competitiveness of Russian ports.\par \par Russia has approximately 102,000 kilometres of internal waterways\par with 119 ports for general use and 723 hydraulic installations, including 110\par locks as well as several pumping stations, pressure dikes and dams and canals.\par A fleet of approximately 2,500 technical vessels operate along these\par waterways. In 2009, freight turnover on Russia's internal waterways equalled\par approximately 52.6 billion tonne-kilometres, representing 2.5% of Russia's\par total freight turnover (excluding pipeline transport).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prior to 1991, each of Russia's commercial seaports was run by a shipping line, while ports were under central state control. Privatisation of commercial seaports began in the 1990s. Currently, sea transport is carried out by the Arctic, Far East, Murmansk, Novorossisk, Primorsk, Sakhalin and North shipping lines, as well as others. Transport on internal waterways is primarily carried out by private operators.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Air Transport\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As of 1 January 2010, Russia had 328 airports, 71 of which were\par international. Over the last five years, the number of Russian airports has\par decreased by 16.5%. The largest international airports are located in Moscow,\par St. Petersburg, Yekaterinburg and Novosibirsk. Air transport in 2009 accounted\par for approximately 24% of total passenger turnover in the country.\par \par Aeroflot was the monopoly carrier until 1991. Following the\par dissolution of the Soviet Union, Aeroflot was split into 140 regional airlines\par and an international carrier, Aeroflot-Russian International Airlines . Most\par of the regional airlines were subsequently privatised. In 1995,\par Aeroflot-Russian International Airlines  was partially privatised, although\par the state continues to own 51% of its shares. Currently, approximately 180\par airlines operate in Russia, the 10 largest of which account for roughly 80% of\par passenger turnover. The largest airlines are Aeroflot, with 19% of the market,\par Open Joint Stock Company "Airline Sibir/S7 Airlines ," with 12% market share,\par State Transport Company "Russia," with 9.7% market share, Open Joint Stock\par Company "Transaero ," with 7.1% market share and Open Joint Stock Company\par "YUTair," with 6.5% market share.\par \par Over the past several years, Sheremetyevo International Airport  has\par undergone a series of renovations. In March 2007, a new Terminal C opened,\par and, in November 2009, a new Terminal D opened. In February 2009, Joint Stock\par Company "International Airport Sheremetyevo" adopted a master plan for\par development of the airport through 2030. Pursuant to this plan, a new luggage\par sorting system was installed at the airport, and Terminal F (formerly known as\par Terminal 2) was expanded by 4,000 square metres. A new Terminal E is scheduled\par to open in the future with a planned transit capacity of seven million\par passengers per year. The master plan also envisages modernisation of Terminal\par B (formerly known as Terminal 1) and the opening of a new business aviation\par terminal (Terminal A). According to the plan, overall transit capacity of the\par airport is expected to reach 64 million passengers per year.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pipelines\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As of the end of 2008, Russia had 230,000 kilometres of trunk\par pipelines, consisting of 165,000 kilometres of natural gas pipelines,\par approximately 49,000 kilometres of oil pipelines and 16,000 kilometres of oil\par product pipelines. In 2009, a total of 2,246.2 billion tonne-kilometres of\par commodities were transported by pipeline, a decrease of 8.8% compared with the\par previous year.\par \par Pipelines from major oil- and gas-producing areas in Russia are\par generally connected to pipelines in the CIS and former Soviet bloc countries.\par A number of significant new pipeline projects are planned or under\par construction. Gazprom , together with international partners, has built new\par trunk pipelines (the "Yamal-Europe project") that extend nearly 6,000\par kilometres from the northern part of the Tyumen region of Russia through\par Belarus and Poland to Germany. Gazprom  is also participating in the Nord\par Stream pipeline construction, expected to extend 1,200 kilometres from Vyborg,\par Russia to Griefswald, Germany. Gazprom  has also constructed a trunk pipeline\par to Turkey (the "Blue Stream project") that crosses the Black Sea,\par significantly increasing Russia's gas export capacity. Initial gas flows\par through the undersea portion of this pipeline commenced in December 2002, with\par the first commercial delivery to Turkey in February 2003. The pipeline for the\par Blue Stream project has an annual projected capacity of 16 billion cubic\par metres. Gazprom  is considering supplying gas to Europe by means of another\par export route that would pass under the Black Sea to the coast of Bulgaria (the\par "South Stream project"). This pipeline is expected to have an annual capacity\par of approximately 31 billion cubic metres. A feasibility study is underway, and\par various routes through south and central Europe are being considered.\par \par New pipeline-related projects intended to increase the export\par capacity of the oil industry are also being implemented or planned in Europe\par and Asia, including the Baltic pipeline system, which connects West Siberia\par with a major new harbour at St. Petersburg. The project was completed in 2006\par and increased annual pipeline capacity to 65 million tonnes of crude oil.\par Other programmes currently under way include upgrading the Druzhba oil\par pipeline through Eastern Europe by constructing by-passes and extensions, and\par the construction of the East Siberia-Pacific Ocean pipeline, which will have a\par capacity of 80 million tonnes and will extend from Taishet to Primorski Krai,\par connecting West and East Siberia with the Asia-Pacific region. The first\par section of the East Siberia-Pacific pipeline came on line in December 2009.\par Work on the second, and final, phase began in January 2010.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A significant portion of Russia's existing oil pipeline network has been in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for at least 20 years. The gas pipeline network is also ageing. Repair and maintenance costs are accordingly high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The crude oil and oil product trunk pipeline networks are owned and\par managed by state-owned monopolies, Transneft  and Transnefteproduct ,\par respectively. Transneft 's pipeline capacity, including its export pipeline\par capacity, is allocated quarterly to oil producers, generally in proportion to\par the amount of oil produced and delivered to Transneft 's pipeline network in\par the prior quarter. Generally, a Russian oil company is given an allocation for\par export to non-CIS countries equal to approximately one-third of its total\par crude oil so produced and delivered to Transneft . The gas trunk pipeline\par network is owned and managed by Gazprom . Third-party access to the gas trunk\par pipeline network is determined generally on the basis of available spare\par transport capacity, the quality and technical parameters of the natural gas\par supplies, the availability of supplier input connections and customer output\par connections and customer demand.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Telecommunications\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of year-end 2009, the length of Russia's intercity telephone lines reached 56 million kilometres, an increase of 11.2% compared to 2008. Fixed-line density equalled 33.2 units per 100 persons. The volume of telephone services grew by 2.7% in 2009 compared to 2008. There are over 4,500 telephone operators on the local market. By the end of 2009, there were approximately 230.5 million mobile telephone subscribers,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 an 86.6% increase compared to 2005. Mobile phone services are available across the entirety of the Russian Federation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent years, in an effort to lower administrative barriers to entry as well as enhance customer choice, the market for intercity and international telephone services began to undergo a process of liberalisation. 20 licences have already been issued for the provision of intercity and international telephone services. Recipients of these licences include Rostelekom, Transtelekom, Interregional Transit Telekom , Vympelkom , ARKTEL, Sinterra, Comstar-OTS and others.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strategic Enterprises\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Decree of the President of the Russian Federation No. 1009\par dated 4 August 2004 established a list of strategic enterprises, which\par includes, as of 10 April 2010, 295 federal state unitary enterprises and 214\par joint stock companies that are strategically important to state defence and\par security or to the public morals, health or rights of the citizens of the\par Russian Federation. As of the date of this Prospectus, companies on this list\par include, among others, Russian Railways , Aeroflot and Transneft , which\par operates an extensive crude oil pipeline network in the country. Such\par companies, which may be privatised only upon their removal from the list by\par decision of the President, are subject to specific rules of corporate\par governance. For example, the Government may retain operating control over\par these companies through the exercise of a "golden share," and dilution of the\par Government's stake in such companies remains subject to approval by the\par President.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sochi Winter Olympics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The southern city of Sochi will host the XXII Olympic Winter Games\par in 2014, the first time that the Winter Olympics will be held in the Russian\par Federation. In preparation for the Games, the Government has allocated\par approximately 327 billion roubles in financing, nearly 200 billion roubles of\par which is expected to come from the federal budget. A substantial portion of\par the financing will be spent on improving the power, transportation and\par telecommunications infrastructure in and around the Sochi region and on\par building and modernising sports venues and hotels. The Government created the\par state corporation Olympstroi, which is overseeing preparations for the Games.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Environment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Following a 2005 Government restructuring, environmental protection\par in Russia has primarily been the responsibility of the Federal Service for\par Ecological, Technological and Atomic Supervision. Environmental regulations\par require enterprises to pay fees for emissions or discharges of most\par pollutants. These fees, which are low by international standards, may be used\par to fund investment to improve the environment. Russia's environmental\par protection programme has focused on replanting forests, constructing spent gas\par treatment plants, installing water recycling systems and constructing sewage\par purification plants. In 2008, the Government issued a plan on the\par socio-economic development of the country through 2020, a major component of\par which sets forth priority measures for protecting the environment, including\par tax incentives for enterprises using ecologically clean technology. Total\par public and private sector investment in environmental protection in Russia\par during 2008 amounted to 0.25% of GDP. Federal expenditure to protect the\par environment and to address the effects of environmental catastrophes and\par natural disasters in 2009 amounted to approximately 0.03% of GDP and 0.14% of\par total non-interest expenditure for the year.\par \par In November 2004, President Putin signed the Kyoto Protocol to the\par United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change, which became effective\par in February 2005. Under the terms of the Kyoto Protocol, Russia is to maintain\par its emissions of greenhouse gases at a level no higher than the level\par prevalent in 1990. Due to the decrease in heavy industry following the\par collapse of the Soviet Union, Russia's current level of greenhouse emissions\par is approximately 34% less than its 1990 level.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Employment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Overall employment declined in Russia from the dissolution of the\par Soviet Union through 1999. Beginning in 2000 and until the onset of the global\par financial crisis in the second half of 2008, employment was increasing, in\par large part due to the rise in export volumes and import substitution that\par began in 1999. The rate of increase in employment was less than the rate of\par increase in real GDP, however, due to gains in labour productivity. Based on\par the methodology of the ILO, the average number of unemployed in 2005, 2006 and\par 2007 amounted to 5.6 million, or 7.6% of the economically active population,\par 5.3 million, or 7.2%\par \par of the economically active population, and 4.6 million, or 6.1% of\par the economically active population, respectively. The decline in unemployment\par levels in 2005-2007 was primarily due to strong domestic demand and a positive\par economic outlook. In late 2008, unemployment began to rise. In the fourth\par quarter of 2008, the average number of unemployed rose to 5.4 million, or 7.1%\par of the economically active population, from an average of 4.4 million, or 5.9%\par of the economically active population, in the third quarter of 2008.\par Unemployment continued to rise in 2009, with the average number of unemployed\par in 2009 amounting to 6.4 million, or 8.4% of the economically active\par population. The rise in unemployment in the second half of 2008 and in 2009\par was primarily the result of the global financial crisis, and, in particular,\par of a contraction both in domestic demand and in worldwide demand for Russian\par products.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth quarterly information regarding unemployment in Russia for the period from 2005 through 2009:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Unemployment (quarterly average)\par \par                                                                                Official (ILO)\par                                                                                  definition            Registered\par                                                                                 unemployed(1)         unemployed(2)\par                                                                                         % of                  % of\par                                                                                     economically          economically\par                                                                                        active                active\par                                                                            \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Million population Million population\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                                                        2005\par First Quarter                                                                   6.0          8.2%     2.0          2.7%\par Second Quarter                                                                  5.5          7.4%     1.9          2.6%\par Third Quarter                                                                   5.4          7.3%     1.8          2.4%\par Fourth Quarter                                                                  5.5          7.5%     1.8          2.4%\par                                                                        2006\par First Quarter                                                                   5.7          7.8%     1.9          2.6%\par Second Quarter                                                                  5.5          7.4%     1.8          2.4%\par Third Quarter                                                                   5.0          6.7%     1.7          2.3%\par Fourth Quarter                                                                  5.0          6.8%     1.7          2.3%\par                                                                        2007\par First Quarter                                                                   5.2          7.0%     1.7          2.3%\par Second Quarter                                                                  4.5          6.0%     1.6          2.1%\par Third Quarter                                                                   4.3          5.7%     1.4          1.9%\par Fourth Quarter                                                                  4.4          5.8%     1.5          2.0%\par                                                                        2008\par First Quarter                                                                   5.1          6.7%     1.6          2.1%\par Second Quarter                                                                  4.3          5.6%     1.4          1.9%\par Third Quarter                                                                   4.4          5.9%     1.3          1.7%\par Fourth Quarter                                                                  5.4          7.1%     1.4          1.8%\par                                                                        2009\par First Quarter                                                                   6.8          9.1%     2.0          2.6%\par Second Quarter                                                                  6.5          8.6%     2.2          2.9%\par Third Quarter                                                                   6.0          7.8%     2.1          2.7%\par Fourth Quarter                                                                  6.1          8.0%     2.1          2.8%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -------------------------\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (1) Numbers based on data from surveys carried out quarterly,\par except for data for the third and fourth quarters of 2009, which are based on\par surveys carried out monthly. Persons not working, looking for a job and ready\par to start work are counted as unemployed.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) Based on number of individuals who choose to register as unemployed with the various employment agencies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Rosstat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These figures do not take into account certain "hidden unemployment" resulting from shortened workdays and temporary lay-offs. The number of workers put on obligatory leave with no pay or partial pay amounted to approximately 397,000 and 944,000 in 2007 and 2008, respectively. The number of workers\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 required by their employers to work reduced hours was approximately 203,000 and 699,000 in 2007 and 2008, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a result of the various economic and social measures adopted in support of domestic industries, the number of idle workers and those working reduced hours began to decrease in the first three months of 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wages and Income\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Real wages increased by 12.6% in 2005, 13.3% in 2006 and 17.2% in\par 2007. In 2008, primarily due to higher inflation and the effects of the\par financial crisis on the labour market in the fourth quarter of 2008, the\par growth in real wages decelerated to 11.5%. In 2009, according to preliminary\par data, real wages declined by 2.8% (compared to 2008) primarily due to rising\par inflation and the negative impact of the global financial crisis on\par private-sector nominal wage levels. Overall, wages grew by 62.1% in real\par terms between 2005 and 2009.\par \par The minimum wage has remained relatively low, amounting to just\par 22.9% of the average monthly wage in January 2010. The minimum wage,\par established by the State Duma, serves as a benchmark in setting the level of\par federal benefits and also plays a role in determining certain types of budget\par expenditures and the size of unemployment benefits. The minimum wage was\par increased by 11% in September 2005 (to 800 roubles/month), by an additional\par 37.5% in May 2006 (to 1,100 roubles/month), by an additional 109% in September\par 2007 (to 2,300 roubles/month) and then once again in January 2009 by 88.3% (to\par 4,330 roubles/month). Beginning 1 September 2007, each Federation subject has\par discretion to set the minimum wage in its territory, provided it is not lower\par than the amount set by the Federal government.\par \par Approximately 17.7%, 15.2%, 13.3% and 13.4% of the population had\par income below the official subsistence level in 2005, 2006, 2007 and 2008,\par respectively. This decline was the result primarily of consistently high\par income growth. In the first nine months of 2009, however, the percentage of\par the population falling below the subsistence level increased slightly to 14.0%\par due mainly to the negative effects of the global financial crisis on the\par Russian economy, including the incomes of Russian citizens. Between 2005 and\par 2007, real disposable income increased by 43.0%. In 2008 and 2009, growth in\par real disposable income decelerated, increasing by 1.9% in 2008 compared to\par 2007 and 2.3% in 2009 compared to 2008, due mainly to the global financial\par crisis.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Organised labour does not play a prominent role in the Russian economy. Whereas nearly 100% of workers in the Soviet Union were unionised, approximately 50% of Russian workers are now members of labour organisations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Social Benefits and Expenditure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Social security reforms were introduced in Russia beginning in 1991, with new institutions founded to deliver social benefits, including unemployment insurance and benefits for the very poor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Total social expenditure (other than housing subsidies), based on the enlarged budget, amounted to 16.8% of GDP in 2005, 16.9% of GDP in 2006, 17.6% of GDP in 2007, 17.7% of GDP in 2008 and 21.3% of GDP in 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Most social expenditure is provided through state extra-budgetary\par funds or by sub-federal authorities. In 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009,\par 44.8%, 43.0%, 43.2%, 44.1% and 46.0% of social expenditure was provided by\par state extra-budgetary funds and 42.1%, 43.4%, 43.5%, 42.0% and 39.5% was\par provided by sub-federal authorities, respectively. Direct payments from the\par federal budget accounted for approximately 13.1%, 13.6%, 13.3%, 13.9% and\par 14.5% of social expenditure in 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively.\par The state extra-budgetary funds finance expenditure on health, certain\par social benefits and pensions. Sub-federal budgets finance housing and\par transportation subsidies, most education and health expenditures and certain\par social benefits, while the federal budget is responsible for a portion of the\par expenditure on education, health, culture and social benefits. See "Public\par Finance--Federal-Sub-Federal Fiscal Relations."\par \par In 2005, several benefit-related fiscal measures took effect.\par These measures eliminated unfunded benefit entitlements and replaced social\par privileges financed by the federal budget (e.g., free passenger transportation\par and free medical drugs) with targeted social benefits largely in the form of\par monthly cash compensation of between 50 roubles and 2,000 roubles, adjusted\par for inflation. This monetisation of federal\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 social benefits is available to the disabled, World War II veterans, survivors of the siege of Leningrad, Chernobyl cleanup workers, heroes of the Soviet Union and Russia and certain other groups.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Decisions related to the eligibility for and the provision of social benefits and compensation to other groups is delegated to regional authorities. Regional beneficiaries include labour veterans, victims of Soviet-era political repression, people with dependent children and students.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pension Reform\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Pension reform legislation was enacted in 2001 and a new social\par security system came into effect on 1 January 2002. Under this system, which\par remained in effect through 2009, a retiree's pension consisted of a basic\par pension, an insurance pension and a funded pension. Amendments to the pension\par legislation were passed in 2009, according to which a retiree's pension,\par effective 1 January 2010, consists of an insurance pension and a funded\par pension.\par \par The insurance pension is made up of two components: a fixed amount,\par which is set by law, and a variable amount, which is dependent on past\par contributions by the pensioner and length of employment. The fixed amount, in\par effect, replaces the pre-2010 basic pension. The basic pension was funded from\par the budget through payments of the Unified Social Tax (the "UST"). The UST was\par abolished at the end of 2009, and as a result both elements of the insurance\par pension come from the Pension Fund of the Russian Federation (the "Pension\par Fund").\par \par The funded pension is based on the amount of funds accumulated in a\par pensioner's individual pension account, which in turn depends on the\par beneficiary's year of birth. For those born before 1967, all mandatory\par contributions to the pension system are made to the insurance pension. For\par those born in 1967 or after, the share of contributions accumulated in funded\par pensions now must equal 6% of the first 415,000 roubles of the employee's\par salary. Regardless of age, an employee may elect to make funded pension\par contributions in excess of his mandatory pension contributions. For voluntary\par contributions between 2,000 roubles and 12,000 roubles per year, the\par Government will make a matching contribution, provided the employee files the\par appropriate application before 1 October 2013. Payments to pensioners from the\par funded pension will start in 2012.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Pension Law envisages that the size of pensions will be adjusted for inflation and growth in average salary levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Contributions to the funded pension system are collected by the\par Pension Fund and are managed by state and private trust companies and private\par pension funds. According to the Law On Investments for Financing the Funded\par Part of Pensions, enacted in 2002, individuals may choose once a year a\par private trust manager from those selected on the basis of a public tender or a\par private pension fund. The initial tender for private trust managers was held\par in September 2003, and 55 private trust companies were selected. Individuals\par were required to choose a trust manager by 31 December 2003, and funds were\par transferred to private trust companies in the beginning of 2004. As of 31\par December 2009, there were 58 private trust companies, and, currently, there\par are 133 private pension funds. Only a limited number of individuals choose\par private trust companies or private pension funds, leaving most pension savings\par to be managed by Vnesheconombank , which was appointed as the State Trust\par Management Company. As the State Trust Management Company, Vnesheconombank  is\par responsible for the management of funds held by individuals who selected\par Vnesheconombank  as their pension fund manager, as well as funds held by those\par citizens who did not select a trust company or private pension fund. Private\par trust companies and private pension funds may invest pension savings in\par various assets, including securities of Russian private and public issuers,\par rouble and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bank deposits and shares in foreign securities\par index funds. Vnesheconombank  may invest pension savings only in Russian state\par securities, rouble and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bank deposits and rouble and foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  accounts with credit organisations. See "Public Finance--State\par Extra-Budgetary Funds" for further discussion of the Pension Fund.\par \par                     BALANCE OF PAYMENTS AND FOREIGN TRADE\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Balance of Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth the balance of payments of the Russian Federation for the years 2005-2009:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par              Balance of Payments of the Russian Federation(1)(2)\par \par                             (billions of dollars)\par \par                                                                                 2005     2006     2007     2008     2009\par \par Current account                                                                 84.6     94.7     77.8    103.7     49.0\par Goods and services                                                             104.6    125.7    112.0    155.4     91.7\par Export                                                                         268.8    334.7    393.8    522.8    345.1\par Import                                                                       (164.2)  (209.0)  (281.6)  (367.3)  (253.4)\par Goods                                                                          118.4    139.3    130.9    179.7    111.6\par Export (fob)                                                                   243.8    303.6    354.4    471.6    303.4\par Import (fob)                                                                 (125.4)  (164.3)  (223.5)  (291.9)  (191.8)\par Services                                                                      (13.8)   (13.6)   (18.9)   (24.3)   (19.9)\par Export                                                                          25.0     31.1     39.3     51.2     41.7