5\par Cash foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                                           (1.4)      9.5     15.5   (30.0)      6.0\par Demand and time deposits                                                       (4.6)   (12.7)   (12.5)   (39.5)      6.4\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade credits and advances(3)                                                  (7.6)    (0.6)    (0.8)    (8.1)      6.8\par Loans extended (excl. arrears)                                                 (5.5)   (28.0)   (35.3)   (54.3)      7.5\par Arrears                                                                         11.0      3.1      8.8    (1.2)     10.0\par Indebtedness on supplies under intergovernmental agreements                      1.9     0.08    (0.3)     0.02    (1.8)\par Non-repatriation of export proceeds and import advances not repaid in\par time(4)                                                                       (27.2)   (19.9)   (34.5)   (39.3)   (26.6)\par Other assets                                                                    0.04    (0.8)    (1.0)    (5.2)    (0.9)\par Liabilities                                                                     42.4     30.3    139.3     62.9   (26.9)\par Cash domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                                           0.09      0.3      0.9      1.4      0.7\par Demand and time deposits                                                         2.3     14.6     11.6    (4.2)    (2.3)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Loans received (excl. arrears)                                             \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2     39.6     18.3    127.3     57.2   (33.0)\par Arrears                                                                         0.02    (3.6)    (1.3)      0.9    (0.3)\par Other liabilities                                                                0.3      0.7      0.9      1.3      8.1\par Reserve Assets(5)                                                             (61.5)  (107.5)  (148.9)     45.3    (3.4)\par Net errors and omissions                                                       (7.9)      9.5   (13.3)   (11.3)    (1.2)\par \par \par -------------------------\par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data due to regular revisions by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) At current rouble/dollar exchange rates. Precise information on the volume of Russia's foreign trade is difficult to obtain because of the importance of so-called "unregistered trade," consisting of commercial (in contrast to private) transactions that escape customs records or are undervalued by customs authorities. Value adjustments for unregistered trade are made in the official balance of payments accounts. These adjustments amounted to approximately U.S. $29 billion in 2005, U.S. $30 billion in 2006, U.S. $29 billion in 2007, U.S. $32 billion in 2008 and U.S. $20 billion in January- September 2009 (on a non-annualised basis).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) Includes received trade credits and advances in export and import of services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Includes the estimated value of the non-supply of goods and services under import contracts and the estimated value of fictitious transactions in securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (5) Changes in reserve assets that arise as a result of transactions with reserve assets, i.e., excluding monetisation of gold and valuation changes. This definition differs from that employed in the calculation of official international reserves. See "--Official International Reserves."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Source: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \par Current Account\par \par Since 2000, Russia has registered a current account surplus. In\par 2005, Russia recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$84.6 billion\par (approximately 11.1% of GDP), a 42.2% increase compared to 2004. The growth in\par the current account surplus was primarily due to an increase in the value of\par exported goods (which grew by 33.1% in 2005 compared to 2004), which, in turn,\par was supported by a 46% rise in the average price of certain important export\par commodities (including the average contract price for Urals oil) in 2005\par compared to 2004. The persistent rise in international oil prices was caused,\par in part, by political instability in oil producing regions and greater demand\par from east and south Asia. The value of imported goods increased by 28.8% in\par 2005 compared to 2004, largely as a result of the continued real appreciation\par of the rouble against the euro and the dollar and a general acceleration of\par economic activity. Notwithstanding the growth in the value of Russia's\par imported goods, Russia's 2005 trade surplus was U.S.$118.4 billion, a 38.0%\par increase compared to 2004. The negative balance of services increased by\par U.S.$0.8 billion in 2005 compared to 2004, amounting to U.S.$13.8 billion. The\par investment income and employee compensation deficit increased by approximately\par 47.7% from U.S.$12.8 billion in 2004 to U.S.$18.9 billion in 2005.\par \par In 2006, Russia recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$94.7\par billion (approximately 9.6% of GDP), an 11.9% increase compared to 2005. The\par growth in the current account surplus was primarily caused by a higher trade\par surplus, which was, in turn, caused largely by a 24.5% increase in the value\par of exported goods in 2006 compared to 2005. The increase in exports, including\par oil and natural gas, was primarily driven by price, not volume. For example,\par in 2006 compared to 2005, the volume of oil exports decreased by 1.6%, whereas\par the value of oil exports increased by 22.6%. Similarly, in 2006 compared to\par 2005, the volume of natural gas exports decreased by 3.1%, while the value of\par gas exports increased by 38.3%. In 2006 compared to 2005, the average export\par price for Urals oil and for natural gas grew by 24.8% and 43.0%, respectively.\par Growth in the average export prices of certain other key commodities,\par including copper (86.7%), nickel (67.6%) and aluminium (23.0%), underpinned\par export growth over the same period. The value of imported goods increased by\par 31.0% in 2006 compared to 2005, largely as a result of the real appreciation\par of the rouble against the euro and dollar and growth in the overall economy.\par Despite the growth in the value of Russia's imported goods, Russia's trade\par surplus was U.S.$139.3 billion, a 17.7% increase compared to 2005. The deficit\par in investment income increased by 42.1% from U.S.$17.8 billion in 2005 to\par U.S.$25.3 billion in 2006. The negative balance in employee compensation\par increased by over 280% from U.S.$1.1 billion to U.S.$4.2 billion.\par \par In 2007, Russia recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$77.8\par billion (approximately 6.0% of GDP), a 17.8% decrease compared to 2006. This\par decrease was primarily the result of a lower trade surplus, combined with a\par weakening balance of trade in services and an increase in outflows of current\par transfers. In 2007, imports of goods increased by 35.2% compared to 2006. This\par growth was caused by the real appreciation of the rouble (by 12.8% against the\par dollar and 4.2% against the euro) and by the expansion of domestic demand, as\par reflected by the 8.1% real GDP growth rate. The rise in the value of imported\par goods was partially offset by a 17.7% increase in the value of exported goods,\par which was primarily price- and not volume-driven. For example, Russia\par recorded growth in the average export price for Urals oil (14.1%), iron ore\par and concentrates (29.8%), coal (15.2%), nickel (40.8%) and aluminum (16.3%) in\par 2007 compared\par \par to 2006. At the same time, as measured in volumes, there was a\par deceleration in the growth of oil and iron ore exports and a contraction in\par exports of natural gas and ferrous metals. The resulting trade balance was\par U.S.$130.9 billion in 2007 compared to U.S.$139.3 billion in 2006. Also\par contributing to the decline in the current account surplus was an increase in\par both the negative balance of services (from U.S.$13.6 billion in 2006 to\par U.S.$18.9 billion in 2007), which was attributable mainly to a rise in\par imports, and the net outflow of current transfers (from U.S.$1.5 billion in\par 2006 to U.S.$3.5 billion in 2007). The investment income deficit decreased by\par 7.5% (from U.S.$25.3 billion in 2006 to U.S.$23.4 billion in 2007); however,\par it was offset by a 73.8% rise in the net outflow of employee compensation\par (from U.S.$4.2 billion in 2006 to U.S.$7.3 billion in 2007).\par \par In 2008, Russia recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$103.7\par billion (approximately 6.2% of GDP), a 33.3% increase compared to 2007. This\par increase was caused predominantly by a higher trade surplus, due, in turn, to\par exports increasing at a faster rate than imports. The value of exported goods\par increased by 33% in 2008 compared to 2007, primarily as a result of an\par increase in export prices, in particular, the price of oil and petroleum\par products. For example, in 2008 compared to 2007, the volume of crude oil\par exports dropped by 5.8%, whereas the value of crude oil exports increased by\par 32.6%. The average export price for Urals oil grew by 41.2% in 2008 compared\par to 2007. Similarly, over the same period, increases in the value of natural\par gas exports (51.3%) outpaced increases in the volume of gas exports (1.8%). An\par increase in the average export price of coal (45.5%), iron ore and\par concentrates (74.9%), petroleum products (45.6%) and mixed fertilisers\par (258.2%) underpinned export growth in 2008 compared to 2007. Concurrently, the\par value of imported goods increased by 30%, primarily as a result of strong real\par appreciation of the rouble (13.4% against the dollar and 6.4% against the\par euro) and rising GDP in the first three quarters of 2008. Despite the growth\par in the value of Russia's imported goods, the trade surplus increased by 37.3%\par compared to 2007 to U.S.$179.7 billion. The increase in the trade surplus was\par partially offset by a rise in the services deficit and the investment income\par deficit, as well as in the net outflows of employee compensation. The negative\par balance of services increased by U.S.$5.4 billion in 2008 compared to 2007,\par amounting to U.S.$24.3 billion. This increase was due to imports of services\par rising at a faster rate than exports. The sharp increase in the investment\par income deficit (U.S.$23.4 billion in 2007 compared to U.S.$34.8 billion in\par 2008) was caused largely by a sharp rise in investment income payable. Net\par outflows in employee compensation increased by 94.5% to U.S.$14.2 billion in\par 2008, compared to U.S.$7.3 billion in 2007.\par \par In 2009, Russia recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$49.0\par billion (approximately 4.0% of GDP), a 52.7% decrease compared to 2008. This\par sharp decline in the current account balance was primarily attributable to a\par 37.9% drop in the trade balance from U.S.$179.7 billion in 2008 to U.S.$111.6\par billion in 2009. In 2009, despite both nominal and real depreciation of the\par rouble, the value of exported goods fell significantly to U.S.$303.4 billion\par from U.S.$471.6 billion in 2008. This 35.7% decline in exports was primarily\par caused by a drop in both the price and volume of commodity exports as well as\par by the overall contraction of the economy due to the global financial crisis.\par The average export price for Urals oil, for example, declined by 38.8% in 2009\par compared to 2008. Although the current account balance deteriorated during\par this period, the value of imported goods fell by 34.3% from U.S.$291.9 billion\par in 2008 to U.S.$191.8 billion in 2009 and the value of imported services fell\par by 18.4% from U.S.$75.5 billion in 2008 to U.S.$61.6% billion in 2009 due to\par contracting domestic demand associated with the global financial crisis. In\par 2009, Russia also experienced a 38.7% decrease in net employee compensation\par outflows from U.S.$14.2 billion in 2008 to U.S.$8.7 billion in 2009 and a 9.5%\par rise in net investment income from a deficit of U.S.$34.8 billion in 2008 to a\par deficit of U.S.$31.5 billion in 2009.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital and Financial Account\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2005, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$76.7 billion, a 43.1% increase compared to 2004, primarily as a result of\par the growth in official foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and the large capital\par transfers deficit caused by debt relief granted mainly to Syria. The balance\par of direct investment was U.S.$0.1 billion in 2005 compared to U.S.$1.7 billion\par in 2004 (caused by a relative decrease in foreign investments in Russia,\par compared to Russian investments abroad), while the net portfolio investment\par balance reversed from a surplus of U.S.$0.6 billion in 2004 to a deficit of\par U.S.$11.4 billion in 2005. The reversal in the net portfolio investment\par balance was primarily caused by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 monetary authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and banking sector's\par combined acquisition of approximately U.S.$8.9 billion principal amount of\par long-term foreign debt securities. The contraction in the direct investment\par balance, the reversal of the net portfolio investment balance and the increase\par in foreign reserves were partially offset by a surplus in the other\par investments balance. In 2005, the other investments surplus reached U.S.$9.0\par billion compared to a\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. $9.0 billion other investments deficit in 2004. The surplus was mainly due to a significant increase in the value of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans extended to Russian businesses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2006, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$104.2 billion, a 35.9% increase compared to 2005. This increase largely\par reflected a growth in official foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and a reversal of the\par other investments balance from surplus to deficit. The rise in foreign\par reserves (U.S.$107.5 billion in 2006 compared to U.S.$61.5 billion in 2005)\par was mainly due to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy of preventing excessive real\par appreciation of the rouble as well as to the Government's expansion of the\par Stabilisation Fund (the Fund's proceeds at the end of 2006 increased by\par approximately 1.2 trillion roubles compared to year-end 2005). See "Public\par Finance--Deficit Financing" for more information regarding the Stabilisation\par Fund. The other investments balance worsened from a U.S.$9.0 billion surplus\par in 2005 to a U.S.$19.1 billion deficit in 2006. The deficit was primarily\par driven by the following factors: (i) an increase in Russian capital outflows\par in the form of loans extended to foreign residents; (ii) a decrease of arrears\par owed to domestic companies; (iii) an increased interest of Russian residents\par in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  demand and time deposits as a form of short-term savings;\par (iv) a reduction in the value of foreign loans to Russian businesses\par (including Russia's prepayment of approximately U.S.$21.6 billion in debt to\par the Paris Club); (v) and an exchange of Russian Federation Eurobonds for a\par portion of the uninsured Soviet-era trade indebtedness for which Russia has\par agreed to be responsible ("FTO Debt"), which led to an approximate U.S.$1.4\par billion decline in foreign liabilities. Net foreign direct investment improved\par from U.S.$0.1 billion in 2005 to U.S.$6.6 billion in 2006 because foreign\par investment in Russia increased at a faster rate than Russian investment\par abroad. The portfolio investment deficit of U.S.$11.4 billion in 2005 improved\par to a net surplus of U.S.$15.7 billion in 2006 due to increased purchases of\par Russian corporate securities by non-residents, a reduction in Russian\par investments in foreign securities and the exchange of newly issued Russian\par Federation Eurobonds for a portion of Russia's FTO Debt. As a result of net\par inflows to both the banking and corporate sectors, net capital inflows to the\par private sector equalled U.S.$41.4 billion in 2006, a significant increase from\par the U.S.$0.1 billion of net outflows in 2005.\par \par In 2007, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$64.4 billion, a 38.2% decrease compared with 2006. This improvement was\par mainly the result of increased foreign lending to Russian businesses. Loans\par received by Russian residents amounted to U.S.$127.3 billion in 2007 compared\par to U.S.$18.3 billion in 2006. The foreign direct investment balance also\par contributed to the decrease in the capital and financial account deficit,\par having increased by U.S.$2.6 billion in 2007 compared to 2006. This 39%\par increase was the result of foreign investments in Russia outpacing Russian\par investments abroad. The net portfolio investment surplus declined from\par U.S.$15.7 billion in 2006 to U.S.$6 billion in 2007. This decline was due to a\par sharp increase of Russian investments in foreign securities from a surplus of\par U.S.$6.2 billion in 2006 to a deficit of U.S.$10.0 in 2007 and was partially\par offset by a 68.4% increase in foreign holdings of Russian securities. The\par overall improvement in the financial account was partially offset by continued\par inflows of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, as reflected in part by the approximate\par 1.2 trillion rouble increase in the Stabilisation Fund, and a sharp rise in\par net capital transfers from a surplus of U.S.$0.2 billion in 2006 to a deficit\par of U.S.$10.2 billion, which was caused by debt relief granted mainly to\par Afghanistan. See "Public Debt--External Assets." As a result of net inflows to\par both the banking and corporate sectors, net capital inflows to the private\par sector equalled U.S.$82.4 billion in 2007, a 99% increase compared to 2006.\par \par In 2008, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$92.9 billion, a 71.4% increase compared to 2007. This increase reflected\par a sharp decline in both the portfolio investment balance and the other\par investments balance. The portfolio investment balance reversed from a U.S.$6.0\par billion surplus in 2007 to a U.S.$35.2 billion deficit in 2008 as Russian\par residents repaid or bought back their foreign debt. The other investments\par balance moved from a U.S.$79.3 billion surplus in 2007 to a U.S.$114.6 billion\par deficit in 2008, as the global financial crisis prompted investors to reduce\par their exposure to Russian assets. In particular, Russian investors increased\par their holdings of hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  demand and time\par deposits leading to combined outflows of U.S.$69.5 billion in 2008 as compared\par with combined inflows of U.S.$3.0 in 2007. Similarly, foreign loans to Russian\par businesses declined from U.S.$127.3 billion in 2007 to U.S.$63.5 billion in\par 2008. At the same time, Russian residents increased the volume of loans\par extended to foreign residents, in part, to foreign subsidiaries of Russian\par companies. As a result, net private sector capital outflows (including\par outflows from both the banking and corporate sectors) in 2008 reached\par U.S.$132.8 billion. A U.S.$38.9 billion decrease in foreign reserves in 2008\par and an increase in the direct investment balance from U.S.$9.2 billion in 2007\par to U.S.$19.4 billion in 2008 partially offset the deterioration in the\par portfolio investment balance and other investments balance. The foreign\par investment balance improved because foreign investment in Russia continued to\par outpace Russian investment abroad.\par \par In 2009, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$47.7, a 48.4% decrease compared to 2008. This improvement was mainly\par attributable to a reduction in the portfolio investment balance deficit and\par the other investments deficit. The portfolio investment balance improved from\par a deficit of U.S.$35.2 billion in 2008 to a deficit of U.S.$2.7 billion in\par 2009 primarily because of an increase of foreign purchases of Russian\par securities as the rouble stabilised and the overall Russian economy began to\par recover in the second quarter of 2009. The deficit in other investments\par improved significantly, from a deficit of U.S.$114.6 billion in 2008 to a\par deficit of U.S.$19.4 billion in 2009, despite a sharp decline in foreign loans\par to Russian residents. The other investments deficit fell for several reasons.\par Russian investments in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cash, demand and time deposits\par decreased, as did the value of Russian loans to foreign residents. Foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cash holdings reversed from a U.S.$30.0 billion deficit in 2008 to a\par U.S.$6.0 billion surplus in 2009, and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deposits decreased from\par a deficit of U.S.$39.5 billion in 2008 to a surplus of U.S.$6.4 billion in\par 2009. The value of Russian loans to foreign residents declined from a deficit\par of U.S.$54.3 billion in 2008 to a surplus of U.S.$7.5 billion. Concurrently,\par an increase in arrears was recorded as foreign residents accelerated their\par repayments of Russian loans. The drop in the other investments deficit was\par also supported by a reduction in the non-repatriation of export proceeds and\par import advances not repaid in time (from U.S.$39.3 billion in 2008 to\par U.S.$26.6 billion in 2009). In 2009, despite net inflows in the second and\par fourth quarters, Russia recorded overall net private sector capital outflows\par of U.S.$56.9 billion, which were mainly driven by the repayment of Russian\par loans and the banking sector's purchase of foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  assets. A\par U.S.$3.4 billion decrease in foreign reserves supported the overall\par improvement in the financial account. The improvement in the portfolio\par investment deficit and other investment deficit as well as the contraction in\par foreign reserves was partially offset by a reversal of the net direct\par investment balance, from a net inflow of U.S.$19.4 billion in 2008 to a net\par outflow of U.S.$7.3 billion in 2009. This reversal was primarily caused by a\par sharp decline in foreign direct investment in Russia. The improvement in the\par financial account deficit was partially offset by net outflows of capital\par transfers amounting to U.S.$11.6 billion, which was the result of debt relief\par granted by Russia to certain of its debtors.\par \par Exchange rate fluctuations and periods of economic uncertainty have\par often caused Russian banks, enterprises and households to convert their\par current holdings and savings into foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (particularly\par dollar-denominated) assets. Though the impact of these factors has declined\par in recent years, capital flight has historically been significant in Russia,\par caused mainly by illegal outflows through non-repatriation of export proceeds\par and unrefunded import advances. Non-repatriation of export proceeds and\par unrefunded import advances fluctuated between 2005 and 2009 (U.S.$27.2 billion\par in 2005, U.S.$19.9 billion in 2006, U.S.$34.5 billion in 2007, U.S.$39.3\par billion in 2008 and U.S.$26.6 billion in 2009). Russia's balance of payments\par accounts include a high level of unaccounted transactions, which likewise have\par historically fluctuated, including outflows of U.S.$7.9 billion in 2005,\par inflows of U.S.$9.5 billion in 2006 and outflows of U.S.$13.3 billion,\par U.S.$11.9 billion and U.S.$1.2 billion in 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively.\par \par In 2005, the stock of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cash held by Russian\par residents increased by U.S.$1.4 billion, due to the nominal depreciation of\par the rouble against the dollar and despite the nominal appreciation of the\par rouble against the euro. The nominal depreciation of the dollar during 2006\par and 2007 encouraged Russian individuals and small businesses to convert dollar\par cash into roubles and resulted in a U.S.$9.5 billion and U.S.$15.5 billion\par decline in the stock of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cash in 2006 and 2007, respectively.\par The sharp depreciation of the rouble in 2008 (19.7% against the dollar and\par 15.3% against the euro, in nominal terms) as well as instability in global\par markets encouraged Russian residents to convert rouble holdings into foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and resulted in a U.S.$30.0 billion increase in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par holdings. In 2009, the rouble continued to depreciate (by 2.9% against the\par dollar and 4.7% against the euro, in nominal terms), which, together with\par continued concern about conditions in global markets, led to a further\par increase of U.S.$6.0 billion of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings among Russian\par households and businesses.\par \par Foreign Trade\par \par Foreign Trade Regime\par \par Following the dissolution of the Soviet Union, Russia introduced\par import tariffs for a broad range of goods. In an effort to bring its external\par trade regime more closely in line with WTO standards, Russia has introduced a\par maximum import tariff rate and has worked to unify rates within commodity\par groups. Currently, the maximum rate of import tariffs is 30%, with the\par exception of five commodity groups, which are levied at various rates,\par reaching 100% for spirits. In January 2009, Russia substantially raised its\par import tariffs on used cars in an effort to support the country's domestic\par automotive industry. The tariffs are calculated in accordance with a formula\par that is based on the age and engine type of the vehicle. In January\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2010, Russia imposed a ban on the import of poultry containing chlorine levels in excess of a certain threshold. This new regulation has led to significant reductions in poultry imports from the United States, one of Russia's primary suppliers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Exports are an important source of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earnings for\par Russia. Currently, Russia has various export restrictions in place, such as\par export quotas, export tariffs and voluntary export restraints, which continue\par to exist on a list of goods agreed between Russia and the European Union (for\par some steel products) and between Russia and the United States (for certain\par consumer goods). The Government has also introduced duties on the export of\par oil and natural gas. Duties on oil exports are based on world oil prices, and\par the duty on natural gas is 30%. See "Public Finance--Russian Tax System" for a\par discussion of these customs duties. On 1 January 2004, a new Customs Code went\par into effect, which seeks to facilitate customs clearance procedures. See "The\par Russian Economy--Civil and Commercial Law" for a discussion of the Customs\par Code.\par \par The Government places a high priority on joining the WTO. Russia\par has concluded bilateral agreements on its accession to the WTO with most of\par the members of the working group established to consider Russia's membership.\par In May 2004, Russia and the European Union signed a protocol on Russia's\par accession to the WTO. Before it can join the WTO, Russia must reach agreement\par with the United States and Georgia on several outstanding trade issues, as\par well as ensure it conforms with WTO standards in five key areas: Access to the\par goods market; intellectual property rights; agricultural tariffs; legislative\par and institutional framework; and access to the market for services. See\par "Russian Federation--International Relations--Russia's Position in the\par International Community."\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Composition of Trade\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table illustrates the composition of Russia's exports and imports on a customs basis (excluding unregistered trade adjustments) for the years 2005 to 2009:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          Structure of Trade(1)(2)(3)\par \par                         (excluding unregistered trade)\par \par                                                    2005           2006           2007           2008           2009\par \par                                                U.S.$          U.S.$          U.S.$          U.S.$          U.S.$\par                                               billion   %    billion   %    billion   %    billion   %    billion   %\par