by 22.6% and 39.6%,\par respectively, whereas the volume of oil and gas exports decreased by 1.6% and\par 2.1%, respectively). Partially offsetting this increase was an approximate 3%\par drop in the contribution of metals and precious stones and metals to total\par exports. In 2007, the overall share of mineral products, metals and precious\par stones and metals as a percentage of total exports declined compared to 2006,\par primarily due to increases in export revenues from chemical products and\par rubber, timber and foodstuffs. This decline occurred despite higher revenues\par in 2007 from oil and gas exports (an increase of 13.9% compared to 2006) and\par exports of metals and precious stones and metals (an increase of 14.4%\par compared to 2006). In 2008, the share of mineral products, metals and precious\par stones and metals as a percentage of total exports rose to 83.0% from 80.6% in\par 2007. This increase was due to a sharp increase in oil prices (in 2008,\par compared to 2007, the value of oil and gas exports increased by 32.6% and\par 54.1%,\par \par respectively, whereas the volume of oil exports decreased by 6.0%\par and the volume of gas exports increased by 1.8%), which, in turn, led to an\par increase in the share of mineral products as a share of total exports from\par 64.7% in 2007 to 69.8% in 2008. Partially offsetting this increase was an\par approximate 17% decrease in the contribution of metals and precious stones and\par metals to overall exports. In 2009, the overall share of mineral products,\par metals and precious stones and metals as a percentage of total exports\par declined to 80.3% from 83.0% in 2008. This decrease was primarily due to the\par global financial crisis, which contributed to a contraction both in the volume\par and value of mineral products and metals.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other significant exports include machinery, equipment and transport (5.6%, 5.8%, 5.6%, 4.9% and 5.9% of total exports in 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively) and chemical products and rubber (6.0%, 5.6%, 5.9%, 6.4% and 6.2% of total exports in 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Imports of machinery, equipment and transport accounted for\par approximately 44% of total imports in 2005, 47.7% in 2006, 50.9% in 2007,\par 52.7% in 2008 and 43.4% in 2009. Russia also imports significant amounts of\par foodstuffs and agricultural products, chemical products and rubber. In the\par aggregate, foodstuffs and agricultural products accounted for 17.7% of imports\par in 2005, 15.7% in 2006, 13.8% in 2007, 13.2% in 2008 and 18.0% in 2009.\par Chemical products and rubber constituted 16.5%, 15.8%, 13.8%, 13.2% and 16.7%\par of total imports in 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Direction of Trade\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following tables illustrate the geographic distribution of Russia's trade on a customs basis (excluding unregistered trade adjustments) for the years 2005 to 2009:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                Exports(1)(2)(3)\par \par                         (excluding unregistered trade)\par \par                                                   2005           2006           2007           2008           2009\par \par                                               U.S.$    %     U.S.$    %     U.S.$    %     U.S.$    %     U.S.$    %\par                                              million change million change\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 million change million change million change\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exports to non-CIS countries                 208,846   37.3 258,934   24.0\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 299,269   15.6 397,925   33.0 254,998 (35.9)\par OECD countries                               155,853   44.5 200,560   28.7 230,558   15.0 312,343   35.5 193,585 (38.0)\par Germany                                       19,735   48.4  24,498   24.1  26,346    7.5  33,164   25.9  18,711 (43.6