r exchange rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Includes Bulgaria, Romania, Vietnam, China, North Korea, Mongolia and Cuba.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources: Customs Service; Rosstat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Between 2005 and 2009, Russia's exports to OECD countries grew by approximately 79.7% compared to 67.8% growth in total Russian exports. As a percentage of total exports, exports to OECD countries remained stable, accounting for 64% to 67% of Russia's total exports in each year from 2005 through 2009. In 2009, Russian exports to OECD countries declined by 38% compared to 2008 as a result of the contraction in worldwide demand resulting from the global financial crisis. Overall total exports in 2009 fell by 35.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russian exports to CIS countries grew by 10.7% in 2005,\par significantly slower than in 2004, due mainly to a decrease in\par Russia-Belarus trade caused by a Russian policy switch to the\par country-of-destination method of calculating VAT payments, which took effect\par on 1 January 2005. In 2006 and 2007, Russian exports to CIS countries grew at\par a faster rate than exports to non-CIS countries (an increase of 29.7% and\par 24.5%, respectively, to CIS countries compared to an increase of 24.0% and\par 15.6%, respectively, to non-CIS countries.) This rise in exports was primarily\par due to growth of overall economic activity in the CIS countries. At a growth\par rate of 32.3%, exports to CIS countries grew in 2008 in parallel with exports\par to non-CIS countries. In 2009, Russia's exports to CIS countries fell by\par 32.9%, which was slightly less than the rate at which total Russian exports\par declined (35.5%). From 2005 through 2009, Russian exports to CIS countries\par recorded an overall growth rate of 58.7%. Exports to CIS countries accounted\par for 13-16% of Russia's total exports in each year from 2005 through 2009.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in exports to transition economies, i.e., Bulgaria, Romania, Vietnam, China, North Korea, Mongolia and Cuba, generally followed trends in Russia's total exports, accounting for approximately 7-8% of total export value between 2005 and 2009. Exports to transition economies grew by approximately 36.4% in 2005, 18.6% in 2006, 5.5% in 2007 and 31.5% in 2008, and fell by 30.8% in 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Supported by a high rate of GDP growth through 2008, Russia's\par imports from OECD countries grew by approximately 166.9% between 2005 and\par 2009, in line with overall import growth during the same period. As a\par percentage of total imports, Russian imports from OECD countries remained\par stable at 60-63% of total import value in each year from 2005 through 2009. In\par 2009, because of the contraction in domestic demand and consumer income\par resulting from the global financial crisis, Russian imports from OECD\par countries dropped by 39.6%, while overall imports declined by 37.3% compared\par to 2008.\par \par Between 2005 and 2009, the share of total imports from transition\par economies followed an upward trend, primarily due to a rise in imports from\par China. In 2005, imports from transition economies accounted for 8.1% of total\par imports, compared with 10.3%, 13.1%, 14.0% and 14.9% in 2006, 2007, 2008 and\par 2009, respectively. In 2009, imports from transition economies declined by\par 33.3% compared to 2008 due to weaker domestic demand.\par \par Russia's economic growth through 2008 led to a decline in the share\par of imports from CIS countries, as imports from other countries became more\par attractive to Russian consumers. In 2005, Russian imports from CIS countries\par accounted for 19.2% of total Russian imports, compared with 16.2%, 15.0%,\par 13.7% and 13.0% in 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively. Imports from CIS\par countries declined by 40.4% in 2009 compared to 2008 due to the effects of the\par global financial crisis.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of the non-CIS countries, Germany, the Netherlands, China, Italy, Turkey, United States, Poland and Japan are Russia's leading trading partners, as measured by value. Belarus, Ukraine and increasingly Kazakhstan are Russia's leading trading partners as measured by value within the CIS.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia's largest export markets in 2008 and 2009 were the\par Netherlands (12.2% and 12.0%, respectively, of Russian exports), Italy (9.0%\par and 8.3%, respectively), Germany (7.1% and 6.2%, respectively), Turkey (5.9%\par and 5.4%, respectively), Belarus (5.0% and 5.5%, respectively), China (4.5%\par and 5.5%, respectively), and Ukraine (5.0% and 4.6%, respectively). In 2008\par and 2009, the most important exporters to Russia were China (13.0% and 13.6%,\par respectively, of Russian imports), Germany (12.8% and 12.7%, respectively),\par Japan (7.0% and 4.3%, respectively), Ukraine (6.1% and 5.4%, respectively),\par the United States (5.2% and 5.5%, respectively), Italy (4.1% and 4.7%,\par respectively), Belarus (4.0% in both years), France (3.7% and 5.0%,\par respectively) and Korea (4.0% and 2.9%, respectively). In 2008 and 2009,\par Ukraine and Belarus together accounted for 69.5% and 67.6%, respectively, of\par Russia's foreign trade with CIS countries.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Official International Reserves\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth information with respect to the official international reserves of the Russian Federation as at 31 December of each year from 2005 to 2009:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Official International Reserves(1)\par \par                                                                                        As at 31 December\par                                                                             2005       2006     2007     2008    2009\par                                                                           \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (millions of dollars, except as indicated)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Official reserves (excluding gold)                                         \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  175,892  295,567  466,750  411,748 416,653\par Special Drawing Rights                                                             6        7        1        1   8,901\par Reserve position in IMF                                                          196      283      374    1,051   1,927\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                                              175,690  295,277  466,376  410,695 405,825\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Official gold reserves (fine troy ounces, million)                              13.4     15.2     14.5     16.7    20.5\par U.S.$ million(2)                                                               6,349    8,164   12,012   14,533  22,382\par Total official reserves (including gold)                                   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  182,240  303,732  478,762  426,281 439,034\par -------------------------\par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Based on the official rouble/dollar exchange rates and reference price for gold, as established by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the relevant reporting date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) At official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reference prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2005, an increase in world oil prices, together with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy of preventing sharp real appreciation of the rouble, led to an\par increase in Russia's reserves. As at 31 December 2005, total official reserves\par (including gold) stood at approximately U.S.$182.2 billion (compared to\par approximately U.S.$124.5 billion as at 31 December 2004), of which\par approximately U.S.$175.7 billion was in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and approximately\par U.S.$6.3 billion was in gold. These reserves represented 13.3 months of import\par coverage.\par \par Russia's international reserves improved further in 2006. As at 31\par December 2006, total official reserves (including gold) stood at approximately\par U.S.$303.7 billion, which represents 17.4 months of import coverage and an\par increase of approximately U.S.$121.5 billion compared to year-end 2005.\par Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves amounted to approximately U.S.$295.3 billion and\par gold reserves stood at approximately U.S.$8.2 billion. This improvement was\par due primarily to an increase in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves (backed by high\par commodity prices and the country's large trade surplus). The growth in\par reserves was also supported by net private sector foreign capital inflows of\par U.S.$41.8 billion, a significant expansion over net inflows in 2005.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The growth of international reserves continued in 2007 due to favourable world commodity prices and high inflows of foreign capital, including net private capital inflows of U.S. $83.1 billion. At 31 December 2007, total official reserves (including gold) stood at approximately U.S. $478.8 billion, representing approximately 20 months of import coverage. As of the same date, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves amounted to approximately U.S. $466.0 billion, and gold reserves stood at approximately U.S. $12.0 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par At 31 December 2008, total official reserves (including gold)\par decreased by approximately U.S.$52.5 billion to U.S.$426.3 billion. These\par reserves represented approximately 13.9 months of import coverage. The\par decrease in total reserves was due to an 11.3% drop in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves to U.S.$411.5 billion. The decline in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, in\par turn, was primarily attributable to the onset of the global financial crisis\par in the second half of 2008 and its negative impact on world commodity prices\par and aggregate demand. As a result of the financial crisis, Russia experienced\par a significant outflow of private capital, amounting to U.S.$132.8 billion.\par \par As at 31 December 2009, total official reserves (including gold)\par stood at approximately U.S.$439.0 billion, an increase of approximately\par U.S.$12.8 billion compared to year-end 2008. These reserves represented nearly\par 21 months of import coverage. The increase in reserves was primarily\par attributable to a significant rise in Russia's balance of special drawing\par rights, i.e., international reserve assets created by the IMF, from U.S.$1\par million in 2008 to U.S.$8.9 billion in 2009 and in the country's gold reserves\par from U.S.$14.5 billion in 2008 to U.S.$22.4 billion in 2009. The increase in\par special drawing rights was due to an improvement in Russia's reserve position\par with the IMF. The increase in gold reserves was the result of Russian\par purchases of gold as a means to diversify and protect its reserves during the\par crisis. The overall rise in total reserves was partially offset by a reduction\par in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves from U.S.$410.7 billion in 2008 to U.S.$405.8\par billion in 2009. This decrease was primarily due to the continued impact of\par the global economic crisis on the Russian economy, particularly in the first\par quarter of 2009 when foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves dropped by 7.0% and net private\par capital outflows amounted to U.S.$35.1 billion.\par \par Since 1999, Russia's international reserves have grown 35-fold and\par have covered an increasing portion of the country's external debt. In 2009,\par Russia's international reserves provided 98% coverage, and total external\par (public and private) debt equalled 37% of GDP. As of year-end 2009, Russia's\par reserves were the second highest among the G-8 countries and the third highest\par in the world.\par \par The Government strictly regulates Russia's output, sale and export\par of precious stones and metals, including gold, platinum and diamonds. As part\par of this regulatory regime, the Government licences and has a right of first\par refusal to purchase Russia's precious metals output. The Government may add to\par its reserves any purchased output or any proceeds from the sale and export of\par precious metals.\par \par Both the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  currently have\par official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and gold reserves. Reserves of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are allocated to either the National Wealth Fund or Reserve Fund. See\par "Public Finance--Deficit Financing" for more information on the National\par Wealth Fund and Reserve Fund. The percentage of Russia's total official\par international reserves that are controlled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  varies\par year to year and depends on the reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  undertaken by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the year. In 2007, for example, the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held approximately one-third of the country's total\par official international reserves, whereas, in 2008, it held nearly one-half.\par This change was generally due to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision to defend the\par rouble at the end of 2008 and to the decision to accumulate reserves in the\par Funds controlled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . At year-end 2009, the share of\par foreign reserves held by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  declined to approximately\par one-third as a result of depletion of the Reserve Fund, the proceeds of which\par were used to cover the budget deficit, and accumulation of reserves at the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was able to increase its reserves because there\par was less need to support the rouble in 2009 than in 2008.\par \par                                 PUBLIC FINANCE\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The information presented herein with respect to the federal budget has been prepared substantially in accordance with the guidelines and definitions set forth in the IMF's publication "Government Finance Statistics" (GFS-1986).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Overview\par \par Russia maintained a strong fiscal position through 2008, running\par surpluses of 8.1%, 8.4%, 6.1% and 4.9% in 2005, 2006, 2007 and 2008,\par respectively. In 2009, Russia recorded a budget deficit of 6.2% of GDP. The\par federal budget has followed a similar trend, producing primary surpluses of\par 8.4%, 8.1%, 5.9% and 4.5% of GDP in 2005, 2006, 2007 and 2008, respectively,\par and a primary deficit of 5.4% in 2009. Russia's positive fiscal position\par through 2008 was attributable largely to favourable conditions in world energy\par markets, resulting in higher revenues from related production, export duties,\par excise and other taxes as well as higher profit tax revenues, combined with\par improvements in the federal budgetary process, allowing for greater control\par over expenditure, and more efficient tax policy. The reduction in the budget\par surpluses in 2006 and 2007 was caused primarily by increases in spending on\par the national economy and state administration as well as growth in the volumes\par of federal transfers to sub-federal budgets. The surplus continued to decrease\par in 2008 primarily because of a contraction in tax revenues (as a share of\par GDP), particularly domestic VAT proceeds, and a slight rise in expenditures\par mainly in the form of transfers to state extra-budgetary funds. The federal\par budget deficit in 2009 was mainly due to the effects of the global financial\par crisis, which reduced revenues (particularly as a result of the decline in\par worldwide demand for Russia's commodity exports) and caused expenditures to\par rise as a consequence of the Government's fiscal stimulus package.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth certain summary information regarding Russia's public finances for the years 2005 to 2009:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                               Enlarged Budget(1)\par \par                                                                 2005       2006        2007        2008        2009\par \par                                                                                 (millions of roubles)\par Enlarged Budget\par Revenue                                                       8,579,637  10,625,812  13,250,675  16,003,378  13,420,670\par Expenditure                                                   6,820,645   8,375,228  11,245,768  13,989,221  15,847,343\par Surplus (deficit)                                             1,758,992   2,250,584   2,004,907   2,014,157 (2,426,673)\par Consolidated budget\par Revenue      