t\uc2 \f2 .\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) Consolidated budget revenues less non-interest expenditure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) Federal budget revenues less non-interest expenditure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Budgetary Process\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is centrally involved in each stage of the federal budgetary process, including establishing fiscal policy objectives, preparation of the budget, adoption of the budget by the Federal Assembly, executing the budget (including later amendments) and auditing the budget's execution. The Audit Chamber also monitors and audits the execution of the budget.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Budget Preparation and Adoption\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Under the Budget Code, the draft budget is prepared by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and presented, first, to the Government, and then, not later than\par 26 August of the current financial year, to the State Duma, where it proceeds\par through three readings. In the first reading, the budget's overall\par macroeconomic framework and the budgeted revenue, deficit (surplus) and\par expenditure are established. The State Duma cannot increase revenues or the\par deficit ceiling without the approval of the Government. The second reading is\par devoted to allocating total authorised expenditure to specific purposes\par according to functional classifications within limits defined after the first\par reading as well as allocating the expenditure among government institutions,\par lending and borrowing programmes and other items. In the third reading, the\par budget as a whole is adopted, and no further amendments are permitted. After\par the State Duma's third reading, the Federation Council votes on the bill. Once\par passed by the Federation Council, the bill is sent to the President for\par consideration. The draft budget becomes law following its signature by the\par President and publication in the authorised print media. The federal law\par becomes effective on 1 January of the relevant year. During the course of the\par fiscal year, the Government may submit to the State Duma amendments to the\par budget law for consideration in accordance with an expedited legislative\par procedure. Pursuant to this\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 procedure, the State Duma is required to consider amendments to the budget in three readings, all of which must take place within 25 days after the amendments have been submitted. Such amendments must then be reviewed by the Federation Council within 14 days after their receipt from the State Duma.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In accordance with amendments introduced in 2007, beginning in 2008, the budget includes figures on a three-year, rather than annual, basis. Therefore, the 2010 Federal Budget Law includes figures for 2010 as well as forecasted figures for 2011 and 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Budget Execution\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is responsible for the execution of the budget law.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The budget law sets overall nominal levels for revenue, expenditure and deficit (surplus), as well as caps for domestic and foreign borrowing and other sources of deficit financing. Subject to the five percent threshold discussed below, approval of the State Duma is required if the budget plan exceeds the nominal level of expenditure or borrowing. In each year since 2005, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has executed the budget using only revenue actually collected and without violating the deficit ceiling established in the budget law.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The expenditure levels established in the budget law are annual\par spending limits with a breakdown for each line item. Upon adoption of the\par budget law, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  promulgates annual allocation targets for\par each line item. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may also establish binding quarterly\par or monthly allocation targets for each line item in order to enhance\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  control over budgetary performance. The Budget Code sets forth an\par exhaustive list of circumstances when the spending limits for a particular\par line item can be modified without legislative amendment. For example, the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may increase expenditure for each line item by no more\par than five percent if the amount initially allocated proves to be insufficient.\par Increases of over five percent and other modifications to spending limits\par beyond those stipulated in the Budget Code can be made only after\par corresponding amendments are made to the budget law.\par \par In 2005, the federal budget provided for a fiscal surplus of 278.1\par billion roubles, or 1.5% of GDP, and a primary surplus of 522.3 billion\par roubles, or 2.8% of GDP. Supported by higher than projected oil prices,\par federal budget revenues in 2005 amounted to 23.7% of GDP, as compared to the\par budgeted estimate of 17.8% of GDP, while total expenditure equalled the\par budgeted estimate of 16.3% of GDP. A significant portion of the budgetary\par surplus from this period (6.5% of GDP) was transferred to the Stabilisation\par Fund. See "--Deficit Financing" for more information on the Stabilisation\par Fund. A portion of these funds, equal to approximately 3% of GDP, was\par subsequently withdrawn to prepay outstanding external debt, including to the\par Paris Club and IMF. See "Public Debt--External Debt."\par \par The 2006 budget law envisaged a fiscal surplus of 776.0 billion\par roubles, or 3.2% of GDP, and a primary surplus of 974.5 billion roubles, or\par 4.0% of GDP. Supported by higher than projected oil prices, federal budget\par revenues in 2006 amounted to 23.3% of GDP, as compared to the budgeted\par estimate of 20.7% of GDP, while total expenditure of 15.9% of GDP was slightly\par lower in relative terms than the budgeted estimate of 17.5% of GDP. The year\par ended with an actual surplus of 7.4% of GDP, considerably higher than what was\par originally budgeted. Federal budget revenues transferred to the Stabilisation\par Fund amounted to 1,805.0 billion roubles, or 6.7% of GDP, in 2006. A portion\par of these funds, equal to approximately 2.3% of GDP, was subsequently withdrawn\par to prepay outstanding external debt to the Paris Club.\par \par The budget law adopted in 2007 provided for a fiscal surplus of\par 1,501.8 billion roubles, or 4.8% of GDP, and a primary surplus of 1,658.7\par billion roubles, or 5.3% of GDP. Supported by higher than projected oil\par prices, federal budget revenues in 2007 amounted to 23.5% of GDP, as compared\par to the budgeted estimate of 22.3% of GDP, while total expenditure of 18.1% of\par GDP was slightly higher in relative terms than the budgeted estimate of 17.5%\par of GDP. The year ended with an actual surplus of 5.4% of GDP, higher than what\par was originally budgeted. Federal budget revenues transferred to the\par Stabilisation Fund amounted to 1,602.8 billion roubles, or 4.9% of GDP in\par 2007.\par \par The 2008 budget law provided for a fiscal surplus of 1,435.7\par billion roubles or 3.4% of GDP, and a primary surplus of 1,614.7 billion\par roubles, or 3.8% of GDP. Federal budget revenues in 2008 amounted to 22.5% of\par GDP, as compared to the budgeted estimate of 21.2% of GDP, while total\par expenditure of 18.4% of GDP was slightly higher in relative terms than the\par budgeted estimate of 17.8% of GDP. The year ended with an actual surplus of\par 4.1% of GDP, higher than what was originally budgeted. In 2008, the\par Stabilisation Fund was replaced by the Reserve Fund and National Wealth Fund,\par and its outstanding balance was\par \par transferred to the two new funds. See "--Deficit Financing" for\par more information on the Reserve Fund and National Wealth Fund." Federal budget\par revenues transferred to the Reserve Fund and National Wealth Fund in 2008\par (excluding transfers from the Stabilisation Fund) equalled 420.60 billion\par roubles (0.9% of GDP) and 1,590.21 billion roubles (3.6% of GDP),\par respectively. At the end of 2008, the balance of the Reserve Fund was 4,027.6\par billion roubles, or 9.8% of GDP, and the balance of the National Wealth Fund\par was 2,584.5 billion roubles, or 6.3% of GDP.\par \par The budget law in 2009 (as of April 2009) envisaged a fiscal\par deficit of 2,978.4 billion roubles, or 7.4% of GDP, and a primary deficit of\par 2,749.3 billion roubles, or 6.8% of GDP. Federal budget revenues in 2009\par amounted to 18.8% of GDP, as compared to the budgeted estimate of 16.6% of\par GDP, while total expenditure of 24.7% of GDP was slightly higher in relative\par terms than the budgeted estimate of 24.0% of GDP. The year ended with an\par actual federal deficit of 5.9%, lower than what was originally budgeted.\par Federal budget revenues transferred to the Reserve Fund in 2009 equalled 489\par billion roubles (1.3% of GDP). No federal budget revenues were transferred to\par the National Wealth Fund in 2009. Proceeds from the Reserve Fund were used to\par finance the budget deficit in 2009, which led to a 54.5% decline in the\par Reserve Fund balance during 2009 to 1,831 billion roubles (4.7% of GDP).\par \par 2010 Federal Budget Law(1)(2)\par \par                                                                        2010               2011               2012\par                                                           