             \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2   0.0       --       --       --       --\par OFZs with fixed rates                                                         0.6      0.8      0.9      0.8      1.8\par OFZs with fixed coupon                                                        0.6      0.4      0.2      0.1       --\par Amortising OFZs(2)                                                            2.8      2.5      2.4      2.1      2.2\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 State savings bonds (OGSZ, GSO)                                                --      0.2      0.3      0.4      0.7\par Other                                                                         0.1      0.1      0.2      0.2      0.7\par Memo:\par Exchange rate, rouble/U.S.$, end of period                                   28.8     26.3     24.5     29.4     30.2\par GDP (billions of roubles)                                                  21,625   26,904   33,103   41,256   39,016\par Total domestic public debt (U.S.$ billion)                                 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 30.4 40.5 53.1 51.0 69.4\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -------------------------\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data due to changes in the accounting methodology of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (2) In accordance with the federal budget law for 2003,\par non-marketable rouble government bonds (fixed rate OFZs) held by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were exchanged for amortising OFZs with a total face value of 282.5\par billion roubles in February 2003. The goal of this exchange \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was to\par reinforce liquidity management by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Similar exchanges have\par occurred since 2005.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government Domestic Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government's domestic indebtedness consists principally of medium- and long-term OFZs. As of 31 December 2009, the Government's domestic debt amounted to 2,094.70 billion roubles (U.S. $69.4 billion), or 5.4% of GDP for 2009. Since 2006, the Government has also issued state savings bonds. The Government has in the past issued short-term GKOs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par OFZs are rouble denominated obligations with a maturity of over one\par year, and pay interest quarterly, semi-annually or annually. Initially issued\par in June 1995, OFZs (including OFZs issued in the GKO/OFZ restructuring\par discussed below) represented 93.1% of the Government's domestic debt (17.5% of\par GDP) at the end of 1998 and 97.2% of the Government's domestic debt (3.9% of\par GDP) at the end of 2005. Since 2005, OFZs as a share of the Government's\par domestic debt has declined to 91.6% (3.6% of GDP) in 2006, 88.2% (3.5% of GDP)\par in 2007, 82.9% (3.0% of GDP) and 74.94% (4.0% of GDP) in 2009.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OFZs were initially issued with floating coupons pegged to the average weighted market yields of GKOs redeemable during the period beginning 30 days before and ending 30 days after the coupon payment. Since 2004, the Government has not issued OFZs with variable coupons.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In June 1996, fixed rate OFZs were introduced and have subsequently\par replaced floating rate OFZs. The Government issued two- and three-year fixed\par rate OFZs until the middle of 1998, paying annual or semi-annual interest at\par rates between 10% and 20% per annum. Fixed rate OFZs represented 14.1% (0.6%\par of GDP), 19.3% (0.8% of GDP), 22.2% (0.9% of GDP), 21.9% (0.8% of GDP) and\par 33.7% (1.8% of GDP) of the Government's domestic debt in 2005, 2006, 2007,\par 2008 and 2009, respectively.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GKOs are rouble denominated zero-coupon instruments with a maturity of less than one year. GKOs were first issued in May 1993 and, as a result of a shift to medium-term borrowing and the restructuring of certain GKOs and OFZs after the events of 17 August 1998, were gradually eliminated by 2005.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As part of the restructuring of GKOs and certain OFZs following the\par events of 17 August 1998, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issued OFZs with maturities\par of three and five years and fixed declining coupons. Fixed coupon OFZs have\par since been issued with maturities varying from two to four years, although\par their overall contribution to domestic debt has declined in recent years.\par Fixed coupon OFZs comprised 15.0% (0.6% of GDP), 8.9% (0.4% of GDP), 4.0%\par (0.2% of GDP), 2.3% (0.1% of GDP) and 0.0% (0.0% of GDP) of the Government's\par domestic debt in 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively.\par \par In May 2002 and September 2002, the Government issued fixed coupon\par amortising OFZs (serial bonds) with maturities of 4.5 and 6 years. In 2004,\par fixed coupon amortising OFZs became the main instrument of domestic borrowing.\par Fixed coupon amortising OFZs represented 68.1% (2.8% of GDP), 63.4% (2.5% of\par GDP), 62.1% (2.4% of GDP), 58.8% (2.1% of GDP) and 41.2% (2.2% of GDP) of the\par Government's domestic debt in 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2006, state savings bonds, which are sold to retail customers and for which there is no trading market, began to play a more substantial role in the Government's domestic borrowing. State savings bonds comprised 4.9% (0.2% of GDP), 7.8% (0.3% of GDP), 11.8% (0.4% of GDP) and 12.8% (0.7% of GDP) of the Government's domestic debt in 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As at 31 December 2009, the average maturity of the Government's\par marketable domestic debt was 1,577 days, as compared to 1,892 days as at 31\par December 2005. OFZs represented 85.0% of the total stock (measured by market\par value) of domestic bonds as of 31 December 2009, as compared to approximately\par 100% as at 31 December 2005.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Non-Residents' Access to Local Bond Market\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Following the events of 17 August 1998 and the adoption of the\par restructuring scheme for GKOs and certain OFZs in late 1998, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reintroduced additional restrictions on non-resident holders of GKOs and OFZs.\par Conversion of rouble investments in GKOs and OFZs was limited to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  auctions held by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , special \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conversion GKO\par issues offered by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and transfers of funds from\par investment "S" accounts to conversion "S" accounts. The latter required funds\par to be deposited in non-interest bearing "transit" accounts for 365 days before\par repatriation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The restrictions on repatriation of money from "S" accounts by non-residents were subsequently liberalised. Effective 15 September 2003, non-residents were allowed to transfer money from investment S accounts to conversion S accounts freely for subsequent repatriation. Certain restrictions on new foreign\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 investment in the Government's domestic rouble bonds were retained, however, to avoid the adverse effect of short-term capital inflows on the balance of payments and securities market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government Domestic Debt Service\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Domestic debt service decreased from 0.3% of GDP in 2005 to 0.2% of\par GDP in each of 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009. The domestic debt to GDP ratio\par remained flat at 4.0% of GDP in 2005 and 2006, after which it fell slightly to\par 3.9% of GDP in 2007 and 3.6% of GDP in 2008. The domestic debt to GDP ratio\par increased in 2009 to 5.4%. Domestic debt service declined in 2006 mainly due\par to a decline in interest rates. Despite a decline in the domestic debt to GDP\par ratio, domestic debt service remained flat through 2007 and 2008, largely due\par to an increase in interest rates. In 2009, domestic debt service did not\par change due to declining interest rates.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government Domestic Debt Policy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the 1998 financial crisis and the events of 17 August 1998, the Government's domestic debt policy was principally aimed at restoring the domestic debt market. The Government has subsequently been able to increase the number of available domestic debt instruments and to improve their liquidity and lengthen their maturity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Going forward, the Government intends in the medium-term to borrow\par domestically to finance the budget deficit and to repay a portion of its\par domestic debt. This will lead to a moderate increase in the domestic debt\par stock. Demand for medium- and long-term debt instruments is expected to\par remain relatively high due to the pension reforms enacted in 2001 and amended\par in 2009, which established a "funded" component to the country's pension\par system, whereby a portion of pension contributions may be invested in domestic\par securities. The Government also intends in the medium-term to further develop\par the domestic securities market by scheduling regular auctions, improving\par settlement systems, introducing exchange-traded debt and increasing its\par issuances of domestic debt.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other Public Sector Domestic Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia's total domestic public debt includes debt of sub-federal\par and local governments in the form of municipal bonds and bank credits. The\par Government is not legally responsible for the debts of sub-federal and local\par governments in the absence of a Government guarantee. The aggregate principal\par amount of consolidated debt of Federation subjects and local authorities\par outstanding at 1 December 2009 is estimated at 936.9 billion roubles, or 2.4%\par of GDP.\par \par External Assets\par \par Following the dissolution of the Soviet Union, the Government\par assumed responsibility for virtually all the external debts contracted on\par behalf of the government of the former USSR pursuant to agreements signed with\par 11 of the other former Soviet republics. In return, Russia received the right\par to the republics' claims on the external assets of the former Soviet Union.\par These assets included claims on other countries, the majority of which are\par claims on less-developed countries. In most cases, the loans are\par non-performing. As of 1 January 2010, the value of these credits was estimated\par at U.S.$19.3 billion, including accrued interest. As of the same date, the\par total amount of Russia's external assets consisting of claims on credits\par provided to other countries by the government of the USSR or by the Government\par of the Russian Federation was estimated at U.S.$66.1 billion. The debt owed to\par the Russian Federation by CIS member states was equal U.S.$6.4 billion.\par \par In September 1997, Russia became a full member of the Paris Club of\par official creditors. As a result, the Government now settles its claims against\par debtor governments within the Paris Club framework. In connection with\par Russia's joining the Paris Club, the Government agreed to apply certain\par up-front discounts on its claims on Paris Club debtors with the\par understanding that claims denominated in Gosbank roubles and transferable\par roubles would be converted into dollars at the rate of 0.6 Gosbank rouble and\par one transferable rouble per dollar, respectively. In 2009, Russia received\par from its government debtors payments on claims restructured through the Paris\par Club totaling approximately U.S.$112.9 million, of which U.S.$50 million\par represented principal repayments and U.S.$62.9 million represented interest\par payments.\par \par Russia is a participant in the international initiative to\par alleviate the debt of highly indebted poor countries (the "HIPC Initiative").\par Under the HIPC Initiative, Russia has since 1999 cancelled 88.7%\par (approximately U.S.$10.7 billion) of the total amount of debt owed to Russia\par by African countries. As of\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 March 2010, within the framework of the HIPC Initiative, Russia has in the aggregate forgiven over U.S. $24 billion of debts of the world's poorest countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1 March 2010, claims on the 18 HIPC-eligible countries (excluding claims on Kyrgyzstan, which has not yet taken a decision to join the HIPC Initiative) amounted to approximately U.S. $2.5 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par At the 2005 G-8 summit, Russia announced its intention to grant up\par to 100% Paris Club debt relief to certain of its poorest debtors. Russia\par provided for this debt relief in its 2006 Budget Law and, in December 2006,\par the Government issued a statement to this effect. Pursuant to this statement,\par debt relief to HIPC-eligible countries outside the framework of the Paris Club\par will be provided through the so-called "debt-for-aid" mechanism. These\par initiatives have brought Russia's debt relief policy in line with that of\par other G-8 members and most of the members of the Paris Club. Moreover, Russia\par continued to grant debt relief to several of its largest debtors. In 2007,\par Russia relieved Afghanistan of more than U.S.$10 billion of its Soviet-era\par debt and agreed to restructure its remaining debt over a 23-year period. In\par February 2008, Russia agreed to forgive Iraq of approximately U.S.$12 billion\par of its Soviet-era debt as well as restructure the remaining U.S.$900 million\par (excluding capitalised interest) over a 17-year period. Most recently, in\par April 2008, Russia agreed to cancel the total amount of Libya's Soviet-era\par debt (approximately U.S.$4.5 billion) in exchange for the purchase of certain\par Russian goods and services.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Relations with International Financial Institutions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government has in the past received financial and technical assistance from various international financial institutions, including the IMF, the World Bank (the IBRD and IFC) and the EBRD.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In January 2005, Russia fully prepaid all of its indebtedness to the IMF.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Through year-end 2009, the IBRD has signed 62 loan agreements with\par Russia (of which 13 are still active) in the amount of U.S.$10.3 billion,\par directed primarily to infrastructure development and industrial restructuring.\par Under the IBRD loans, U.S.$9.7 billion has been disbursed, of which U.S.$0.6\par billion represents active projects and U.S.$9.1 billion represents completed\par projects. Approximately U.S.$6.0 of the U.S.$9.7 billion has been repaid. In\par 2009, the IBRD extended one loan to Russia in the amount of U.S.$200 million\par for housing reform. Disbursements of up to U.S.$0.3 billion are envisaged for\par 2010, aimed at modernising the treasury system, the information systems of\par customs organs and the tax service, and the meteorological service, developing\par the capital markets, improving public sector management, reforming the\par judicial system and mitigating social and environmental risks. In addition,\par the IFC has financed 118 private sector projects amounting to U.S.$2.3\par billion, which were implemented without recourse to borrowings on behalf of\par the Russian Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The EBRD has signed 14 loan agreements with the Russian Federation amounting to U.S. $1.2 billion. In addition, the EBRD has financed approximately 585 private sector investment projects in Russia amounting to \u8364\'20\'AC12.2 billion and relating to the financial sector, industry, agriculture and infrastructure development.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                                    TAXATION\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following discussion summarises certain Russian tax and United States federal income tax considerations that may be relevant to Bondholders. It also includes a limited discussion of certain European Union tax considerations. This summary is based on laws, regulations, rulings and decisions now in effect and is subject to changes in tax law, including changes that could have a retroactive effect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par This summary does not describe all of the tax considerations that\par may be relevant to holders of Bonds, particularly Bondholders subject to\par special tax rules. Bondholders are advised to consult their own professional\par tax advisors as to the consequences under the tax laws of the country of which\par they are resident of purchasing Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian Taxation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The following is a summary of certain Russian tax considerations\par relevant to the acquisition, ownership and disposal of Bonds. This summary is\par based on the tax laws of the Russian Federation and official clarifications of\par those laws in effect on the date of this Prospectus.\par \par Unless specifically indicated otherwise, this summary is limited to\par the tax treatment of Bonds that are held by foreign legal entities and\par organisations, otherwise than through a permanent establishment in the Russian\par Federation (a "non-resident holder"). A legal entity or organisation is a\par foreign legal entity or organisation if not organised under Russian law, and a\par legal entity or organisation will generally not be deemed to have a permanent\par establishment in the Russian Federation if it does not have a branch,\par representative office, division, office, bureau, agency or other separate\par division or place of economic activity through which it conducts business on a\par regular basis within the Russian Federation. A Bondholder will not be deemed\par to have a permanent establishment in Russia solely by virtue of having\par acquired the Bonds.\par \par This summary does not address the availability of, or procedures\par for claiming, double tax treaty relief, the practical difficulties involved in\par claiming such relief, or the applicability of, or procedures in relation to,\par taxes levied by regions, municipalities or other non-federal level authorities\par of the Russian Federation.\par \par The substantive provisions of Russian tax law applicable to\par financial instruments and the interpretation and application of those\par provisions by the Russian tax authorities are not clear in all respects and\par are subject to significant uncertainty. The interpretation and application of\par these provisions will in practice rest largely with local tax inspectorates,\par and are subject to more rapid and unpredictable change and to greater\par inconsistency than is generally the case in jurisdictions with more developed\par capital markets.\par \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is officially authorised to issue\par clarifications of Russia's tax laws, and its clarifications are binding on\par Russia's tax authorities, including Russia's Federal Tax Service, the federal\par authority responsible for the collection of Russian taxes, to the extent not\par inconsistent with the Tax Code. On 16 April 2010, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par issued Letter No. 03-00-08/61, addressed to the Russian Federal Tax\par Service (the "Tax Letter"), providing clarifications to be followed by the tax\par authorities in the taxation of the Bonds. None of the clarifications included\par in the Tax Letter are inconsistent with the Tax Code.\par \par Prospective investors should consult their own tax advisors with\par respect to the tax consequences of the acquisition, ownership and disposal of\par Bonds and the application of the Tax Letter to the tax treatment of the Bonds.\par No representation is made with respect to the Russian tax consequences of any\par particular Bondholder's acquisition, ownership or disposal of Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Interest on Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Tax Letter states that no tax is required to be withheld on\par interest payable on Bonds held by a non-resident holder because tax is\par required to be withheld only by an organisation within the meaning of the Tax\par Code that pays income in the form of interest, and (i) the Russian Federation,\par the issuer of the Bonds, is not an organisation for these purposes, and (ii)\par none of the other parties participating in the transfer of interest payments\par (including the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the banks making payments for the account\par of the Russian Federation, the fiscal agent, the paying agents and the\par depositaries for the Bonds) is (a) deemed to be paying interest on the Bonds\par and (b) is required to withhold tax on the interest payments made on the\par Bonds.\par \par Condition 8 of the Terms and Conditions of the 2015 Bonds and the\par Terms and Conditions of the 2020 Bonds requires the Russian Federation to\par increase the payment of principal or interest made in respect of the Bonds in\par the event any Taxes (as defined in Condition 8) are withheld or deducted,\par subject to the exceptions therein provided. There has been some uncertainty as\par to whether the payment of additional amounts to a lender in the event Russian\par tax is withheld from payments made to that lender is consistent with Russian\par law. The Tax Letter states that the payment of such additional amounts on the\par Bonds will not contradict the Tax Code.\par \par The Tax Letter further states that if Bonds are sold or disposed of\par between Bond interest payment dates by a non-resident holder (i) to a\par Russian legal entity or to an individual who is tax resident in the Russian\par Federation, (ii) to a foreign legal entity that has a permanent establishment\par in the Russian Federation, provided that such permanent establishment is a\par party to the agreement, (iii) through a Russian licensed professional\par participant in the securities market, or (iv) through an agreement made in the\par territory of the Russian Federation, then the proceeds attributable to any\par accrued but unpaid interest will be treated as Russian source income.\par \par Under these circumstances, the purchaser of the Bonds may be\par required to withhold 15% of the purchase price attributable to the accrued but\par unpaid interest from the proceeds payable to the seller. The taxation of\par accrued interest under these circumstances may give rise to a tax liability\par even if the seller realises a capital loss on the disposal of the Bonds.\par Depending on the residence of the non-resident holder, any tax withheld in\par respect of accrued interest may potentially be reduced or eliminated under the\par terms of an applicable double tax treaty.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Disposal or Redemption of Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gain on the sale, redemption or disposal of Bonds by a non-resident holder from a source within the Russian Federation will not be subject to withholding tax except to the extent attributable to interest accrued but not paid on the Bonds between interest payment dates, as discussed above.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other Taxes\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Except as described in this Prospectus, including as set forth\par below under "General Information" (i) no federal stamp, registration,\par documentary or similar federal taxes are payable in the Russian Federation by\par reason of the issue of the Bonds or in relation to any enforcement proceedings\par in respect of the Bonds brought in Russian courts, (ii) non-resident holders\par of Bonds will not incur any federal tax on income or capital gain, stamp duty,\par registration, transfer or other similar federal taxes by reason only of the\par acquisition, ownership or disposal of Bonds and (iii) all payments by the\par Russian Federation of principal and interest on the Bonds to non-resident\par holders may be made without withholding or deduction for or on account of any\par other federal taxes, duties, assessments or governmental charges in the\par Russian Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 United States Taxation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par TO ENSURE COMPLIANCE WITH U.S. TREASURY DEPARTMENT CIRCULAR 230,\par HOLDERS ARE HEREBY NOTIFIED THAT: (A) ANY DISCUSSION OF UNITED STATES FEDERAL\par TAX ISSUES IN THIS PROSPECTUS IS NOT INTENDED OR WRITTEN TO BE RELIED UPON,\par AND CANNOT BE RELIED UPON, BY HOLDERS FOR THE PURPOSE OF AVOIDING PENALTIES\par THAT MAY BE IMPOSED ON HOLDERS UNDER THE INTERNAL REVENUE CODE; (B) SUCH\par DISCUSSION IS INCLUDED HEREIN BY THE ISSUER IN CONNECTION WITH THE PROMOTION\par OR MARKETING (WITHIN THE MEANING OF CIRCULAR 230) BY THE ISSUER OF THE\par TRANSACTIONS OR MATTERS ADDRESSED HEREIN; AND (C) HOLDERS SHOULD SEEK ADVICE\par BASED ON THEIR PARTICULAR CIRCUMSTANCES FROM AN INDEPENDENT TAX ADVISOR.\par \par The following summary contains a description of certain U.S.\par federal income tax consequences of the purchase, ownership and disposition of\par the Bonds by a U.S. holder (as defined below). This summary addresses only\par U.S. holders that purchase Bonds at their issue price as part of the initial\par offering and that hold Bonds as capital assets for U.S. federal income tax\par purposes. It does not purport to be a comprehensive description of all tax\par considerations that may be relevant to a particular investor, and we have\par assumed that U.S. holders are familiar with the tax rules applicable to\par investments in securities generally and with any special rules to which they\par may be subject. In particular, this summary does not address considerations\par that may be relevant to an investor that may be subject to special tax rules,\par such as a bank, thrift, real estate investment trust, regulated investment\par company, insurance company, tax-exempt organisation, dealer in securities or\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , trader in securities or commodities that elects mark-to\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 market treatment, person that will hold the Bonds as a hedge against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk or as a position in a "straddle" or conversion transaction, person subject to the alternative minimum tax, or person whose "functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " is not the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This summary is based upon the U.S. Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as amended (the "Code"), Treasury regulations issued thereunder and judicial and administrative interpretations thereof, each as in effect on the date hereof, as well as on the income tax treaty between the United States and Russia (the "Treaty"), all of which are subject to change, possibly with retroactive effect. Furthermore, there can be no assurance that the U.S. Internal Revenue Service (the "IRS") will not assert a position contrary to those discussed herein.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As used herein, a "U.S. holder" means a beneficial owner of a Bond\par that is, for U.S. federal income tax purposes, (i) a citizen or resident of\par the United States; (ii) a corporation, or any entity treated as a corporation\par for U.S. federal income tax purposes, created or organised in the United\par States or under the laws of the United States or of any political subdivision\par thereof; (iii) any estate the income of which is subject to U.S. federal\par income taxation regardless of its source; and (iv) any trust if a court within\par the United States is able to exercise primary supervision over the\par administration of the trust and one or more U.S. fiduciaries have the\par authority to control all substantial decisions of the trust, or if a valid\par election is in place to treat the trust as a United States person. A "non-U.S.\par holder" is a beneficial owner of the Bonds (other than a partnership) that is\par not a U.S. holder.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If a partnership (or other entity treated as a partnership for U.S. federal income tax purposes) holds the Bonds, then the tax treatment of a partner in such partnership generally will depend upon the status of the partner and the activities of the partnership. Such a partner or partnership should consult its own tax advisor as to the tax consequences of acquiring, owning and disposing of the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors should consult their own tax advisors regarding the tax consequences of the acquisition, ownership and disposition of the Bonds, including the application to their particular circumstances of the tax considerations discussed below, as well as the application of U.S. federal estate and gift tax laws, U.S. state and local tax laws, and foreign tax laws.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payments of Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Payments of interest on the Bonds will be taxable to a U.S. holder\par as ordinary interest income at the time that such payments accrue or are\par received, in accordance with the U.S. holder's method of accounting for U.S.\par federal income tax purposes. In the event that a Russian withholding tax is\par imposed on the interest payments, then in addition to interest on the Bonds, a\par U.S. holder would be required to include in income any increased amounts\par received (as described under "Terms and Conditions of the 2015\par Bonds--Taxation" and "Terms and Conditions of the 2020 Bonds--Taxation") and\par any Russian tax withheld from interest payments notwithstanding that the tax\par withheld was not in fact received by such U.S. holder.\par \par A U.S. holder may be entitled to deduct or credit such Russian\par withholding tax, subject to applicable limitations and conditions under U.S.\par federal income tax laws. However, the election to deduct or credit foreign\par taxes applies to all of the U.S. holder's foreign taxes for a particular\par taxable year. U.S. holders that are eligible for the benefits under the Treaty\par will not be entitled to a foreign tax credit for the amount of any Russian\par taxes withheld in excess of the reduced Treaty rate and for which the holder\par can obtain a refund from the Russian tax authorities, even though a U.S.\par holder may be required to pay the amount of such refund to the Issuer.\par Payments of interest on the Bonds generally will constitute foreign source\par `passive category income" for U.S. foreign tax credit purposes. The\par calculation and availability of foreign tax credits and, in the case of a U.S.\par holder that elects to deduct foreign taxes, the availability of such deduction\par involves the application of complex rules that depend on a U.S. holder's\par particular circumstances. U.S. holders should consult their own tax advisors\par regarding the availability of foreign tax credits.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dispositions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par A U.S. holder generally will recognise gain or loss on the sale,\par exchange, redemption or other disposition of the Bonds in an amount equal to\par the difference between the amount realised on such disposition (less any\par amounts attributable to accrued but unpaid interest, which will be taxable as\par interest income to the extent not previously included in income) and the U.S.\par holder's adjusted tax basis in the Bonds. A U.S. holder's adjusted tax basis\par in a Bond generally will equal its initial investment in that Bond. Gain or\par loss realised by a U.S. holder on a sale, redemption or other disposition of\par the Bonds generally will\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 be capital gain or loss, and generally will be long-term capital gain or loss if, at the time of the disposition, the Bonds have been held for more than one year. The deduction of capital losses is subject to limitations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Gain or loss realised by a U.S. holder on the sale, exchange,\par retirement or other disposition of a Bond generally will be U.S.-source gain\par or loss for U.S. federal income tax purposes. Accordingly, if Russian tax is\par imposed on the sale or disposition of the Bonds, a U.S. holder may not be able\par to fully utilise its U.S. foreign tax credits in respect of such tax unless\par such U.S. holder has other foreign-source income. Prospective investors\par should consult their own tax advisors as to the U.S. tax and foreign tax\par credit implications of such sale, exchange, retirement or other disposition\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of\par a Bond.\par \par Non-U.S. Holders\par \par A non-U.S. holder generally will not be subject to U.S. federal\par income tax (including withholding tax) on payments of interest on the Bonds.\par In addition, a non-U.S. holder generally will not be subject to U.S. federal\par income tax on gain realised on the sale, exchange, redemption or other\par disposition of the Bonds. U.S. federal income tax will apply to such interest\par and gain, however, to the extent that such income is effectively connected\par with the conduct of a U.S. trade or business by such non-U.S. holder (subject\par to the provisions of an applicable income tax treaty); furthermore, gain\par realised by an individual non-U.S. holder will be subject to U.S. federal\par income taxation if such holder is present in the United States for 183 days or\par more in the taxable year of the disposition and certain other conditions are\par met.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Information Reporting and Backup Withholding\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Payments in respect of the Bonds that are paid within the United\par States or through certain U.S.-related financial intermediaries are\par generally subject to information reporting, unless the U.S. holder is a\par corporation or other exempt recipient. Such payments to a non-exempt recipient\par may also be subject to backup withholding, unless the U.S. holder provides a\par taxpayer identification number and certifies that it has not lost its\par exemption from backup withholding. The amount of any backup withholding\par collected from a payment to a U.S. holder will be allowed as a credit against\par the U.S. holder's U.S. federal income tax liability and may entitle the U.S.\par holder to a refund, provided that certain required information is furnished to\par the IRS. A non-U.S. holder generally will not be subject to information\par reporting or backup withholding, but such a holder may have to comply with\par certification procedures to establish that it is not a United States person.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Legislation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Under recent legislation, individual U.S. holders (and certain U.S.\par entities that may be specified in future IRS guidance) who, during any taxable\par year, hold any interest in any "specified foreign financial asset" generally\par will be required to file with their U.S. federal income tax returns a\par statement setting forth certain information if the aggregate value of all such\par assets exceeds $50,000. A "specified foreign financial asset" generally\par includes any financial account maintained with a non-U.S. financial\par institution and will likely include the Bonds if they are not held in an\par account maintained with a U.S. financial institution. A U.S. holder who fails\par to file any such form could be required to pay a penalty of U.S.$10,000, or a\par penalty of up to $50,000 for ongoing failure to file. U.S. holders should\par consult their own tax advisors with respect to these or any other reporting\par requirements that may apply with respect to the Bonds.\par \par In addition, newly enacted legislation requires certain U.S.\par holders who are individuals, estates or trusts to pay an additional tax on,\par among other things, interest on and capital gains from the sale or other\par disposition of debt securities for taxable years beginning after 31 December\par 2012. U.S. holders should consult their tax advisors regarding the effect, if\par any, of this legislation on their ownership and disposition of the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EU Savings Directive\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Under Council Directive 2003/48/EC on the taxation of savings\par income (the "EU Savings Directive"), Member States are required to provide to\par the tax authorities of another Member State details of payments of interest\par and other similar income paid by a person within its jurisdiction to or for an\par individual resident or certain limited types of entity established in that\par other Member State. However, for a transitional period Austria and Luxembourg\par are instead required (unless during such period they elect otherwise) to\par operate a withholding tax in relation to such payments (except that no\par withholding will be required where the beneficial owner of the payment\par authorises the person making the payment to report the payment or presents a\par certificate from the relevant tax authority establishing exemption therefrom).\par The current rate\par \par of withholding is 20% and it will be increased to 35% with effect\par from 1 July 2011. Belgium has replaced this withholding tax with information\par reporting from 1 January 2010. The transitional period will end after\par agreement on exchange of information is reached between the European Union and\par certain non-European Union states.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A number of non-EU countries (including Switzerland) and certain dependent or associated territories of certain Member States have adopted measures equivalent to the EU Savings Directive (a withholding system in the case of Switzerland).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On 13 November 2008, the European Commission published a proposal to amend the EU Savings Directive. The proposal has been considered by the European Parliament and is under discussion by the European Council. If adopted, the proposal may amend or broaden the scope of the requirements described above.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                         FORM AND TRANSFER OF THE BONDS\par \par Form of Bonds\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All Bonds will be in definitive registered form, without interest coupons attached.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Unrestricted Bonds which are offered and sold in reliance on\par Regulation S will be represented by beneficial interests in the Unrestricted\par Global Bonds in registered form without interest coupons attached, which will\par be registered in the name of a nominee for, and shall be deposited on or about\par the Closing Date with a common depositary for, and in respect of interests\par held through, Euroclear and Clearstream, Luxembourg.\par \par Restricted Bonds that are offered and sold in reliance on Rule 144A\par will be represented by beneficial interests in the Restricted Global Bonds in\par registered form without interest coupons attached, which will be deposited on\par or about the Closing Date with the Custodian for, and registered in the name\par of Cede & Co. as nominee for, DTC.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beneficial interests in the Global Bonds will be shown on, and transfers thereof will be effected only through, records maintained by DTC, Euroclear and Clearstream, Luxembourg and their participants.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Restricted Global Bonds and any Bond Certificate (as defined\par below) issued in exchange therefor (a "Restricted Bond Certificate") will be\par subject to certain restrictions on transfer contained in a legend appearing on\par the face of such Bonds set forth below.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Unrestricted Global Bonds and the Restricted Global Bonds will have separate CUSIP and ISIN numbers and separate Common Codes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All Bonds will initially be in the form of the Unrestricted Global Bonds and/or the Restricted Global Bonds. Bond Certificates will only be available in certain limited circumstances described below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exchange of Interests in Global Bonds for Bond Certificates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Registration of title to Bonds initially represented by the\par Restricted Global Bonds in a name other than DTC or a successor depositary or\par one of their respective nominees will not be permitted in respect of the Bonds\par unless (a) such depositary notifies the Russian Federation that it is no\par longer willing or able to discharge properly its responsibilities as\par depositary with respect to the Global Bonds or ceases to be a "clearing\par agency" registered under the United States Securities Exchange Act of 1934\par (the "Exchange Act"), or is at any time no longer eligible to act as such, and\par the Russian Federation is unable to locate a qualified successor within 90\par days of receiving notice of such ineligibility on the part of such depositary,\par or (b) following a failure to pay principal in respect of the Bonds that is\par due and payable, the Fiscal Agent has received notice from the registered\par holder of any Global Bond requesting the exchange of such Global Bond for\par individual Restricted Bond Certificates.\par \par Registration of title to Bonds initially represented by the\par Unrestricted Global Bonds in a name other than the common depositary for\par Euroclear and Clearstream or any nominee of such common depositary will not be\par permitted in respect of the Bonds unless (a) Euroclear or Clearstream is\par closed for business for a continuous period of 14 days (other than by reason\par of legal holidays) or announces an intention permanently to cease business or\par (b) following a failure to pay principal in respect of the Bonds that is due\par and payable, the Fiscal Agent has received a notice from the registered holder\par of the Unrestricted Global Bonds requesting the exchange of the Unrestricted\par Global Bonds for individual bond certificates (the "Unrestricted Bond\par Certificates" and, together with the Restricted Bond Certificates, the "Bond\par Certificates").\par \par In such circumstances, the relevant Global Bonds will be exchanged\par in full or in part, as the case may be, for Bond Certificates and the Russian\par Federation will, at the cost of the Russian Federation (but against such\par indemnity as the Registrar or any relevant Transfer Agent may require in\par respect of any tax or other duty of whatever nature which may be levied or\par imposed in connection with such exchange), cause sufficient Bond Certificates\par to be executed and delivered to the Registrar for completion, authentication\par and dispatch to the relevant holders of the Bonds. A person having an interest\par in the Global Bonds must provide the Registrar with (a) a written order\par containing instructions and such other information as the Russian Federation\par and the Registrar may require to complete, execute and deliver such Bond\par Certificates and (b) in the case of the Restricted Global Bonds only, a fully\par completed, signed certificate substantially to the effect that the exchanging\par holder is not transferring its interest at the time of such exchange or, in\par the case of a simultaneous sale pursuant to Rule 144A, that the transfer is\par being made in compliance with\par \par the provisions of Rule 144A. Bond Certificates issued in exchange\par for a beneficial interest in the Restricted Global Bonds will bear the legends\par applicable to transfers pursuant to Rule 144A, as set out under "Transfer\par Restrictions" herein. Such transfer restrictions will terminate with respect\par to the Bonds one year (or such other period as provided by Rule 144) after the\par date on which Bonds represented by an interest in the Restricted Global Bonds\par were issued, assuming compliance with Condition 6(b) of the Terms and\par Conditions of the 2015 Bonds or the Terms and Conditions of the 2020 Bonds, as\par applicable.\par \par The holder of a Bond may transfer such Bond in accordance with the\par provisions of Condition 2 of the Terms and Conditions of the 2015 Bonds or the\par Terms and Conditions of the 2020 Bonds, as applicable. Bond Certificates may\par not be eligible for trading in the DTC, Euroclear or Clearstream systems.\par \par If principal in respect of any Bonds represented by the\par Unrestricted Global Bonds is not paid when due and payable (but subject as\par provided below), the registered holder of such Unrestricted Global Bonds may\par from time to time elect that direct enforcement rights ("Direct Rights")\par against the Russian Federation will come into effect. Thereupon, each relevant\par holder of an account with Euroclear or Clearstream shall, at the relevant\par time, acquire against the Russian Federation all rights (including the right\par to receive payments due on the Bonds) that such accountholder would have\par received if it had been the holder of Bond Certificates. Such election shall\par be made by the holder of the Unrestricted Global Bonds by notice to the\par Registrar and presentation of each Unrestricted Global Bond to or to the order\par of the Registrar whereupon the Registrar shall reduce the principal amount of\par Bonds represented by such Unrestricted Global Bond entered on the Register and\par shall make the appropriate entry or entries on the Register to reflect that\par such Direct Rights have come into effect. Such Direct Rights may not be\par eligible for trading in the Euroclear or Clearstream systems.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No Direct Rights election may be made on or before an Exchange Date (as defined in the Unrestricted Global Bonds) unless the registered holder elects in such notice that the exchange in question shall no longer take place.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par At the same time that each relevant holder of an account at\par Euroclear or Clearstream acquires Direct Rights, each holder of a beneficial\par interest in the Restricted Global Bonds shall have all rights against the\par Russian Federation which such holder of a beneficial interest would have had\par if it were the holder of a Restricted Bond Certificate issued immediately\par before the relevant time in an aggregate principal amount equal to such\par holder's beneficial interest in the Restricted Global Bonds, including,\par without limitation, the right to receive all payments due at any time in\par respect of the Restricted Bond Certificate other than payments corresponding\par to payments already made pursuant to the Restricted Global Bonds.\par \par Upon the transfer, exchange or replacement of a Restricted Bond\par Certificate bearing the legend referred to under "Transfer Restrictions," or\par upon specific request for removal of the legend on a Restricted Bond\par Certificate, the Russian Federation will deliver only Restricted Bond\par Certificates that bear such legend, or will refuse to remove such legend, as\par the case may be, unless there is delivered to the Russian Federation and the\par Registrar such satisfactory evidence, which may include an opinion of counsel,\par as may reasonably be required by the Russian Federation that neither the\par legend nor the restrictions on transfer set forth therein are required to\par ensure compliance with the provisions of the Securities Act. Such transfer\par restrictions will terminate with respect to the Bonds one year (or such other\par period as provided by Rule 144) after the date on which the Bonds represented\par by an interest in the Restricted Global Bonds were issued, assuming compliance\par with Condition 6(b) of the Terms and Conditions of the 2015 Bonds or the Terms\par and Conditions of the 2020 Bonds, as applicable.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Registrar will not register the transfer of or exchange of interests in Global Bonds for Bond Certificates during the three Business Days ending on the due date for any payment of principal of the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DTC, Euroclear and Clearstream Arrangements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as DTC or its nominee or Euroclear, Clearstream or the\par common depositary or any nominee of such common depositary is the registered\par holder of a Global Bond, DTC, Euroclear or Clearstream, the common depositary\par or such nominee, as the case may be, will be considered the sole owner or\par holder of the Bonds represented by such Global Bond for the purposes of the\par Fiscal Agency Agreements and the Bonds. Payments of principal, interest and\par additional amounts, if any, in respect of Global Bonds will be made to DTC,\par Euroclear, Clearstream or such common depositary or nominee, as the case may\par be, as the registered holder thereof. Neither the Russian Federation nor any\par affiliate controlled by it for the purposes of the Securities Act will have\par any responsibility or liability for any aspect of the records relating to or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 payments made on account of beneficial ownership interests in Global Bonds or for maintaining, supervising or reviewing any records relating to such beneficial ownership interests.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Holders of book-entry interests in Bonds held through DTC will receive from the Fiscal Agent through DTC, to the extent received by DTC from the Fiscal Agent, all distributions of principal and interest made with respect to book-entry interests in such Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Distributions of principal and interest with respect to book-entry\par interests in Bonds held through Euroclear or Clearstream will be credited, to\par the extent received by Euroclear or Clearstream from the Fiscal Agent, to the\par cash accounts of Euroclear or Clearstream customers in accordance with the\par relevant system's rules and procedures.\par \par Interest on the Bonds (other than interest on redemption) will be\par paid to the holder shown on the register maintained by the Registrar on the\par Clearing System Business Day immediately before the due date for such payment\par so long as the Bonds are represented by Global Bonds, and on the fifteenth day\par before the due date for such payment if the Bonds are in the form of Bond\par Certificates (the "Record Date"). Trading of Bonds will therefore be net of\par accrued interest from the relevant Record Date to the relevant interest\par payment date. "Clearing System Business Day" means Monday to Friday inclusive\par except 25 December and 1 January.\par \par The laws of some states of the United States require that certain\par persons take physical delivery of securities in definitive form. Consequently,\par the ability to transfer interests in Global Bonds to such persons may be\par limited. Because DTC, Euroclear and Clearstream can only act on behalf of\par direct and indirect participants, the ability of a person having an interest\par in Global Bonds to pledge such interest to persons or entities which do not\par participate in the relevant clearing system, or otherwise take actions in\par respect of such interest, may be affected by the lack of a physical\par certificate in respect of such interest.\par \par The holdings of book-entry interests in the Bonds through DTC,\par Euroclear and Clearstream will be reflected in the book-entry accounts of each\par institution. The Registrar will adjust the amounts of Bonds on the Register as\par necessary for the accounts of (a) Cede & Co. and (b) Citivic Nominees Limited\par to reflect the amounts of Bonds held through DTC, Euroclear and Clearstream,\par respectively. Beneficial ownership in Bonds will be held through financial\par institutions as direct and indirect participants in DTC, Euroclear and\par Clearstream.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Interests in the Global Bonds will be in uncertificated book-entry form.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trading between Euroclear and/or Clearstream Accountholders\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Secondary market sales of book-entry interests in Bonds held through Euroclear or Clearstream to purchasers of book-entry interests in Bonds through Euroclear or Clearstream will be conducted in accordance with the normal rules and operating procedures of Euroclear and Clearstream and will be settled using the procedures applicable to conventional eurobonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trading between DTC Participants\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Secondary market sales of book-entry interests in Bonds between DTC participants will occur in the ordinary way in accordance with DTC rules and will be settled using the procedures applicable to U.S. corporate debt obligations in DTC's Same Day Funds Settlement System.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trading between DTC Seller and Euroclear/Clearstream Purchaser\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par When a book-entry interest in Bonds is to be transferred from the\par account of a DTC participant holding a beneficial interest in the Restricted\par Global Bonds to the account of a Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder\par wishing to purchase a beneficial interest in the Unrestricted Global Bonds\par (subject to such certification procedures as are provided in the Fiscal Agency\par Agreements), the purchaser must send instructions to Euroclear or Clearstream\par at least one business day prior to the settlement date. Euroclear or\par Clearstream, as the case may be, will instruct the common depositary to\par receive the beneficial interest and make payment for it. Payment will include\par interest accrued on the beneficial interest in the Bonds from and including\par the last interest payment date to and excluding the settlement date. On the\par settlement date, the common depositary will make payment to the DTC\par participant's account against delivery of the beneficial interest. After\par settlement has been completed, the beneficial interest will be credited to the\par respective clearing system, and by the clearing system, in accordance with its\par usual procedures, to the Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder's account. The\par securities credit will appear the next day,\par \par European time. The cash debit will be back-valued to, and interest\par on the Unrestricted Global Bonds will accrue from, the value date, which will\par be the preceding day when settlement occurs in New York. If settlement is not\par completed on the intended value date, that is, if the trade fails, the\par Euroclear or Clearstream cash debit will be valued instead as of the actual\par settlement date, whenever that may be.\par \par The Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder will need to make\par available to its clearing system the funds necessary to process same-day funds\par settlement. The most direct means of doing so is to pre-position funds for\par settlement, either from cash on hand or existing lines of credit, as it would\par for any pre-settlement occurring within Euroclear or Clearstream. Under this\par approach, the purchasing accountholder may take on credit exposure to\par Euroclear or Clearstream until the beneficial interest in the Unrestricted\par Global Bonds is credited to its account one day later. As an alternative, if\par Euroclear or Clearstream has extended a line of credit to the purchasing\par accountholder, it can elect not to pre-position funds and allow that credit\par line to be drawn upon to finance settlement. Under this procedure, the\par Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder purchasing the beneficial interest in\par the Unrestricted Global Bonds would incur overdraft charges for one day,\par assuming it cleared the overdraft when the beneficial interest was credited to\par its account. However, interest on the Unrestricted Global Bonds would accrue\par from the value date. Therefore, in many cases, the investment income on the\par Unrestricted Global Bonds earned during that one-day period may substantially\par reduce or offset the amount of such overdraft charges, although this result\par will depend on each accountholder's particular cost of funds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because the settlement is taking place during New York business hours, the DTC participant can use its usual procedures for transferring a beneficial interest in the Global Bonds to the common depositary for the benefit of the Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder. The sale proceeds will be available to the DTC seller on the settlement date. Thus, to the DTC participant, a cross-market transaction will settle no differently than a trade between two DTC participants.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Day traders that use Clearstream or Euroclear to purchase interests\par in the Bonds from DTC participants for delivery to Clearstream participants or\par Euroclear participants should note that these trades will automatically fail\par on the sale side unless affirmative action is taken. At least three techniques\par should be readily available to eliminate this potential problem:\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Borrowing through Clearstream or Euroclear for one day, until the purchase side of the day trade is reflected in their Clearstream or Euroclear accounts, in accordance with the clearing system's customary procedures; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Borrowing the interests in the United States from a DTC participant no later than one day prior to settlement, which will give the interests sufficient time to be reflected in their Clearstream or Euroclear account in order to settle the sale side of the trade; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par - Staggering the value date for the buy and sell sides of the trade\par so that the value date for the purchase from the DTC participant is at least\par one day prior to the value date for the sale to the Clearstream participant or\par Euroclear participant.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trading between Euroclear/Clearstream Seller and DTC Purchaser\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Due to time zone differences in its favour, a Euroclear or\par Clearstream accountholder may employ customary transfer procedures when\par transferring a book-entry interest in the Unrestricted Global Bonds to the\par account of a DTC participant wishing to purchase a beneficial interest in\par Restricted Global Bonds (subject to such certification procedures as are\par provided in the Fiscal Agency Agreements). The seller must send instructions\par to Euroclear or Clearstream at least one business day prior to the settlement\par date. Euroclear or Clearstream will instruct the common depositary to credit\par the beneficial interest in the Global Bonds to the DTC participant's account\par and receive payment. Payment will include interest accrued on the beneficial\par interest in the Bonds from and including the last interest payment date to and\par excluding the settlement date. Payment will be reflected in the account of the\par Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder the following day. Receipt of cash\par proceeds in the Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder's account will be\par back-valued to the value date, which will be the preceding day, when\par settlement occurs in New York. If the Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder\par has a line of credit with its clearing system and elects to draw on such line\par of credit in anticipation of receipt of sale proceeds in its account, the\par back-valuation may substantially reduce or offset any overdraft charges\par incurred over that one-day period. If settlement is not completed on the\par intended value date, that is, if the trade fails, receipt of the cash proceeds\par in the Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder's account will instead be valued\par as of the actual settlement date, whenever that may be.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For a further description of restrictions on the transfer of Bonds, see "Transfer Restrictions" below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par DTC has advised the Russian Federation that it will take any action\par permitted to be taken by a holder of Bonds (including, without limitation, the\par presentation of Global Bonds for exchange as described above) only at the\par direction of one or more participants in whose account with DTC interests in\par Global Bonds are credited and only in respect of such portion of the aggregate\par principal amount of the relevant Global Bonds as to which such participant or\par participants has or have given such direction. In the circumstances described\par above, DTC will surrender the Global Bonds for exchange for individual Bond\par Certificates, which will, in the case of Restricted Bond Certificates, bear\par the legend applicable to transfers pursuant to Rule 144A.\par \par DTC has advised the Russian Federation as follows: DTC is a\par limited-purpose trust company organised under the laws of the State of New\par York, a member of the United States Federal Reserve System, a "clearing\par corporation" within the meaning of the New York Uniform Commercial Code and a\par "clearing agency" registered pursuant to the provisions of Section 17A of the\par Exchange Act. DTC was created to hold securities for its participants and to\par facilitate the clearance settlement of transactions between its participants\par through electronic book-entry changes in accounts of its participants, thereby\par eliminating the need for physical movement of certificates. DTC participants\par include securities brokers and dealers, banks, trust companies and clearing\par corporations and may in the future include certain other organisations.\par Indirect access to the DTC system is also available to banks, brokers, dealers\par and trust companies that clear through, or maintain a custodial relationship\par with, a participant, either directly or indirectly. DTC is owned by a number\par of its direct participants and by the New York Stock Exchange, Inc ., the\par American Stock Exchange, Inc. and the National Association of Securities\par Dealers.\par \par Euroclear and Clearstream have advised the Russian Federation as\par follows: Euroclear and Clearstream hold securities for participating\par organisations and facilitate the clearance and settlement of securities\par between their respective accountholders through electronic book-entry changes\par in accounts of such accountholders. Euroclear and Clearstream provide to their\par accountholders, among other things, services for safekeeping, administration,\par clearance and settlement of internationally traded securities and securities\par lending and borrowing. Euroclear and Clearstream interface with domestic\par securities markets. Euroclear and Clearstream accountholders are financial\par institutions such as underwriters, securities brokers and dealers, banks,\par trust companies and certain other organisations. Indirect access to Euroclear\par or Clearstream is also available to others such as banks, brokers, dealers and\par trust companies that clear through or maintain a custodian relationship with a\par Euroclear or Clearstream accountholder, either directly or 