\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  To Step Up  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 271 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2009\par 17:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Adds details, context.) \par  \par    By Ira Iosebashvili \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plans to increase its use of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions to combat a tide of speculative capital flowing into Russia, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  First Deputy Chairman Alexei Ulyukayev said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be "more active" in using \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the ruble within the floating corridor of RUB35-RUB38 against a basket of dollars and euros, Ulyukayev said.\par \par \uc2 Like other emerging-market economies, Russia is grappling with a deluge of hot money attracted by its comparatively high interest rates, which are significantly above those found in developed nations.\par \par \uc2 Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut the refinancing rate by 400 basis points since April to a record low 9.0% and bought $16 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October and the first half of November, the ruble has kept on rising, appreciating by around 10% against the dollar since September.\par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev's comments failed to budge the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which closed down four kopecks against the dollar at RUB28.79.\par \par \uc2 Russia will continue cutting interest rates into next year, as there are no inflationary risks on the horizon, Ulyukayev said. Russian consumer prices edged up 0.1% in the week to Nov. 23, bringing inflation to 8.4% since January, data released by the Federal Statistics Service showed Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Inflation for next year will be "much lower" than 9%, Ulyukayev said.\par \par \uc2 -By Ira Iosebashvili, Dow Jones Newswires; +7 495 937 8445; ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 25, 2009 11:22 ET (16:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020091125e5bp004hf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
