\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Russia, Kazakhstan eye  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  action to stem rallies\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2009\par 17:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Russian, Kazakh \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed up by strong oil\par \par \uc2 * Russia considering soft measures to limit inflows\par \par \uc2 * Kazakhstan warns foreigners betting on tenge rally\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background, quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Yelena Fabrichnaya and Olzhas Auyezov\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW/ALMATY, Nov 18 (Reuters) - Russia and Kazakhstan on Wednesday stepped up rhetoric against speculative bets on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with Moscow eyeing new measures against capital inflows and Astana ready to crack down on foreign players.\par \par \uc2 Investors keen to cash in on the rally in oil prices [O/R] have rushed into the two energy-exporting neighbours, sending their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher and causing some concern that fragile economic recoveries could be compromised.\par \par \uc2 Russia has to date allowed the rouble to appreciate gradually to 35.03 versus a euro-dollar basket, its strongest since January and up 9 percent since September <RUS=MCX>.\par \par \uc2 But there are some signs officials are getting concerned about the inflow of hot money.\par \par \uc2 "I am against resuming capital control measures. At the same time, there are other measures, softer ones, which could slow down the inflow of speculative capital ... It is being discussed," Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman Sergei Ignatyev told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Measures could include monitoring external borrowing of state-controlled companies and re-introducing differentiated minimum reserve requirements for banks' liabilities in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus those in the rouble, he added. [ID:nLI559680]\par \par \uc2 Analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also step up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and press on with interest rate cuts in a bid to further restrain the rouble's rally. [ID:nLH521118]\par \par \uc2 FOREIGNERS\par \par \uc2 Kazakhstan has kept the tenge in a fairly narrow 145-155 range versus the dollar since February <KZT=>, but the rise in oil prices as well as the rally in the rouble are putting more appreciation pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We ... had to buy over $2 billion to stop the tenge from appreciating too fast," Kazakh \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Grigory Marchenko said on Wednesday, adding that about a quarter of that sum had come from foreign players.\par \par \uc2 "They (foreign players) will be able to earn some money," he said. "But we won't allow them to gain a lot." [ID:nLI22425]\par \par \uc2 Marchenko pledged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more if the tenge goes beyond 147.50 per dollar compared to 149.14 now and 150.69 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 In other emerging markets, the weak dollar has prompted countries to look for new ways to cushion their economies, with Brazil introducing a tax on foreign investment in its fixed income and stock markets. [ID:nN19264977]\par \par \uc2 Analysts have said Moscow is unlikely to follow in Brazil's footsteps, but could implement less radical measures.\par \par \uc2 Moscow has repeatedly pledged not to re-introduce capital controls -- scrapped in 2006 -- and resisted pressure to do so during the height of crisis-time capital flight.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Vladimir Putin has said that a lack of capital controls is key to attracting foreign investment into Russia, which is looking for fresh cash to help the economy recover from its first recession in a decade. [ID:nLI729685]\par \par \uc2 Russia hopes to raise $3 billion from privatisations next year and cut state share in the economy to 30 percent from 50 percent in the next few years. Analysts and investors say the country needs to improve its investment climate and the rule of law if it wants to attract long-term, strategic capital.\par \par \uc2 Kazakhstan has introduced legislation allowing it to enforce capital controls if deemed necessary [ID:nLA336688] but so far the authorities have not used the option. (Writing by Toni Vorobyova and Maria Golovnina; Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-KAZAKHSTAN/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091121e5bi000bn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
