\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW-IMF: \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  shd shift spending, limit  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2009\par 08:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats, without changes, story first published on Monday)\par \par \uc2 * IMF: \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to have 'fairly good' recovery in Q4 '09\par \par \uc2 * Stimulus should focus on social, infrastructure spending * Urges decisive moves to address bank capitalisation\par \par \uc2 * Rouble \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to avoid short-term swings\par \par \uc2 By Lidia Kelly\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Nov 9 (Reuters) - Russia needs to make its economic recovery more self-sustaining by shifting stimulus spending towards infrastructure and social needs and ensuring banks have enough capital, the IMF's senior representative in Moscow told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Odd Per Brekk also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was right to aspire to a policy of inflation targeting and should confine its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to smoothing out excessive short-term swings in the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "The economy has turned a corner," Brekk said in an interview. "It's driven by the recovery in the world economy, it's driven by the large budget stimulus and it is driven by a rebound in inventories ... Now, looking ahead, there is a question how sustained these factors will be."\par \par \uc2 Russia's gross domestic product grew by 0.6 percent during the third quarter, seasonally adjusted, ending a recession that wiped out more than tenth of its economy in year-on-year terms. [ID:nLO623617].\par \par \uc2 The IMF expects Russia's economy to shrink by 7.5 percent this year, milder than the government's 8.5 percent estimate.\par \par \uc2 "We're seeing a fairly good recovery continued also in the fourth quarter of 2009, but then as these factors (budget stimulus) become less potent, the economy will grow at a slower pace during 2010," Brekk said, estimating growth at around 1.5 percent next year.\par \par \uc2 This is more conservative than the consensus in a recent Reuters poll of economists for 3.1 percent growth next year. [ID:nLU728233].\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL SPENDING\par \par \uc2 Brekk reiterated the IMF's earlier praise for the government's overall response to the global economic crisis but said spending should have focused on social and infrastructure needs rather than on injecting liquidity into companies.\par \par \uc2 "You would get more boost to domestic demand and therefore more positive effect on growth," he said.\par \par \uc2 The IMF has previously recommended scaling back the fiscal stimulus to 2.5 percent of GDP from 5.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Social and infrastructure spending "will have a relatively larger effect on domestic demand in the short term and then they will self-correct, self-reverse once the economy starts gaining momentum on its own," he added.\par \par \uc2 The government should also "deal decisively" with the banking system to ensure it extends credit to companies and households, Brekk said.\par \par \uc2 "The main point is to make sure there is a clear picture of capitalisation needs first," he said. "The second step is to ensure that the necessary capital is actually injected."\par \par \uc2 ROUBLE SWINGS\par \par \uc2 Brekk said the IMF supports the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current managed appreciation of the rouble -- relatively high prices for Russia's oil exports [O/R] and increased optimism in global economic recovery have been pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up <RUB=>.\par \par \uc2 But he added the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to avoid excessive short-term swings in the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "If there are long-term fundamental changes in the underlying economy they should not try to resist that," he said. "If there are permanent changes in oil prices it may not be possible to resist changes in the real exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 He said it was more "productive" to think of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's goal of moving towards inflation targeting as a gradual process. However, "if you want to move to a formal inflation targeting framework ... that is not compatible with also trying to target the exchange rate," Brekk added.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting and writing by Lidia Kelly; Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA/IMF/ (REPEAT, INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091110e5ba000sh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
