\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Czech Ctrl Bk Chief To Talk In London\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Langley   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,156 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2009\par 05:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 0900/0700 Hungarian 2009 cereal crop. \par  \par 1000/0800 Hungarian government, trade unions, employers meet over 2010 budget, wages. \par  \par 1000/0800 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Zdenek Tuma to address banking conference in London. \par  \par  \par    MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Zdenek Tuma will speak at a finance conference in London Friday, where he's unlikely to comment in detail on monetary policy ahead of the next rate decision later this month but could offer his views on the recent appreciation of the koruna. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tuma is also likely to reiterate his view that Czech politicians should restrain their plans for expansionary budget spending and adopt spending austerity measures outlined by interim Finance Minister Eduard Janota.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In Budapest, the Hungarian Interest Coordination Council - comprising representatives of the government, trade unions and employers - meets Friday to continue discussing the 2010 draft budget and start debating recommended wage increases for next year, among other issues.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par        Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                   2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 25.1890-90 25.1600-00 +0.12  25.2250 25.1990  -5.85 \par EUR/HUF 271.00-30  270.92-42  +0.03  271.08  271.30   +2.83 \par EUR/PLN 4.1290-50  4.1336-94  -0.11  4.1290  4.1340   +0.34 \par USD/RUB 30.361-67  30.327-32  +0.11  30.444  30.319   -0.54 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The forint is expected to remain strong Friday and trade in the HUF269-HUF272 range against the euro, a trader said. That's in line with the strengthening trend seen in other emerging European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , driven by continued dollar weakness against the euro, a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  dealer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday, the zloty will closely watch global sentiment and follow euro/dollar trade, dealers said. "Everything depends on global stock markets," said one local trader. "A good close in the U.S. will likely support the zloty." The trader expects the zloty to trade in a 4.07-4.13 range against the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech koruna may continue to strengthen against the euro as sentiment remains positive on stock markets in the region and in the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Since U.S. stocks are still doing well and (U.S. macroeconomic) data are also decent, one can expect further easing of risk aversion, which in turn may plays in the koruna's favor at least in the short run," Raiffeisenbank analyst Michal Brozka said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ruble is likely to continue benefiting Friday from tight liquidity, high oil prices and comments by Finance Minister Alexei Kudrin that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to reduce exchange-rate volatility rather than cap \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation. "We think the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is on the sidelines and is likely to step into trading when the ruble reaches 36.40 against the euro-dollar basket," said Renaissance Capital.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS - The three-year bond yield was at 7.55% late Thursday from 7.70% late Wednesday. The five-year was at 7.65% from 7.70%, and the 10-year was at 7.80% from 7.95%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian government bonds are expected to strengthen further Friday in anticipation of the Monetary Policy Council's rate meeting at the end of September, a trader said. The market has priced in a 100-basis-point interest rate cut, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par POLISH BOND YIELDS - Two-year paper was at 5.05% late Thursday, compared with 5.04% late Wednesday. The five-year was at 5.74% from 5.79%, while the 10-year was at 6.14% from 6.16%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders expect Friday to be quiet in the Polish bond market due to a lack of macroeconomic data. T-Bonds are likely to follow moves in the zloty.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CZECH BOND YIELD - The yield on the benchmark 10-year paper late Thursday was 5.05%, compared with 5.06% late Wednesday. \par  \par  \par STOCKS \par WIG20                   2164.82  gain 19.07   up 0.8% \par BUX                    20650.96  gain 433.51  up 2.1% \par PX                      1,164.4  gain 9.6     up 0.8% \par RTS                     1235.73  loss 11.08   dn 0.9% \par DJ Stoxx EU Enlarged 15  2124.69 gain 25.38   up 1.2% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: Despite a two-day rally, the WIG20 index isn't in a clear upward trend, brokers said. They added that Friday trade is difficult to call as WIG20 futures contracts are set to expire. "We might (therefore) expect a rather volatile day, which won't provide any hints for next week," a local broker said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Banking stocks are seen rising with peers in the region Friday, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Komercni Banka\f28 \uc2 \f2  to test CZK3,500 as long as sentiment remains upbeat, a trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: Stocks are expected to take a breather Friday after the rally during the previous two days, a Buda Cash brokerage trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN MOSCOW: Russian stocks could struggle after snapping a nine-day winning streak Thursday. "Our view is that the market rally will run out of steam and that now is not the time to buy Russian clunkers," said UralSib. [ 09-17-09 2317ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: The U.S. and its North Atlantic Treaty Organization allies did not discuss any possible deal with Russia involving the U.S. reworking its missile defense plans, a NATO spokesman said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: The seventh round of international talks aimed at resolving the security and humanitarian issues left over by the Russian-Georgia conflict in August 2008 concluded here Thursday without any substantial agreement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: The Russian government will reduce subsidies to balance regional budgets to 90 billion rubles in 2010 from 193.4 billion rubles in 2009, Deputy Finance Minister Anton Siluanov said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Polish Foreign Minister Radoslaw Sikorski said Thursday that the U.S. has pledged to deploy ground-to-air Patriot-type missiles in Poland, despite the U.S. revision of anti-missile plans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Ahold Czech Republic AS, the Czech unit of Dutch retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ahold NV\f28 \uc2 \f2  (AH.AE), will reduce the floorspace of its 11 largest Czech superstores by as much as half, a company spokesman said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Illegal economic activity has picked up in Hungary, dampening budget revenues, state news agency MTI says Thursday, quoting a report by a government committee monitoring the black market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romania's Foreign Ministry on Thursday hailed the removal of a visa regime imposed in April on Romanians traveling to Moldova.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Bulgaria is considering the sale of part of its 51% share in a new nuclear power plant at Belene on the Danube, Economy and Energy Minister Traicho Traikov said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrew Langley, Dow Jones Newswires; +7 495 937 845; andrew.langley@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Veronika Gulyas in Budapest, Malgorzata Halaba in Warsaw, and Leos Rousek in Prague contributed to the briefing.) [ 09-17-09 2318ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020090918e59i0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
