\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ IMF: Signs Russian Economy Has Reached Turning Point\b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2009\par 15:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ  IMF: Tighter Monetary Policy Exacerbated Bkg Sector Pressure\par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ  IMF: Russia Needs More Proactive Approach To Bank Problems\par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ  IMF:Russia Should Stress Test Larger Bks;Tighten Supervision\par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ  IMF: Difficult To Assess Russian Bks' Asset Quality, Risks\par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ  IMF: Concerned Russian Stimulus "May Become Entrenched"\par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ  IMF: Recommends Scaling Back Stimulus,Make It Self-Reversing\par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ  IMF:Russia Should Proceed Cautiously With Interest Rate Cuts\par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ  IMF: Russia Should Support Ruble If Rate Becomes Unsettled\par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ  IMF: Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Should Be Temporary\par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT =DJ  IMF: Russia Must Be Proactive On Banks, Redirect Stimulus\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Andrew Langley and Will Bland \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--Russia should take a more hands-on approach to tackling problems in its banking sector and redirect its stimulus package towards more temporary spending in order to build on early signs of an economic recovery, the International Monetary Fund said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The IMF - which sees the Russian economy shrinking 6.5% in 2009 - also welcomed recent interest rate cuts by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but warned against being too aggressive in easing monetary policy further amid volatility in the ruble exchange rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Banks' financial situation and loan portfolios are now deteriorating at a notably faster pace. The key immediate challenge facing the authorities at this time is how to deal with these mounting problems," the IMF's executive board wrote in an annual report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It said Russia should be more "proactive" in addressing the issues affecting the sector to avoid a prolonged credit freeze, advising the authorities to recapitalize struggling lenders, stress-test the largest institutions and beef up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's supervisory powers, in part to better understand thus-far opaque asset quality.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reductions in lending rates, which have been cut five times since April, will eventually filter through to the real economy, although banks remain inclined to strengthen their balance sheets rather than lend at present, the IMF said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Absent a more determined policy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , there is a risk that banks will continue to struggle to adjust balance sheets, stifling credit expansion and impeding a recovery."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Battered by lower commodity prices and scant access to credit, Russia's economy shrank by more than 10% on the year between January and June. Gross domestic product will fall by 8.5% for the whole 2009, according to the latest government forecasts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But recent reports on manufacturing and rail cargo suggest that the worst of Russia's first recession in a decade has now passed and that the second half of the year will be better.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The latest data...provide tentative signs that the economy may have recently reached a turning point," the IMF said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it warned that growth in 2010 will be slow, while the focus of the government's current stimulus program could endanger future economic expansion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A better-targeted yet smaller stimulus could have a similar impact on economic activity...but would reduce the risk of causing a permanent change in the tax and expenditure structure."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "[The IMF] recommends reorienting the stimulus toward a temporary increase in social transfers targeted on credit-constrained households, and suggests a front-loading of infrastructure projects for which preparations are advanced alongside a strengthening of procurement procedures."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the ruble, the IMF said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets on a temporary basis if rates become unsettled.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It said that the rates should be flexible but that the current exchange rate "remains broadly in equilibrium."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fund Web site: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.imf.org"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.imf.org}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrew Langley, Dow Jones Newswires; +7 495 937 8445; andrew.langley@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ \par IMF: Post-crisis Monetary Policy Should Target Inflation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ \par IMF: Russian Ruble Rate "Broadly In Equilibrium"\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7 Aug 2009 09:00 EDT *DJ \par IMF: Sees 6.5% GDP Contraction Forecast For Russia\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7 Aug 2009 09:01 EDT *DJ \par IMF: Russian Economy To Recover Only Slowly In 2010\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 07, 2009 09:01 ET (13:01 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090807e587003q9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
